# The annotations of the game image: the single source of truth for src/game/*.asm (kit/mkgame.py).
# The format is in kit/mkgame.py, class Annotations. One line an entry:
#
#   @module NAME ; title            the entries from here on go to NAME.asm, the title heads the file
#   @about a paragraph              the module file's header text, one paragraph a line
#   $ADDR c Name ; title            a routine
#   > a description line
#   $ADDR b Name ; title            data (w words, t text, s space; "b Name 8" rows of 8)
#   @res graphics/NAME.bin          the entry is a resource: src/res/..., INCBIN
#   $ADDR Name ; comment            a label and comment inside an entry: on an instruction, or on the data line
#                                   that starts at ADDR (a data entry's rows are cut there)
#   @isub $ADDR LD HL,-6            the instruction written so
#   @equ $ADDR Name [N] ; comment    a variable past the image: an EQU, its operands by name
#   @inline $ADDR                   a routine that reads its data after the CALL

@equ $028E KEY_SCAN ; the ROM's KEY-SCAN: E = the key value ($FF none), NZ for more than two keys
@equ $03B5 BEEPER ; the ROM's BEEPER: HL = the period, DE = the cycles; DI on entry, EI before its RET
@equ $04C2 SA_BYTES ; the ROM's SA-BYTES: IX = start, DE = length, A = flag byte
@equ $0556 LD_BYTES ; the ROM's LD-BYTES: IX, DE, A = flag byte; carry set = load, clear = verify; carry out = success
@equ $0B16 RomBlipLengths 15 ; 15 ROM bytes used as the lengths of the time-bonus blip's half-cycles
@equ $4000 Screen 6144 ; the display file
@equ $5280 ScreenRow162 ; the screen line of pixel row 162 (two rows under the ground's top row $A0)
@equ $5760 ScreenRow159 ; the screen line of pixel row 159 (the row over the ground's top row $A0)
@equ $5800 Attributes 768 ; the screen attributes
@equ $5A40 AttrRow18 ; the attribute line of character row 18 (the green plant's head)
@equ $5A80 AttrRow20 ; the attribute line of character row 20
@equ $5B00 FeedFarBand 32 ; printer buffer: the next 8 columns of the far band (HillsStrip), one byte a line (lines 64-95)
@equ $5B20 FeedMiddleBand 32 ; printer buffer: the next 8 columns of the hills, one byte a line (lines 96-127)
@equ $5B40 FeedRoad 32 ; printer buffer: the next 8 columns of the road, one byte a line (lines 160-191)
@equ $5B60 ShotForward 5 ; printer buffer: the forward shot: y ($FF free), x, height (3; $FF = hit), width, end x
@equ $5B65 ShotsUp 16 ; the buggy's four upward shots, 4 bytes each: y, x, height, width (+2 = $FF: spent)
@equ $5C08 LAST_K ; the ROM's last key, filled by the IM 1 keyboard scan
@equ $5C3B FLAGS ; the ROM's flags (bit 3: L mode)
@equ $5C3D ERR_SP 2 ; the ROM's error stack pointer: the word there is where an error report returns to
@equ $5C41 MODE ; the ROM's keyboard mode (0 = K/L/C)
@equ $5C48 BORDCR ; system variable: the border colour in bits 3-5
@equ $5C6A FLAGS2 ; the ROM's flags 2 (bit 3: CAPS LOCK)
@equ $5C78 FRAMES 3 ; the ROM's frame counter, counted by Im2Handler in play

@module leftover ; Build leftovers below graphics A: bytes no code reads, with a copy of the tape header
@about $60F8-$6131 (58 bytes) loads with the game and is never read, written or executed in any recording (l1sum.py --reads 60F8-6131: no reads in game, a1, g1, t1, p2, h1, i1, i2, h2). Kept as bytes, not as a resource.
$60F8 b BuildLeftover 8 ; Build leftover: 38 unidentified bytes, then a copy of the CODE header of the game block
> Never read, written or executed (l1sum.py --reads 60F8-6131: no build reads a byte). The first 16 bytes ($0F $E0 $1F $E0 0 ...) look like the first rows of TitleMoon $6132, the next 22 are not code that any path reaches.
> $611E-$612E is a 17-byte CODE header: type 3, the name "MOON" + $AF (the CODE token) + five spaces, length $9F08 (the game block's 40712 bytes), start $60F8, parameter $80F8. The header on the tape (block 3) reads "MOON      " and parameter $8000, so this is a copy from the build, not the tape's. Stage 1 put the name at $611D; it is at $611F.
$6108 ; 22 bytes, not identified
$611E LeftoverHeader ; CODE header copy: type 3
$611F ; the name: "MOON", $AF (CODE), five spaces
$6129 ; length $9F08, start $60F8, parameter $80F8
$612F ; three more bytes ($F8 $08 $FF)

@module graphicsa ; Graphics A: the title logo, the buggy's wreck, the explosions and bursts, one build-date string
@about The sprites here and in graphics B are drawn by the sprite drawer $A826 through SpriteTable $B2D6 (sprite number -> drawer, graphics address). Every drawer XORs a pre-shifted image onto the screen: the graphics address plus a shift slot chosen by the low bits of x, then rows of W bytes, top row first, no mask. The drawers and their formats: $B1C8 5x23 bytes in 128-byte slots, 4 slots (x AND 6); $B184 4x16, 4 slots of 64; $B10D / $B115 3x16, 4 slots of 48 ($B115 also colours 3x3 cells); $B0E2 3x12, 4 slots of 36; $B0BA 3x8, 8 slots of 24 (x AND 7); $B069 3x8, 4 slots of 24 (also colours 3x2 cells); $B02A 2x9, 8 slots of 18; $B003 2x8, 4 slots of 16; $AFDD 2x4, 8 slots of 8. A frame is all its slots; a frame set is consecutive frames.
@about The title logo is copied, not XORed: the copier $AFAB copies rows of BlitWidth bytes to the screen at BlitX (in bytes), lines BlitTop to BlitBottom-1.
$6132 b TitleMoon 10 ; The title logo's "Moon": 10 bytes x 25 lines, copied to x 88, lines 24-48
> Copied by the copier $AFAB from the title $8004 ($8024: BlitTop 24, BlitBottom 49, BlitX $58 = byte 11, width 10) and at each life start from $9136 ($9163, the HUD header). Linear rows of 10 bytes, one bit a pixel, set = ink; no mask, no shifts. All 250 bytes are read in every build (game, a1 twice, g1, t1, p2, h1 twice, i1, i2).
@res graphics/TitleMoon.bin
$622C b TitlePatrol 14 ; The title logo's "Patrol": 14 bytes x 25 lines, copied to x 72, lines 55-79
> Copied by the copier $AFAB from $8036 (BlitTop $37, BlitBottom $50, BlitX $48, width 14). Same format as TitleMoon $6132. The last 58 bytes, $6350-$6389, are also the first 3 1/4 shift slots of BuggyWreck1's frame: SpriteTable entry $3C points at $6350, so wreck piece 1 drawn at x AND 7 = 0-3 shows bytes of the logo (see BuggyWreck1 $638A).
@res graphics/TitlePatrol.bin
$638A b BuggyWreck1 2 ; Wreck piece 1 of the buggy (sprite $3C): the visible part of its 2x9 frame, slots 3 1/4 to 7
> Sprite $3C, drawer $B02A: 2 bytes x 9 lines, 8 shift slots of 18 bytes, one per pixel of x AND 7, from $6350. Its first 58 bytes, $6350-$6389, are the end of TitlePatrol $622C, so this entry starts where the piece's own bytes start: the middle of slot 3 (its line 2). Slots 4-7 ($6398-$63DF) hold a wheel with its hub on top, moving one pixel a slot.
> Drawn by the wreck routine $AB37 ($AB95) after a death: the three pieces $3C-$3E fly from the buggy along the path at $AC31. Read in game, a1, g1, p2, h1, h2 (deaths), never in the immune runs i1 / i2. A piece that leaves the top of the screen is drawn with a line number past 191, so the line table read runs past ScreenLineTable $FC00 into the print code at $FD80 (reads of $FDA6-$FDB7, game / g1 / p2 / h1 / h2) and the XOR lands on $6F75-$6F79, $77B5-$77B9, $7D15-$7D19 and $CB5B-$CB5F; every byte there is written an even number of times (draw and erase), so the image is restored.
@res graphics/BuggyWreck1.bin
$63E0 b BuggyWreck2 2 ; Wreck piece 2 of the buggy (sprite $3D): 2x9, 8 shift slots of 18 bytes
> Drawn by the wreck routine $AB37 ($ABD0). A wheel with a stub; slots 0-2 move right, slot 3 restarts 5 pixels left (the image's origin moves with the slot). All 144 bytes read in game, a1, g1, p2, h1, h2.
@res graphics/BuggyWreck2.bin
$6470 b BuggyWreck3 2 ; Wreck piece 3 of the buggy (sprite $3E): 2x9, 8 shift slots of 18 bytes
> Drawn by the wreck routine $AB37 ($AC0B). A smaller wheel. Only the even slots were read (72 of 144 bytes): the piece's x stays even in the recordings.
@res graphics/BuggyWreck3.bin
$6500 b BuggyExplosion 5 ; The buggy's explosion (sprites $1F-$26): 8 frames of 5x23 bytes, 4 shift slots of 128 bytes each
> Drawer $B1C8: 5 bytes x 23 lines = 115 bytes at the start of each 128-byte slot (13 bytes of padding), slot = x AND 6 (2-pixel steps), 4 slots = 512 bytes a frame, frames at $6500, $6700, ..., $7300. Drawn by the buggy routine $AA0F ($AABC): sprite $1E + DeathStep $B216 (1-8) at the buggy's place, one frame a pass; the main loop $96CC erases frame $26 when DeathStep reaches 8. The tank's explosion, sprites $33-$36, reuses the first four frames (SpriteTable $B3A2).
> Slot 0 of frame 1 ($6500-$6572) is not an explosion: lines 0-11 and 20-22 hold other bytes (rows of 2-pixel stripes), lines 12-19 are blank. It is read in game, a1, g1, p2, h1, h2, so a buggy dying at an x with x AND 6 = 0 shows those stripes for one frame; how it looks is for a run to confirm. 3685 of the 4096 bytes are read.
@res graphics/BuggyExplosion.bin
