$7500 b PlantScore 4 ; The "500" sign shown where a green plant was shot (sprite $73): 4x16 bytes, 4 shift slots of 64
> Drawer $B184: 4 bytes x 16 lines, slot = x AND 6, 256 bytes. Drawn by the plant routine at $A415 (LD A,$73) after the plant is shot. Read only in i2 (Champion), slots 0 and 2 (128 of 256 bytes).
@res graphics/PlantScore.bin
$7540 ; slot 1 (x AND 6 = 2)
$7580 ; slot 2
$75C0 ; slot 3
$7600 b BurstLarge 3 ; Large burst, 4 frames of 3x16 bytes (sprites $2F-$32 with colour, $60-$63 without)
> Drawers $B115 (sprites $2F-$32, also colours 3x3 cells) and $B10D ($60-$63): 3 bytes x 16 lines, 4 slots of 48 bytes (x AND 6), 192 bytes a frame, frames at $7600, $76C0, $7780, $7840. $60-$63 are the large boulder's break-up: the boulder handler sets its frame to $5F ($A1FC) and $A29C counts it up until (frame - $5C) AND $D7 is 0, at $64. $2F-$32 are a shot UFO's burst: the UFO handler counts its frame up and ends at $33 ($9CCA-$9CD4). Read in i1 1920 and i2 8064 times, a few reads in the other builds.
@res graphics/BurstLarge.bin
$76C0 ; frame 2
$7780 ; frame 3
$7840 ; frame 4
$7900 b Debris 3 ; Debris, 4 frames of 3x12 bytes (sprites $2B-$2E, $5C-$5F and $7B-$7E)
> Drawer $B0E2: 3 bytes x 12 lines, 4 slots of 36 bytes (x AND 6), 144 bytes a frame, frames at $7900, $7990, $7A20, $7AB0. Sprites $2B-$2E are a shot rock's pieces (the rock handler counts its frame up to $2E, $9A79 CP $2F); $7B-$7E a shot plant's ($A439 CP $7F). $5C-$5F point here too, but the medium boulder's count stops at $5B: no user of $5C-$5F was found. Read in every build except t1.
@res graphics/Debris.bin
$7990 ; frame 2
$7A20 ; frame 3
$7AB0 ; frame 4
$7B40 b BurstMedium 3 ; Medium burst, 4 frames of 3x12 bytes (sprites $58-$5B)
> Drawer $B0E2 (as Debris $7900), frames at $7B40, $7BD0, $7C60, $7CF0. The medium boulder's break-up: the boulder handler sets its frame to $57 ($A1F3) and $A29C counts $58-$5B. Read in i2 2880 times; the few reads in game, a1, g1, p2, h1, h2 ($7D15-$7D19) are the wreck pieces' stray XOR (BuggyWreck1 $638A).
@res graphics/BurstMedium.bin
$7BD0 ; frame 2
$7C60 ; frame 3
$7CF0 ; frame 4
$7D80 b BurstSmall 3 ; Small burst, 4 frames of 3x8 bytes with colour (sprites $27-$2A and $37-$3A)
> Drawer $B069: 3 bytes x 8 lines, 4 slots of 24 bytes (x AND 6), 96 bytes a frame, frames at $7D80, $7DE0, $7E40, $7EA0; the drawer also colours 3x2 cells with the colour in E'. $37-$3A are the low flyer's explosion (its handler counts to $3A, $A160 CP $3B); $27-$2A a UFO's smaller burst, ended at $2B by the same count ($9CCD). Read only in i1 (6144) and i2 (4416).
@res graphics/BurstSmall.bin
$7DE0 ; frame 2
$7E40 ; frame 3
$7EA0 ; frame 4
$7F00 t BuildDate0335 ; A print string with the build date 03:35 30/10/84, printed at each life start; its first byte $1F ends it at once
> The life start $90D3 prints it ($911A LD HL,$7F00; CALL $FFD0) and PrintText $FFD0 stops at the first byte, $1F: nothing is printed. Read once per life start (game 20, a1 4, g1 4, p2 8, h1 4, i1 6, i2 6); $7F01-$7F13 are never read. The rest reads as a whole print string: x 0, line $19, FONT 2, "03:35 30/10/84", end ($1F). With $16 (AT) instead of the first $1F it would print the date at x 0, line 25. The three other dates are disabled the same way: $90B8 (line $21), $A182 (line $29) and BuildDate0245 $B2C2 (line $31), printed one after the other by $90D3.
@res text/BuildDate0335.bin

@isub $95A7 LD BC,-127
@isub $9C45 LD BC,-4
@isub $9C7D LD BC,-5
@isub $9CC6 LD BC,-4
@isub $9D1B LD BC,-10
@isub $9D22 LD BC,-5
@isub $9E32 LD BC,-4
@isub $9E45 LD BC,-10
@isub $A858 LD DE,HillsStrip+$0440
@isub $802A LD HL,$50*256+$37
@isub $813E LD HL,$DE*256+$A0
@isub $8EF4 LD DE,-7

@module interrupt ; The IM 2 interrupt handler: FRAMES, the tune player and the play clock
@about The IM 2 vector is the ROM word at $3AFF ($FFFF); the byte $18 at $FFFF and the ROM's $F3 make JR $FFF4, and JP $7F14 there enters Im2Handler. IM 2 is set only in play (SetIm2 $A7D4 at each life start); the menus run in IM 1 with the ROM's handler, so none of this runs on the menu pages except the demo (DemoPage calls LifeStart at $8196: IM 2, with the tune off by $819B) (t1, the tape recording that never starts a game, has no entry at all).
@about One entry per accepted frame interrupt: FRAMES + 1, TunePlayer (a note through the ROM's BEEPER every third entry), Im2Clock (the play time in BCD seconds every 50 entries). i1: 25 747 entries in 30 440 frames.
@about The handler is not re-entrant by design (DI on entry, all registers on the stack, EI only before its RET), and the only EI inside it is the one BEEPER executes before its RET. Every note ends 19 000-34 000 T before the next frame interrupt (TunePlayer), so the window in which an interrupt could be accepted at $7F8D is never open: no recording has an interrupt taken inside $7F14-$7FBE or BEEPER (the L1 builds attribute every interrupt to the routine it interrupted, none to these).
@about After the code: Atari's copyright line $7FBF and zero fill to $7FFF, never read by any build: a leftover. The title prints its own copyright (TitleText $8413).

$7F14 c Im2Handler ; The IM 2 interrupt handler: FRAMES + 1, one TunePlayer step, one Im2Clock step
> Entered from JP $7F14 at $FFF4 (the IM 2 vector $FFFF). DI, then pushes AF, BC, DE, HL, AF again (EXX does not swap AF, so AF' is never saved: neither the handler nor BEEPER uses it), BC', DE', HL', IX, IY: with the return address 22 bytes. FRAMES $5C78 + 1, and FRAMES+2 ($5C7A, as IY+$40 with the BASIC's IY = $5C3A) + 1 when the low word wraps. Then TunePlayer $7F47 and Im2Clock $7F98, the registers back, EI, RET.
> The stack: interrupted at the game's lowest SP $C316, the frame fills $C300-$C315 (IY = $5C3A at $C300), CALL TunePlayer puts $7F34 at $C2FE, TunePlayer's CALL BEEPER puts $7F8D at $C2FC, then CALL Im2Clock overwrites $C2FE with $7F37. So the words stage 1 saw at $C2FC-$C311 (i1 frame 10000: $7F8D, $7F37, $5C3A ...) are one ordinary, non-nested frame: $7F8D left from the note, $7F37 from the clock call (not the tune player call), $5C3A the pushed IY. $C2FC is the deepest the stack goes.
> A nested entry is possible only at $7F8D: BEEPER ends EI, RET, so an interrupt still asserted when that RET ends would be taken before the DI at $7F8D. It would do no harm: it pushes a second frame below $C2FC (down to $C2E6), FRAMES + 1 and the clock + 1 are correct for the frame that passed, and the tune counter $B240, just reset to 0 by the outer pass, only goes to 1 (no second note, the tune pointer $B23E untouched). It does not happen (Interrupt module header; open point in the notes).
> Witness: game 11 644 entries (frames 1560-16 759), a1 2090, g1 3351, p2 4612, h1 3351, i1 25 747 (264-30 439), i2 28 601, h2 288; t1 none. Each entry runs the whole body.
$7F2E ; FRAMES+2 through IY: the handler relies on IY = $5C3A
$7F31 Im2HandlerSteps ; the tune, then the clock
$7F37 ; $7F37, the return of CALL Im2Clock, is the word stage 1 saw at $C2FE
$7F45 ; the only EI of the handler: the next interrupt is taken after the RET, in the interrupted code

$7F47 c TunePlayer ; One step of the tune: every third entry the next word of the tune at $B4E6, a note through BEEPER or a rest
> $B240 counts the handler's entries 0-2; on the third it goes back to 0 and one tune word is taken from the pointer $B23E (then pointer + 2): 0 is a rest, $FFFF sends the pointer back to $B4E6 and takes the first word again, anything else is BEEPER's period itself. With $B288 bit 7 set (the tune off: the T key, $AF15) a note is a rest too, so the tune keeps its place while silent.
> A note: HL = the word, BEEPER's period (INC HL at $7F68 for the end test, DEC HL at $7F7C back), DE = the cycles: the smallest n with n x H >= 16 (H the period's high byte), so every note lasts about the same: 8 n (HL + 30) T. Over the tune's 16 periods ($0231-$06CE) that is 35 700-50 500 T, so a note started by an entry at the frame interrupt ends well inside that frame, never across the next interrupt. A rest is a delay loop of $05C0 passes (about 38 000 T). After BEEPER (which ends EI) a DI at $7F8D before returning into the handler.
> Witness: entered once per handler entry (i1 25 747); a word taken i1 8600 times, a note i1 7793 (BEEPER called from $7F8A), a rest i1 787; the tune wrapped at $7F6D i1 20 times, i2 22. a1: notes 341 of 626 words (the tune off 2540-2740).
$7F50 ; the third entry: counter back to 0, one tune word
$7F52 TuneNextWord ; HL = the next word, the pointer + 2
$7F64 ; 0: a rest
$7F68 ; $FFFF: the end mark, back to the start of the tune $B4E6
$7F75 TuneNote ; the tune off ($B288 bit 7): a rest instead
$7F7C ; HL = the period, B = 16, DE = 1, A = H
$7F83 TuneCycles ; DE + 1 until DE x H >= 16
$7F8A TuneBeep ; BEEPER: HL period, DE cycles
$7F8D ; BEEPER's EI is undone here: the return address $7F8D is the word at $C2FC on the deepest stack
$7F8F TuneRest ; a silent note: $05C0 passes of the delay loop
$7F92 TuneRestLoop

$7F98 c Im2Clock ; The play clock: every 50 entries the BCD seconds $B285/$B286 + 1 and the HUD time marked stale
> $B287 counts the handler's entries; at 50 it goes back to 0, $B239 = 0 (the HUD redraws the time: $922A) and the BCD seconds $B286 (low) / $B285 (high) + 1. It counts handler entries, not frames: an interrupt that is not taken is not counted.
> The branch $7FA1-$7FAB tests the tune counter $B240 for 3 and would set $B287 to 49 (the second one entry later); TunePlayer always leaves $B240 at 0-2, so $7FA8 never ran in any recording (game, a1, g1, t1, p2, h1, i1, i2, h2): known only from the code.
> Witness: one entry per handler entry; a second counted game 231 times, a1 41, i1 511, i2 570.
$7FA1 ; the tune counter is never 3 here (TunePlayer resets it): the next two lines never run
$7FAC ClockSecond ; the next second: counter 0, the HUD time stale, BCD + 1

$7FBF t CopyrightLeftover ; Leftover: "c 1984 Atari Inc. All Rights Reserved" with a 0 after it, never read
> No build reads $7FBF-$7FFF (l1sum.py --reads 7FBF-7FFF: no reads in game, a1, g1, t1, p2, h1, i1, i2, h2) and no operand in the image points at it. The text the title prints is TitleText $8413.
$7FE5 s CopyrightPadding ; Leftover: 27 zero bytes up to $7FFF, never read

@module menus ; The entry, the title, the options page, the score advance table, the demo, the hi-score page, the tape
@about The game's entry $8000 and the attract cycle, all in IM 1: TitlePage (100 passes) falls into OptionsPage (100 passes), then ScoreAdvancePage (200 passes), DemoPage (100 main loop passes), HeroesPage (200 passes) and back to TitlePage. a1 went round it once while idle: the score advance table about 19 800, the demo about 20 800, the heroes about 21 200.
@about Every page pass reads the keys through small checks that jump straight to the next page with SP reset from $B25D: MenuCheckStart (S or fire: the game $8F10), MenuCheckHeroes (H), MenuCheckTape (L load, J save, G verify the hi-score tables), MenuCheckAnyKey (the options page). The options page itself reads one key with the ROM's KEY_SCAN and sets the players $B257, the course $B258 and the controls $B259.
@about The tape code saves, loads and verifies $12E bytes from $88AC (the all-time tables, HiScores module) with the flag byte $6C, with ERR_SP hooked to TapeErrorHandler $82F3 so that a BREAK (report D) comes back to the menu. A block whose flag byte is $6B is reported as an Atarisoft Robotron hi-score table: the tape code is shared with Atarisoft's Robotron.
@about The pages draw a pixel frame (DrawPixelFrame) whose attribute rings change colour each pass (CycleFrameColours), and every text attribute steps through ColourCycle each pass (CycleScreenColours). The texts are print strings of $FFD0 (codes in the notes: $10-$1A with arguments, $0D new line, $1F end).
@about Two leftovers of a BREAK-to-BASIC exit: MenuBreakTest $8215 is called on every page but its result is never tested, and MenuBreakToBasic $8222, which nothing calls, would set IM 1 and stop with report L.

$8000 c MenuEntry ; The game's entry (USR 32768): keeps the BASIC's SP at $B25D, then the title
> Runs once, from BASIC's USR (c1 frame 256; the stage-1 recordings start here from the entry snapshot). The SP saved at $B25D ($C338 in c1) is the one every page reloads before jumping to the next, so the stack never grows over the pages, and the game never returns to BASIC.
> Witness: game 1, a1 1, g1 1, t1 1, p2 1, h1 1, i1 1, i2 1.

$8004 c TitlePage ; The title page: Moon Patrol, the copyright and the key list; 100 passes, then the options page
> SP = $B25D. The print variable $F0A2 = 8, the screen cleared with attribute $44 ($9044), the pixel frame (DrawPixelFrame) and its colours (CycleFrameColours). The two picture words through $AFAB: "Moon" from $6132 (lines $18-$30, 10 bytes wide at x $58: $B247/$B248 = $18/$31, $B249/$B24A = $58/$0A) and "Patrol" from $622C (lines $37-$4F, 14 bytes at x $48). Then TitleText $8413.
> 100 passes, each: MenuCheckStart (S or fire: the game), MenuCheckHeroes (H), MenuCheckTape (L, J, G), MenuCheckAnyKey (any other key: the options page), CycleScreenColours, CycleFrameColours, MenuDelay 100. Unlike the other pages it does not call MenuBreakTest. After the 100th pass it falls into OptionsPage.
> Entered from MenuEntry, from HeroesPage at its end ($81FF) and from the abort $AABF. Witness: a1 2 (the start, the abort at 3190), h1 2, the others 1; the loop ran 100 passes and fell through only in t1 and h1 ($8063).
$8018 ; "Moon": lines $18-$30, x $58, 10 bytes a line
$802A ; "Patrol": lines $37-$4F, x $48, 14 bytes a line
$803C ; the copyright line and the key list
$8046 ; 100 passes
$8048 TitleLoop ; one pass: the keys, the colours, a delay
$8063 ; the passes ran out: the options page

$8064 c OptionsPage ; The options page: one or two players, the course, the controls; 100 passes, then the score advance table
> The screen cleared (attribute 0), the frame, OptionsText $84FA. The three normal colours of the option groups are reset ($B25A = 6, $B25B = $46, $B25C = 4) and OptionsHighlightLine marks the current choices in bright white: the players $B257 (row $19 + 8 x value), the course $B258 ($31 + 8 v), the controls $B259 ($49 + 8 v) and the line "S Start Game" ($99).
> 100 passes, each: MenuBreakTest (result unused), MenuCheckStart, MenuCheckHeroes, MenuCheckTape, CycleScreenColours, CycleColourRange over $B25A-$B25C (the un-highlight colours step through ColourCycle too, so a line un-highlighted later gets the current colour), CycleFrameColours, then one key from KEY_SCAN: a single key goes through OptionsSelect against OptionPlayerKeys (O, T), OptionCourseKeys (1, 2) and OptionControlKeys (Z X C V B K), which moves the highlight and stores the new value. MenuDelay 100. No MenuCheckAnyKey here: keys select. When the passes run out, ScoreAdvancePage.
> The values (initial ones in the image): $B257 1 one player, 2 two players (1); $B258 1 Beginner, 2 Champion (1); $B259 1 Sinclair with 1 joystick (Z), 2 Kempston (X), 3 Cursor (C), 4 Old-type Fuller Box (V), 5 Sinclair with 2 joysticks (B), 6 Keyboard (K) (6). The play-time reading of $B259 is $AE17 / $AE2C (the table $AE2E indexed by $B259 - 1); MenuCheckStart uses it for fire.
> Entered from TitlePage and from MenuCheckAnyKey ($832F). Witness: a1 21 entries (414-21 155), t1 2, p2 1 (153-334), h1 1, i2 1 (the 2 key: Champion), game 3. a1 typed O, T, 1, 2, Z, X, C, V, B, K: OptionsSelect a1 2619 calls (537-18 573).
$8074 ; the normal colours of the three groups
$8083 ; the current choices in bright white
$809B ; and "S Start Game"
$80A7 OptionsLoop ; one pass
$80C4 ; one key: NZ = more than two keys
$80C9 ; E = $FF: no key
$80CD ; the players: O = 1, T = 2
$80E4 ; the course: 1 = Beginner, 2 = Champion
$80FB ; the controls: Z X C V B K = 1-6
$8110 OptionsNextPass ; the delay, the next pass

$8118 c ScoreAdvancePage ; The score advance table: the enemies drawn on a piece of ground with their points; 200 passes, then the demo
> Entered when OptionsPage runs out of passes. The screen cleared, the ground drawn by $95B0 and $9541 with $B27D forced to $FF for the drawing (then put back), three gaps cut into the ground line at row $A0 ($A7F4: x $22 20 pixels, x $3F 10, x $DE 28), sixteen sprites from ScoreTableSprites through $A826, then ScoreAdvanceText $8655 (the points in two rows: shot, jumped).
> 200 passes: MenuBreakTest, MenuCheckStart, MenuCheckHeroes, MenuCheckTape, MenuCheckAnyKey (any key: the options page), CycleScreenColours, MenuDelay 100. Then DemoPage.
> Witness: a1 20 times, t1 once ($8118). The 200 passes ran out once (a1 $8180, about 19 800-20 800); the other 19 times a key went back to the options page.
$8118 ; the options passes' counter put back
$811D ; $B27D = $FF while the ground is drawn
$812E ; three gaps in the ground line, row $A0
$8147 ; E' = 7
$814A ; sixteen records: y, x, sprite
$814F ScoreTableSpriteLoop ; HL = x, y; A = the sprite: $A826 draws it
$815C ; the title and the points
$8165 ScoreTableLoop ; one pass

$8180 c DemoPage ; The demo: a life on the course run by the main loop for 100 passes, "DEMO" flashing; then the hi-score page
> The screen cleared, the clock zeroed ($B285/$B286 = 0, $B287 = 0), $B27D complemented (so successive demos alternate it), the life start $90D3 (which sets IM 2), the tune off ($B288 = $80) and $B238 = $80, DemoText $8706.
> 100 passes, each one pass of the main loop's call list $96FA, then MenuBreakTest, MenuCheckStart, MenuCheckHeroes, MenuCheckTape, MenuCheckAnyKey (each leaves through SetIm1 $A7CB) and CycleColourRange over the five attributes $58AE-$58B2 ("DEMO"). Then SetIm1 and HeroesPage.
> Witness: a1 once (about 20 800, the 100 passes completed, $81CD 1).
$81A1 ; "DEMO"
$81A7 ; 100 passes of the main loop
$81AA DemoLoop
$81BD ; "DEMO" changes colour
$81CD ; back to IM 1

$81D0 c HeroesPage ; The hi-score page: the heroes and the all-time heroes; 200 passes, then the title
> The screen cleared, the frame, HeroesDrawTables $8AE0. 200 passes: MenuBreakTest, MenuCheckStart, MenuCheckTape, MenuCheckAnyKey, CycleScreenColours, CycleFrameColours, MenuDelay 100 (no MenuCheckHeroes: H does nothing here). Then TitlePage.
> Entered from MenuCheckHeroes (H), from DemoPage, from TapeLoadDone after a good load ($831E), and from the game over $8F9F after the hi-score entry. Witness: a1 2 (H at 153; the demo about 21 200), h1 1 (4524-6060), game 4; the 200 passes ran out only in h1 ($81FE).
$81E3 HeroesPageLoop ; one pass
$81FE ; the passes ran out: the title

$8202 c OptionsHighlightLine ; Marks an option line bright white: A = the value, B = the first line's pixel row
> Row = 8 A + B; LineAttrAddress, then HighlightLine28 (28 cells $47 from the third column). Called four times by OptionsPage (the players, the course, the controls, and A = 0 for "S Start Game" at row $99). Witness: a1 84, game 12, t1 8, p2 4, h1 4, i2 4.

$820D c MenuDelay ; A delay of C x 256 DJNZ passes (C = 100 on the pages: about 333 000 T, near 5 frames)
> B = 0, C counted down. Called once a pass by every page and by the bonus screen $A4EE. Witness: a1 2327 calls, i1 142.
$820F MenuDelayLoop

$8215 c MenuBreakTest ; Dead BREAK test: A bit 0 = 0 when CAPS SHIFT and SPACE are both held; no caller tests it
> Reads half-rows $FE (CAPS SHIFT) and $7F (SPACE), ORs them and keeps bit 0: 0 only when both keys are down. Every caller (OptionsPage, ScoreAdvancePage, DemoPage, HeroesPage, the tape message loop) goes on to its next call without looking at A or the flags, so CAPS+SPACE does nothing on the menus. It looks like the test that once led to MenuBreakToBasic $8222. Witness: a1 2424 calls, h1 296, t1 216.

$8222 c MenuBreakToBasic ; Unreferenced stub: IM 1, then RST 8 with report L (BREAK into program): back to BASIC
> CALL SetIm1 $A7CB, RST 8, DEFB $14. Nothing calls or jumps here (no operand $8222 in the image) and no recording ran or read it (game, a1, g1, t1, p2, h1, i1, i2, h2): known only from the code. With MenuBreakTest it would have made CAPS+SPACE leave the game for BASIC, a development aid left in.
$8225 ; RST 8: the byte after it, $14 (shown as INC D), is report L, BREAK into program

$8227 c MenuCheckStart ; S, or fire on the chosen joystick: IM 1 kept, SP reset, the game $8F10
> Half-row $FD alone with S (bit 1) held starts the game. Otherwise, unless the controls $B259 are 6 (the keyboard), $AE17 reads the chosen joystick and A = 1 (fire) starts it. Start: SetIm1 $A7CB, SP = $B25D, JP $8F10. Witness: called a1 2450 times; the joystick read ran game 55, a1 39 (a1 chose a joystick on the options page); the start game 5, a1 1 (1314), g1, p2, h1, i1, i2 1 each.
$8231 ; the keyboard: no fire to read
$8237 ; the joystick read of the chosen controls (annotator 04's $AE17): A = 1 for fire
$823D MenuStartGame ; IM 1, the BASIC's SP, the game

$8247 c MenuCheckHeroes ; H (half-row $BF bit 4): IM 1, SP reset, the hi-score page
> Witness: called a1 2365 times; taken a1 once ($8250, frame 153).

$825A c MenuCheckTape ; L, J or G: load, save or verify the hi-score tables on tape, with ERR_SP hooked to TapeErrorHandler
> Half-row $FD bit 4 (G) and half-row $BF bits 1 (L) and 3 (J); nothing held returns. Otherwise: SP saved at $B263, the word at ERR_SP (where the ROM's error report returns) swapped for $82F3 and kept at $B265, the keys kept at $B23A, then DE = $12E bytes, A = flag $6C:
> L: LD_BYTES with carry set (load) into the buffer $BEF8 (the object list, free on the menus). J: SA_BYTES from $88AC (the all-time tables, HiScores module), then ERR_SP put back, RET. G: LD_BYTES with carry clear (verify) against $88AC. When both run, L wins over J and G, and G over J.
> After LD_BYTES (TapeLoadOrVerify), ERR_SP put back; carry set (the flag and the checksum good) goes to TapeLoadDone $8309. A failure (carry clear) prints a message: A = L, the last byte LD_BYTES read; when that was the flag byte, $6B, the block is a Robotron table (the flag byte Atarisoft's Robotron saves its table with) and RobotronWarningText $873B is printed, anything else gives TapeErrorText $8716 "Tape error - hit any key." (TapeFailed). Then the message loop: the menu checks forever, any key goes to the options page.
> Witness: t1 J (SA_BYTES 153-479), L and G (LD_BYTES 1077-2650): $826B 3, $8290 2 (not L), $8299 1 (J), $82A9 2 (L and G). LD_BYTES never returned in t1: it waits for a tape edge until SPACE is pressed (the ROM has no time-out there), and SPACE ended both with report D through TapeErrorHandler. So $82AC-$82F2, the failure messages and the message loop, never ran in any recording (game, a1, g1, t1, p2, h1, i1, i2, h2): known only from the code.
$826B ; hook ERR_SP: the ROM's error return now goes to TapeErrorHandler
$827E ; $12E bytes, flag $6C, load into $BEF8 by default
$828C ; L: load
$8290 ; not L: verify (carry clear) or save, the tables at $88AC
$8295 ; G: verify
$8299 ; J: save, ERR_SP back, return to the page
$82A9 TapeLoadOrVerify ; LD_BYTES: returns only with a tape signal (else BREAK, report D)
$82AC ; A = L: the last byte read, the flag byte when the flag did not match
$82B9 ; carry: loaded or verified
$82BB TapeFailed ; the flag byte $6B: an Atarisoft Robotron table
$82C2 ; any other failure: "Tape error - hit any key."
$82C5 TapeShowMessage ; the screen cleared, the frame, the message
$82D7 TapeMessageLoop ; the menu checks forever: any key leaves for the options page

$82F3 c TapeErrorHandler ; The ROM's error report during a tape operation returns here: wait for CAPS/SPACE up, unhook ERR_SP, back to the page
> Reached by the ROM's error exit (LD SP,(ERR_SP), RET) while MenuCheckTape has the hook in: report D (BREAK) from SA_BYTES or LD_BYTES. Waits until neither CAPS SHIFT nor SPACE (bit 0 of half-rows $FE and $7F) is held, puts the old word back at ERR_SP, reloads the SP MenuCheckTape saved at $B263 and returns to the page that called MenuCheckTape.
> Witness: t1 twice (the L and the G load stopped with SPACE, frames 1390-2670), the wait loop 65 060 instructions; no entry count because it is reached by RET.
$82F3 ; CAPS SHIFT or SPACE still held: wait
$82FC ; ERR_SP back, the SP of MenuCheckTape's call, return to the page

$8309 c TapeLoadDone ; A good tape block: after G (verify) back to the page; after L the buffer copied over the tables and the hi-score page
> MenuCheckTape's success path ($82B9). $B23A bit 4 clear (G held) returns. Otherwise (L) the $12E bytes loaded at $BEF8 replace $88AC-$89D9, SP = $B25D, HeroesPage. The flag byte and the ROM's checksum are the only checks. Not executed in any recording (game, a1, g1, t1, p2, h1, i1, i2, h2; there is no tape of a saved table): known only from the code.

$8321 c MenuCheckAnyKey ; Any key (all half-rows at once): IM 1, SP reset, the options page
> Witness: called a1 445 times, h1 317; taken game 3, a1 21, t1 1, p2 1, i2 1 ($8328).

$8332 c CycleScreenColours ; Every attribute of the screen found in ColourCycle steps to the next colour there
> $5800-$5AFF, each byte searched in the ten bytes of ColourCycle and replaced by the byte after it; other attributes stay. The menu texts are printed in these colours, so they change colour each pass. Witness: a1 2327 calls, h1 413.
$8335 CycleColoursLoop
$8342 CycleColoursNext

$8349 c CycleColourRange ; As CycleScreenColours for the bytes DE to IX - 1 only
> Used by OptionsPage on its three normal colours $B25A-$B25C, by DemoPage on "DEMO" ($58AE-$58B2) and by the bonus screen $A4EE. Witness: a1 2103 calls, i1 125 (the bonus screens), i2 144.
$8356 CycleRangeNext

$8360 c OptionsSelect ; One option group: if key E is one of its B keys, highlight the new choice, un-highlight the old one, D = the new value
> IX = the group's table (records: key value, option value, pixel row), B = its records, C = the group's normal colour, D = the current value, E = the key. NZ: not this group's key. Z with D unchanged: the key's option is already chosen. Otherwise the new line goes bright white (HighlightLine28), the old value's line gets C in its 28 cells, D = the new value; the flags are those of AND A on it (NZ for every value the tables hold, 1-6), so the caller goes on to the next group.
> Witness: a1 2619 calls (537-18 573), a new choice a1 10 times ($836E), game 2, p2 1, i2 1.
$836A ; A = the option value of the key
$836F ; the new line bright white
$8379 ; find the line of the old value
$838C OptionsUnhighlightLoop ; 28 cells in the group's normal colour

$8394 c OptionsFindEntry ; Searches B records of 3 bytes from HL for the byte A: Z and HL on it, or NZ (B = $FF)
> Witness: a1 2629 entries.

$839D c LineAttrAddress ; HL = the attribute address of pixel row A (the line address table at $7E00), column 0
> Witness: a1 104 calls.

$83AF c HighlightLine28 ; 28 cells from HL + 2 set to $47 (bright white on black)
> Witness: a1 94 calls.
$83B3 HighlightLoop

$83BA b OptionPlayerKeys 3 ; The players group of the options page: key value, option value, pixel row (O = 1 at $21, T = 2 at $29)
> Read by OptionsPage $80D7 through OptionsSelect (a1 958 reads of $83BA). The key values are KEY_SCAN's ($1A = O, $05 = T); the row is the line's pixel row in OptionsText.
$83C0 b OptionCourseKeys 3 ; The course group: 1 = Beginner (key value $24, row $39), 2 = Champion ($1C, row $41)
$83C6 b OptionControlKeys 3 ; The controls group: Z X C V B K ($1F $17 $0F $07 $00 $11) = 1-6, rows $51-$79
> The values stored in $B259: 1 Sinclair with 1 joystick, 2 Kempston, 3 Cursor, 4 Old-type Fuller Box, 5 Sinclair with 2 joysticks, 6 Keyboard (OptionsText). a1 827 reads.
$83D8 b ScoreTableSprites 3 ; The sixteen sprites of the score advance table: y, x, sprite number for $A826
> Read by ScoreAdvancePage $814F: a1 20 times, t1 once.
$8408 b ColourCycle 11 ; The menu colour cycle: each attribute found in the first ten bytes becomes the one after it ($44 $04 $46 $06 $42 $41 $43 $03 $05 $45, back to $44)
> Read by CycleScreenColours and CycleColourRange (CPIR over ten bytes, the eleventh byte closes the loop). a1 1 793 645 reads.
$8413 t TitleText ; The title page text: the copyright line, "Press S to start." and the key list
> Printed by TitlePage $803C ($FFD0). Every build read it once a title (a1 2). A print string: codes in the notes; the first glyph "C" stands for the copyright sign in the title's look.
@res text/TitleText.bin
$84FA t OptionsText ; The options page text: SELECT OPTIONS, the players, the courses, the six controls, the keys
> Printed by OptionsPage $806E: a1 21 times. The control lines name the choices of $B259 in order 1-6; the keyboard line adds "A=Slow, Q=Fast, O=Jump, p=Fire".
@res text/OptionsText.bin
$8655 t ScoreAdvanceText ; The score advance table's text: the title, the underline and the points of each enemy (shot / jumped)
> Printed by ScoreAdvancePage $815C: a1 20, t1 1. The "???" is the game's own: the low flyer's points for a shot (stage 1 table).
@res text/ScoreAdvanceText.bin
$8706 t DemoText ; "DEMO" in font 1, then font 2 selected
> Printed by DemoPage $81A1: a1 once.
@res text/DemoText.bin
$8716 t TapeErrorText ; "Tape error - hit any key.": printed by TapeFailed when a load or verify fails, never read
> Selected at $82C2 for any failed LD_BYTES whose last byte read was not $6B. Not read in any recording (game, a1, g1, t1, p2, h1, i1, i2, h2): known only from the code.
@res text/TapeErrorText.bin
$873B t RobotronWarningText ; "WARNING- You have attempted to load an Atarisoft Robotron hi-score table. Please try again!!!", never read
> Selected at $82BB when the failed block's flag byte was $6B. Not read in any recording (game, a1, g1, t1, p2, h1, i1, i2, h2): known only from the code.
@res text/RobotronWarningText.bin

$87AC c FrameHLine ; Plots D pixels across from x H, row L ($FD80 one pixel each)
> Witness: a1 400 calls.
$87AD FrameHLineLoop
$87B7 c FrameVLine ; Plots D pixels down from x H, row L
> Witness: a1 400 calls.
$87B8 FrameVLineLoop
$87C2 c FrameRectangle ; A rectangle inset B pixels from the left and right edges and C from the top and bottom of the 256 x 192 screen
> Top and bottom lines 256 - 2B wide, sides 192 - 2C high. Witness: a1 200 calls.
$87F1 c FrameRectangles ; D rectangles, each one pixel further in (B, C + 1)
> Witness: a1 200 entries (50 calls).
$87FC c DrawPixelFrame ; The pages' pixel frame: rectangles at insets 0-3 and 8-11, two four-pixel bands around the screen
> Called by TitlePage, OptionsPage, HeroesPage, TapeShowMessage and HiScoreEntry. Witness: a1 25, g1 2, t1 3, p2 3, h1 5, h2 1.

$880D c PaintFrameAttrs ; The frame's two attribute rings: D on the outer cells (row 0, row 23, columns 0 and 31), E on the ring inside
> Witness: a1 2162 calls.
$8812 PaintFrameTop ; row 0 and the first cell of row 1 in D
$8818 PaintFrameInnerTop ; row 1 columns 1-30 in E
$8820 PaintFrameSides ; rows 2-21: D E at the left, E D at the right
$8832 PaintFrameInnerBottom ; row 22 columns 1-30 in E
$8838 PaintFrameBottom ; the last cell of row 22 and row 23 in D

$883D b FrameColourPairs 2 ; The frame's eight colour pairs (outer ring, inner ring): each inner colour is the next pair's outer one
> Read by CycleFrameColours (a1 270 reads of $883D).

$884D c CycleFrameColours ; The frame colours one step on: $B255 - 1 (0-7) picks a pair of FrameColourPairs for PaintFrameAttrs
> Called on every pass of TitlePage, OptionsPage, HeroesPage and the tape message, so the rings appear to move. Witness: a1 2162 calls, h1 422.

@module hiscores ; The hi-score tables, their page, the hi-score entry after GAME OVER
@about Two tables. The heroes: ten records of three initials and a four-byte BCD score at $8866. The all-time heroes: number 1, THE GREATEST, with a 20-letter name, a score, initials and the course point reached ($88AC-$88C8), then 39 records like the heroes' ($88C9-$89D9): 40 places. $88AC-$89D9 ($12E bytes) is the block the tape code saves, loads and verifies (MenuCheckTape); the heroes are not saved.
@about HiScoreEntry $8B8F, called at GAME OVER by $8F9F, checks the score $B272 against the last all-time and the last hero score, asks for the initials (EntryFieldEdit, three letters into $B289) and puts the record into both tables where it fits. A score over THE GREATEST's goes to GreatestEntry $8E4E, which asks for a name of up to 20 letters typed over the old one, then the initials.
@about Scores are four BCD bytes, the highest first, compared byte by byte by ScoreCompare. Texts are print strings of $FFD0 (codes in the notes).

$8866 b HeroesTable 7 ; The heroes: ten records of three initials and a four-byte BCD score, best first (MIK 1200 ... PJS 120 in the image)
> Drawn by HeroesDrawTables; the tenth score $88A8 is HiScoreEntry's threshold, and records shift down from the insert place (HeroesInsert). Not part of the tape block. h1 put MPA (score 1260) at the top.
$88AC b GreatestPoint ; THE GREATEST's course point (1-25, A-Z without O): $18 = Y. The first byte of the tape block $88AC-$89D9
> Written by GreatestEntry from $B27C; the HUD prints it after the hi-score ($91DD with $9258). Read in every build that drew the HUD (i1 12 reads).
$88AD t GreatestName ; THE GREATEST's name, 20 letters (SPIKE NOTWELL), ended by $1F for $FFD0
> Printed by HeroesDrawTables; GreatestEntry edits it in place (h2 typed STAGE ONE over SPIKE NOTWELL: the untyped tail stays, "STAGE ONEWELL").
$88C2 b GreatestScore 4 ; THE GREATEST's score, BCD (00 05 00 00 = 50000): the hi-score of the HUD
> Read by the HUD ($91DD), HeroesDrawTables and HiScoreEntry (the GreatestEntry test). i1 24 reads.
$88C6 t GreatestInitials ; THE GREATEST's initials (JAS)
$88C9 b AllTimeTable 7 ; The all-time heroes 2-40: 39 records of initials and BCD score, best first; the last score $89D6 is HiScoreEntry's first threshold
> h1 put MPA at 40 (the last record, $89D3). GreatestEntry shifts the whole table down and makes the old greatest number 2.

$89DA t HeroesTitleText ; "MOON PATROL HEROES"
> Printed by HeroesDrawTables $8AE0 (a1 2, h1 1, game 4).
@res text/HeroesTitleText.bin
$89FE t AllTimeTitleText ; "ALL TIME HEROES" and "1) " for THE GREATEST
@res text/AllTimeTitleText.bin
$8A1F t HeroesTapeKeysText ; "L=LOAD, J=SAVE, G=VERIFY HI-SCORES." at the foot of the page
@res text/HeroesTapeKeysText.bin

$8A4C c HeroesPrintRow ; One heroes line: rank ($B256), ")", the initials and the six-digit score at x D, row E; HL to the next record, E + 8
> The rank through $9369 (B = 1, C = 6), the score through $9367 (C = 6). Witness: a1 20 calls, h1 10.
$8A7F HeroesInitialsLoop

$8A96 c AllTimePrintRow ; One all-time line in the small font: rank, ")", initials, score at x D, row E; E + 6
> As HeroesPrintRow with narrower spacing and C = 4 for the print calls. Witness: a1 78 calls, h1 39.
$8AC9 AllTimeInitialsLoop

$8AE0 c HeroesDrawTables ; The hi-score page's tables: heroes 1-5 and 6-10 in two columns, THE GREATEST, all-time 2-40 in three columns
> HeroesTitleText; ranks 1-5 at x $1A and 6-10 at x $89 from row $1C (rank 10 as BCD $10); AllTimeTitleText, then THE GREATEST: GreatestName, its score six columns after the cursor, "(" its initials ")"; font 0, then the all-time ranks 2-$14 at x $14, $15-$27 at x $5E, $28-$40 at x $AA from row $59 (BCD counters); HeroesTapeKeysText; CycleFrameColours.
> Called by HeroesPage only. Witness: game 4, a1 2 (174, 21 208), h1 1 (4545).
$8AEF HeroesLeftColumn ; ranks 1-5
$8AFC ; ranks 6-10 at x $89
$8B00 HeroesRightColumn
$8B07 HeroesRankSet ; rank 10 printed as BCD $10
$8B13 ; THE GREATEST
$8B34 GreatestInitialsLoop
$8B40 ; font 0 for the all-time ranks
$8B50 AllTimeColumn1 ; ranks 2-14
$8B63 AllTimeColumn2 ; ranks 15-27
$8B76 AllTimeColumn3 ; ranks 28-40

$8B8F c HiScoreEntry ; GAME OVER: a score for the tables asks for the initials (or THE GREATEST's name) and goes into the all-time and heroes tables
> Called by the game over $8F9F with the player's score at $B272 and the player number at $B271. A score not over the last all-time score $89D6 and not over the tenth hero $88A8 returns at once. Otherwise: the screen cleared, the frame, $93C5 and $9136, "CONGRATULATIONS PLAYER n"; a score over THE GREATEST's goes to GreatestEntry $8E4E. Else "YOU ARE AN ATARI MP HERO, PLEASE ENTER YOUR INITIALS", "3 LETTERS MAXIMUM" (the digit printed before the tail of "20 LETTERS MAXIMUM"), EntryFieldEdit with InitialsField (three letters into $B289).
> Then the all-time table: a score over $89D6 is placed by HiScoreFindSlot (39 records), the records below shift down 7 bytes (LDDR), the initials and score go in. Then HeroesInsert does the same for the heroes (ten records). Equal scores do not enter: the older record stays above.
> Witness: entries game 4, g1 1 (4144), p2 1 (7830), h1 1 (4144-4524), h2 1 (488). The prompt: game, g1, p2, h1 ($8BE0); g1 and p2 never finished the initials (the scripts typed play keys, no ENTER); h1 did: all-time ($8C1D, place 40) and heroes ($8C58, place 1). game (c1's random play) entered once and only the heroes.
$8B9A ; not in the all-time table: is it in the heroes?
$8BA5 HiScoreQualifies ; the congratulations page
$8BBA ; the colour of row 3 copied to row 2
$8BD4 ; over THE GREATEST: the name entry
$8BE0 ; a hero: YOU ARE AN ATARI MP HERO
$8BF8 ; "3" before " LETTERS MAXIMUM"
$8C03 ; the initials field
$8C11 ; not over the last all-time score: only the heroes
$8C20 ; the all-time place, the rest shifted down
$8C44 AllTimeStoreEntry ; the initials and the score into the place
$8C58 HeroesInsert ; the heroes: not over the tenth score returns
$8C63 ; the heroes place, the rest shifted down
$8C87 HeroesStoreEntry ; the initials and the score into the place

$8C9C c EntryFieldClear ; Clears ten pixel lines of 28 bytes from the field's row ($B24E)
> Witness: g1 121 calls, h2 11.
$8CA1 EntryClearLine
$8CB2 EntryClearBytes

$8CBC c EntryFieldDraw ; Draws the field: cleared, AT x, y, its characters, a five-pixel line under the one at the cursor $B254
> The field descriptor is at $B24B: length, the buffer address, y, x (EntryFieldDescriptors). $B252 counts the characters left, $B253 the index. Witness: g1 121 calls, p2 615, h2 11.
$8CDD EntryFieldCharLoop
$8CE7 ; the cursor's character: a line 8 rows under the print position
$8CF1 EntryCursorLoop
$8CFB EntryFieldPrintChar

$8D0D c EntryFieldEdit ; Edits the field at $B24B with the ROM's LAST_K: characters, cursor left / right, DELETE; ENTER ends
> Cursor 0, FLAGS bit 3 and FLAGS2 bit 3 (CAPS LOCK: capitals) set, MODE 0. Draws the field, clears LAST_K and waits for the IM 1 handler to put a key there. ENTER returns. $20-$5E: stored at the cursor, cursor right (stops at the last place). 8 (cursor left): EditCursorLeft; 9 (cursor right): EditCursorRight; 12 (DELETE): EditDelete. Other codes are ignored. Typing over a longer old text leaves its tail (h2).
> Witness: entries g1 1 (4172, never left: no ENTER), p2 1 (7859, never left), h1 1 (4172-4524), h2 2 (517-1141 the name, then the initials, not finished), game 1. Characters typed: g1 120, p2 614, h1 3, h2 9. The keys 8, 9 and 12 never came ($8D5D, $8D7C and the direct jump to $8D6B never ran in any recording: known only from the code).
$8D20 EditRedraw
$8D23 EditWaitKey ; LAST_K = 0, wait for a key
$8D28 EditWaitLoop
$8D46 ; a character into the buffer, cursor right

$8D4E b InitialsField 5 ; Entry field descriptor (length, buffer, y, x): 3 letters into $B289 at y $7C, x $77
> Copied to $B24B before EntryFieldEdit by HiScoreEntry $8C03 (read in game, g1, p2, h1). The three descriptors InitialsField, GreatestNameField, GreatestInitialsField have one layout: length, the buffer address, y, x.
$8D53 b GreatestNameField 5 ; Entry field descriptor: THE GREATEST's 20 letters at $88AD, y $7C, x $44
> Used by GreatestEntry $8E96 (h2).
$8D58 b GreatestInitialsField 5 ; Entry field descriptor: the initials after the name, 3 letters into $B289 at y $A0, x $77
> Used by GreatestEntry $8EB6 (h2).

$8D5D c EditCursorLeft ; Cursor left (key 8, and after a DELETE), not below 0, then EntryFieldEdit's redraw
> Not executed in any recording (game, a1, g1, t1, p2, h1, i1, i2, h2): known only from the code. The labels below are EntryFieldEdit's other key paths.
$8D6B EditCursorRight ; cursor right, not past the last place (after every character; key 9 never came)
$8D7C EditDelete ; DELETE: the characters after the cursor move left one, a space at the end, then cursor left (never ran)
$8DA1 EditDeleteTail

$8DA6 c EditCursorAddress ; HL = the field's buffer + the cursor $B254
> Witness: g1 120 calls, p2 614, h2 9.

$8DB1 t HiScoreTexts ; The texts of the hi-score entry, printed in pieces; first INK 6 BRIGHT 1, printed after the player number
> All printed by HiScoreEntry and GreatestEntry with $FFD0; the pieces are reused ("3" + the tail of "20 LETTERS MAXIMUM", "YOU ARE " without its AT for THE GREATEST, "PLEASE ENTER YOUR " again after the name).
> CongratulationsText $8DB6: "CONGRATULATIONS PLAYER " (the player digit follows)
> YouAreText $8DDE: "YOU ARE " without the AT (THE GREATEST)
> AnAtariHeroText $8DF4: "AN ATARI MP HERO", AT, "PLEASE ENTER YOUR "
> AtariHeroText $8DF6: " ATARI MP HERO" and the rest (after THE GREATEST)
> PleaseEnterText $8E07: "PLEASE ENTER YOUR " without the AT (after the name)
> LettersAtText $8E27: AT, INK 5 for the letters line
> LettersMaximumText $8E2F: " LETTERS MAXIMUM" (after a "3")
> GreatestAtText $8E40: AT for "YOU ARE THE GREATEST"
> InitialsAtText $8E44: AT for the initials prompt after the name
@res text/HiScoreTexts.bin
$8DB6 CongratulationsText
$8DDB YouAreAtText ; AT, then "YOU ARE "
$8DDE YouAreText
$8DE7 TheGreatestText ; "THE GREATEST"
$8DF4 AnAtariHeroText
$8DF6 AtariHeroText
$8E04 ; AT for "PLEASE ENTER YOUR "
$8E07 PleaseEnterText
$8E1A InitialsText ; "INITIALS"
$8E23 NameText ; "NAME", then AT, INK 5
$8E27 LettersAtText
$8E2D TwentyLettersText ; "20 LETTERS MAXIMUM"
$8E2F LettersMaximumText
$8E40 GreatestAtText
$8E44 InitialsAtText
$8E48 ; leftover: " ALSO", never read (no operand points here)

$8E4E c GreatestEntry ; A score over THE GREATEST's: the name (20 letters, over the old one), the initials, the all-time table shifted down
> Jumped to from HiScoreEntry ($8BDD). Prints "YOU ARE THE GREATEST ATARI MP HERO, PLEASE ENTER YOUR NAME, 20 LETTERS MAXIMUM". The all-time records 2-39 shift down one place (the 40th is lost) and the old greatest's initials and score become number 2; its name is not kept: GreatestNameField edits GreatestName in place. Then "PLEASE ENTER YOUR INITIALS" (GreatestInitialsField into $B289); the score, the initials and the course point $B27C go to GreatestScore, GreatestInitials and GreatestPoint, and HeroesInsert puts the record into the heroes too.
> Witness: h2 once (513-1141); the shift and the name ran ($8E81 266 bytes moved), the initials prompt was reached (the second EntryFieldEdit) but not finished, so $8EC4-$8EE0 never ran in any recording (game, a1, g1, t1, p2, h1, i1, i2, h2): known only from the code.
$8E78 ; all-time 2-39 down one place
$8E83 ; the old greatest becomes number 2
$8E96 ; the name, in place
$8EA4 ; then the initials
$8EC4 ; the new greatest: score, initials, course point

$8EE3 c HiScoreFindSlot ; Walks a table up from its last score HL for B records while the score DE is not less: HL = the score field of the new record's place
> Each step ScoreCompare; a record with a higher score stops the walk and the place is the one below it (HL + 7); $B250 / $B252 keep HL and DE. Witness: game 1, h1 2 (4524: all-time, then heroes).
$8EEA FindSlotLoop
$8EF4 ; one record up
$8F01 FindSlotDone ; the place below the higher record

$8F06 c ScoreCompare ; Compares the four BCD bytes at DE with those at HL, highest first: carry if (DE) < (HL), Z if equal
> Witness: game 16 calls, g1 2, p2 3, h1 16, h2 2.
$8F08 ScoreCompareLoop

@module gamestart ; The game start, the lives and the players, GAME OVER, the life start and the HUD
@about GameStart $8F10 (from the options page, S) sets up both player records, then plays lives: the "PLAYER n" banner, the life start LifeStart $90D3 and the main loop $9696, until the lives of every player are gone; GAME OVER (or GAME OVER PLAYER n in a two-player game), the hi-score entries $8B8F and back to the heroes table $81D0.
@about The current player is the 27-byte record at $B271; the other player's is at $B28C, swapped through $B2A7 by PlayerSwap $9022. Fields the code here uses: $B271 the player number, $B272-$B275 the score (8 BCD digits), $B276-$B279 the score of the next extra life, $B27A the course script pointer, $B27C the point reached (bits 0-4 the point number, bit 5 the Champion course), $B27D the scenery (0 or $FF), $B27E the warning lamps (0-3), $B27F the restart pointer, $B281 the start of the segment last read, $B283 the player still in the game, $B284 the lives in reserve, $B285-$B286 the time (BCD seconds), $B287 the clock's frame count, $B288 flags (bit 3 a mine entered, bit 4 the hold, bit 7 the tune off).
@about The HUD (rows 0-2): the player labels 1P / 2P and the scores with the point letter reached, the hi-score, four buggy icons for the lives, the course name and the progress bar, TIME and POINT, and the three warning lamps with DANGER! and CAUTION. The scenery colours and the mountains or city backdrop are set here too.
$8F10 c GameStart ; The game start: both player records set, then the first life of player 1
> Entered by a JP from the options page ($8244) when S is pressed. Makes sure the record at $B271 is player 1's (PlayerSwap if $B271 holds 2), then fills both records, player 2 first (D = 2, 1): the player number, the score and the extra-life score 00010000 ($B277 = 1), the course script $B7E8 (Beginner, $B27C = 0) or $BB24 (Champion, $B27C = $20, from the course choice $B258), the scenery, lamps and pointers zeroed, $B283 = 1 (in the game), four lives, the time zeroed, and $B288 cleared but for bit 7 (the tune off survives). A one-player game then zeroes player 2's $B283 and lives, so the swaps after each life always come back to player 1.
> GameStartLife $8F71 is the start of every life: one life taken from the reserve ($B284), the screen cleared in white on black (ClearScreen $9044), "PLAYER n" printed (TextPlayer $9073 and the digit) and held for 100 frames (Wait100Frames $903D, 300 frames in a two-player game), the screen cleared again, then LifePlay $90CC: the life start and the main loop until the buggy dies. It goes on at GameAfterLife $8F9F.
> Witness: game 5 runs (1448-16318), a1 1 (1314), g1 1 (153), p2 1 (334), h1 1 (153), i1 1 (153), i2 1 (334). GameStartLife: g1 4 lives, p2 8 (four each of two players), i1 / i2 1 (immune).
$8F1A GameStartInit ; D = 2 records to fill (player 2, then player 1), E = 0
$8F1D GameStartRecord ; the player number
$8F24 GameStartScore ; the score $B272-$B275 and the top byte of the extra-life score zeroed
$8F28 ; the extra-life score 00010000: the first extra life at 10000 points
$8F2F ; the course script: Beginner $B7E8, point base 0
$8F3A ; Champion: $BB24, point base $20 (bit 5 of $B27C)
$8F3F GameStartCourse ; $B27A-$B27B the course pointer, $B27C the point base
$8F47 GameStartClear ; $B27D-$B282 zeroed: the scenery, the lamps, the restart and segment pointers
$8F4B ; $B283 = 1: in the game; $B284 = 4 lives
$8F53 GameStartTime ; the time $B285-$B287 zeroed, the flags $B288 kept only for bit 7 (the tune off)
$8F5B ; into the other record: the second pass fills the other player
$8F68 ; one player: player 2 is out of the game, no lives
$8F6E GameStartNextPlayer ; the other player's record in
$8F71 GameStartLife ; a life: one life from the reserve, the PLAYER n banner
$8F8F ; a two-player game holds the banner 300 frames
$8F97 GameStartPlay ; the screen cleared, the life played (LifePlay)

$8F9F c GameAfterLife ; After a life: the next player's turn, or GAME OVER, the hi-score entries and the heroes table
> Reached when LifePlay $90CC returns (the buggy has died: the main loop $9696 returns after the death). With lives left the turn passes to the other player when that player is still in the game ($B283), else back to this one ($8F6E swaps the record back), and GameStartLife $8F71 plays the next life.
> No lives left: $B283 = 0 (out of the game). One player: 8 cells cleared on rows 9 and 10 ($484C, $486C) and "GAME OVER" printed (TextGameOver $9086). Two players: 14 cells cleared and "GAME OVER PLAYER n" (TextGameOverPlayer $909B and the digit). 200 frames held. When the other player is still in the game, that player's next life is played. Else the game is over: one player, the record is put back to player 1 when needed and its hi-score entry run ($8B8F); two players, the entry of each player in turn. Then JP $81D0, the heroes table.
> Witness: game 19 runs, a1 2, g1 4, p2 8, h1 4, h2 1 (the immune runs never die). GAME OVER: g1 1 (~3950), h1 1, h2 1, game 4; GAME OVER PLAYER: p2 2 (both players, the second at 7700). The two-player end GameOverBoth $9006 ran in p2 once; the second player's entry $9009-$900F is not executed in any recording (game, a1, g1, t1, p2, h1, i1, i2, h2): p2 stopped in the first entry. Known only from the code.
$8FA5 ; lives left: the other player's turn when that player is still in the game, else this player again
$8FB0 GameOverPlayer ; no lives: out of the game
$8FBA ; one player: 8 cells cleared on two rows, GAME OVER
$8FD2 GameOverPlayerN ; two players: 14 cells cleared, GAME OVER PLAYER n
$8FF0 GameOverHold ; 200 frames, then the other player's next life if still in the game
$9006 GameOverBoth ; two players: the hi-score entry of each player, then the heroes table
$9012 GameOverOne ; one player: back to player 1's record, its hi-score entry, the heroes table
$901C GameOverEntry

$9022 c PlayerSwap ; Swaps the current player record $B271 with the other player's $B28C (27 bytes each, through $B2A7)
> $B271 -> $B2A7, $B28C -> $B271, $B2A7 -> $B28C, with the alternate registers so BC, DE, HL are kept. Called by GameStart (twice per record set-up), after each life and at GAME OVER, and twice by HudDraw $9136 (to print the other player's score).
> Witness: game 100 runs (1448-16320), a1 16, g1 22, p2 30, h1 22, i1 28, i2 28, h2 4.

$903D c Wait100Frames ; Waits 100 frames: EI and 100 HALTs (the PLAYER n banner, GAME OVER, the bonus screen)
> Called by GameStart, GameAfterLife and the sector bonus screen $A4EE. Interrupts are on in IM 1 here (the ROM's handler): stage 1 of the power-on recording (WALKTHROUGH 1) is this loop resumed by the interrupt's RET.
> Witness: game 28 runs (1452-16316), a1 3, g1 6, p2 28, h1 6, i1 6, i2 6, h2 2. The HALT $9040: 100 a call (game 2800, p2 2800, i1 600).
$9040 Wait100Loop

$9044 c ClearScreen ; Clears the screen: pixels to 0, the attributes to A; ATTR-P, BORDCR and the border follow A
> A = the attribute (7 white on black in the game, 0 for the title). ATTR-P $5C8D = A; BORDCR $5C48 = A AND $38 (the paper), with ink 7 added when the paper is dark (bit 5 clear); the border = the paper colour (OUT $FE); ($5CC6) = the paper colour XOR 7.
> Called by the entry $8000, the title and menus ($8064, $81D0, $825A), the hi-score entry $8B8F, GameStart and the bonus screen $A4EE. Witness: game 49 runs (from 256), a1 52, g1 10, t1 4, p2 19, h1 13, i1 13, i2 14, h2 1.
$9063 ClearScreenBorder ; BORDCR, the border colour

$9073 t TextPlayer ; Print string (PrintText $FFD0): white on black, AT x $68 y $55, font 1, "PLAYER " (the digit follows)
> The print codes of $FDCC that these strings use: $10 n ink, $11 n paper, $13 n bright, $16 x y a position in pixels, $18, $19, $1A n the font; $1F ends the string (PrintText $FFD0 stops on it). Printed by GameStartLife $8F7A: read game 20, a1 3, g1 4, p2 8, h1 4, i1 1, i2 1 (one read a life).
@res text/TextPlayer.bin
$9086 t TextGameOver ; Print string: AT x $65 y $55, font 1, "GAME OVER" (one player)
> Printed by GameAfterLife $8FCD: read game 4, g1 1, h1 1, h2 1.
@res text/TextGameOver.bin
$909B t TextGameOverPlayer ; Print string: AT x $4B y $55, font 1, "GAME OVER PLAYER " (the digit follows)
> Printed by GameAfterLife $8FE5: read p2 2.
@res text/TextGameOverPlayer.bin
$90B8 t TextNoDate ; An empty print string ($1F only): LifeStart prints it, and the build stamp after it is never printed
> LifeStart $9123 prints from here at every life start (read game 20, a1 4, g1 4, p2 8, h1 4, i1 6, i2 6). The $1F ends the string at once, so the stamp BuildStamp $90B9 that follows is never read (no reads in any recording). LifeStart prints three more strings of this shape: $7F00, $A182, $B2C2, each a $1F before a stamp.
@res text/TextNoDate.bin
$90B9 t BuildStamp ; A build time stamp, never printed: $00 $21, font 2, "20:15 29/10/84", $1F
> Not read in any recording (game, a1, g1, t1, p2, h1, i1, i2, h2): known only from the bytes. The other stamps: $7F01 "03:35 30/10/84", $A183 "03:40 30/10/84", $B2C3 "02:45 29/10/84".
@res text/BuildStamp.bin

$90CC c LifePlay ; One life: LifeStart, then the main loop until the buggy dies
> Witness: game 20 runs (1554-16323), a1 3, g1 4, p2 8, h1 4, i1 1, i2 1.
$90D2 c LifePlayDone ; The return of LifePlay, reached when the main loop $9696 returns after a death
> An entry of its own because h2 began in play with this address on the stack (h2: entered at 287). Witness: game 19 runs, a1 2, g1 4, p2 8, h1 4, h2 1.

$90D3 c LifeStart ; The life start: the colours, the HUD, the backdrop, the buggy, the shots, IM 2
> SceneryColours $95B0, the lamps $B27E to 0, HudDraw $9136, ScenerySetup $9541. $B223 = $FF; the buggy alive, not dying ($B216 / $B217 = $FF), its box at $B212 = y $92, x $38 (drawn at y $89, sprite $00 through $A826). $B237 (UFOs leave), $B220, $B234, $B240 = 0. ShotBoxes $9681 (21 bytes) copied to $5B60 (the printer buffer). The ground pattern pointer $B232 = $A9AF (the plain ground), $B23E = $B4E6 (the tune). Four empty strings printed ($7F00, TextNoDate $90B8, $A182, $B2C2: build stamps cut off by a $1F). Then SetIm2 $A7D4: IM 2 for play.
> Called by LifePlay, the title's demo $8064 and the bonus screen $A4EE (after each sector). Witness: game 20 runs (1554-16323), a1 4, g1 4, p2 8, h1 4, i1 6, i2 6.
$90E0 ; the buggy alive, not dying
$90EB ; the buggy's box: y $92, x $38
$90F1 ; drawn at y $89: sprite 0 ($A826)
$9103 ; the shot boxes to $5B60
$910E ; the ground pattern: plain ground
$9114 ; the tune from its start
$911A ; four empty print strings (build stamps never printed)
$9132 ; IM 2 for play

$9136 c HudDraw ; Draws the HUD (rows 0-2): colours, icons, lamps, labels, scores, hi-score, TIME, POINT, the course name
> Attributes of rows 0-2 from HudAttributes $9621; the player icon HudPlayerIcon $9469 at $4000; the HUD panel HudPanel $9481 (64x24 pixels at x $50, the four buggy icons, the course marks and the progress bar frame) through $AFAB with $B247 = $1800 (lines 0-$17), $B249 = $0850 (x $50, 8 bytes a line); the lamp glyph HudLampGlyph $9479 in column 27 of rows 0-2; HudTextLamps $93D3 (DANGER! and CAUTION, ink = paper until HudLamps colours them); a few attributes of column 0 (rows 1-3).
> Then HudLives $9263, HudLamps $927E, HudProgress $92AA; the label 1P (row 1 when two players, else row 2) and 2P (row 2), the current player's label inverted (two cells at $4020 or $4040); the hi-score from the heroes table ($88C2, four BCD bytes at x $10 y 1) with "-" and its point letter ($88AC); both players' scores (HudPrintScore $92DA, PlayerSwap, again, PlayerSwap); TIME and POINT (HudTextTimePoint $93FF) and the point letter (HudPrintPoint $9247); BEGINNER or CHAMPION from bit 5 of $B27C after 7 cells cleared at $4053; the time (HudPrintTime $922A, its flag $B239 cleared first).
> Called by LifeStart, the hi-score entry $8B8F, the bonus screen $A4EE and the course switches $BB07 / $BED4. Witness: game 21 runs (1554-16321), a1 4, g1 5, p2 9, h1 5, i1 12, i2 12, h2 1. 2P and the inverted label: p2 only ($93F9 read 9 times in p2).
$9149 HudIconLoop ; 8 lines of two bytes
$9154 ; the HUD panel: lines 0-$17 at x $50, 8 bytes a line ($AFAB)
$9166 ; the lamp glyph in column 27 of rows 0, 1, 2
$916B HudLampGlyphRow
$9170 HudLampGlyphLine
$9176 ; HL + $F820: back up 8 lines and one row down
@isub $9176 LD DE,$F820
$917D ; DANGER! and CAUTION, invisible until the lamps are lit
$9183 ; column 0 of rows 3, 2, 1: attributes 7, $16, $16
@isub $9186 LD DE,-32
$919A ; 1P: row 1 (y 9) in a two-player game, else row 2 (y $11)
$91A9 HudPlayerLabel ; AT x 2 y E, then the label
$91BF ; two players: 2P, then the current player's label inverted
$91D2 HudLabelInvert
$91DD HudHiScore ; the hi-score from the heroes table at x $10 y 1, "-", its point letter
$91F3 ; both players' scores: the other one's through two swaps
$91FF ; TIME and POINT, the point letter
$9208 ; CHAMPION when bit 5 of $B27C is set, else BEGINNER
$9215 HudCourseName ; 7 cells cleared at $4053, then the name
$9222 ; the time printed now: its flag cleared first

$922A c HudPrintTime ; Prints the time $B285-$B286 (4 digits at x $B3 y 1) once after each clock change
> Does nothing while the flag $B239 is non-zero; the clock $7F98 clears it. Then sets it, clears 3 cells at $4017 and prints the two BCD bytes of the time ($9369, 6 pixels a digit).
> Called once a main-loop pass ($96FA) and by HudDraw. Witness: game 1597 runs, a1 296, g1 498, p2 673, h1 498, i1 3613, i2 3947, h2 41. Printing ($9230): i1 523 times, about once a second of play.
$9230 ; the time changed: print it
@isub $923D LD DE,$B301

$9247 c HudPrintPoint ; Prints the letter of the point reached ($B27C) on the HUD after POINT
> One cell cleared at $4038, the position from HudTextPointAt $9427 (x $C2 y 9, font 2), then falls into HudPrintPointLetter with A = $B27C.
> Called by HudDraw and when a point is passed (ObjPoint $9980). Witness: game 21 runs, a1 4, g1 5, p2 9, h1 5, i1 37 (12 HUD draws + 25 points), i2 39, h2 1.

$9258 c HudPrintPointLetter ; Prints the point letter of A: bits 0-4 the point number, 1 = A, O skipped
> A AND $1F, then $40 added, plus 1 from 15 on: 1-14 are A-N, 15-25 are P-Z ('O' is no point). 0 prints '@' (no point passed yet). Called by HudPrintPoint, HudPrintScore (the score's point letter), HudDraw (the hi-score's) and the bonus screen $A4EE. Witness: game 88 runs, i1 382, i2 519.

$9263 c HudLives ; Shows the lives in reserve ($B284) as buggy icons: up to four, colour pairs at $580A-$5811
> Four pairs of cells on row 0 (columns 10-17, the icons of HudPanel): a pair whose index is below the lives gets attribute $1F (white on magenta, seen), the others $1B (ink = paper, hidden). Called by HudDraw and by ScoreAddBcd $9311 when an extra life is given. Witness: game 21 runs, i1 14 (12 HUD draws + the two extra lives at 10000 and 30000), i2 15.
$9270 HudLivesLoop
$9277 HudLivesSet

$927E c HudLamps ; Colours the warning lamps from $B27E (0-3): 3 rows of 5 attributes at $581B from HudLampColours
> The pattern is HudLampColours + 15 x ($B27E AND 3): column 27 the lamp, columns 28-31 the text. 0 lights the bottom lamp green; 1 the middle lamp yellow and CAUTION white; 2 the top lamp red and DANGER! white; 3 both, red and yellow. Called by HudDraw and by the lamp object ObjLamps $99A3. Witness: game 21 runs, i1 36, i2 41; the patterns read (HudLampColours reads) in i1: 0 15 times, 1 13, 2 6, 3 2; in i2: 13, 10, 10, 8.
$9297 HudLampsRow
$929A HudLampsCell

$92AA c HudProgress ; Plots the course progress bar: a 2x2 dot every 2 pixels from x $57 to 2 x point + $56, y $13
> Nothing at point 0. Uses HudPlot $92CD (the OR plot $FD80, H = x, L = y). Called by HudDraw and when a point is passed (ObjPoint). Witness: game 21 runs, i1 37, i2 39; the dots ($92CD) i1 1900.
$92B7 HudProgressLoop

$92CD c HudPlot ; Plots one pixel at H = x, L = y ($FD80), keeping HL
> Witness: i1 1900 runs, i2 1912 (only from HudProgress, no dots at point 0).

$92D3 c ScoreAdd ; Adds DEBC (8 BCD digits) to the score, gives the extra lives, prints the score
> ScoreAddBcd $9311, then HudPrintScore $92DA. The values the callers pass (BC, DE = 0): 100 the rock shot or hit (ObjRock $9A21), 80 the rock jumped ($9A3E), 100 the crater jumped ($9AD8), 50 the mine jumped ($9B36), 25 the kill $A7DD; the other handlers from $9B6B on (annotator 03) and the bonus $A4EE pass their own.
> Witness: game 25 runs (2029-16149), a1 4, g1 16, p2 16, h1 16, i1 291, i2 424, h2 3.

$92DA c HudPrintScore ; Prints the current player's score at x $10 (row 1 for player 1 of two, else row 2), "-", the point letter
> E = y: $11 (row 2), or 9 (row 1) for player 1 of a two-player game; the record of player 2 in a one-player game prints nothing. 8 cells cleared at $4022 or $4042, the 8 digits ($9364, 6 pixels apart, leading zeros blank), "-", the point letter of $B27C. Witness: game 67 runs, a1 12, g1 26, p2 34, h1 26, i1 340, i2 475, h2 5. Player 1 of two ($92EB): p2 34.
$92EB HudScoreTwo
$92F1 HudScoreAt ; HL = $4022 + $20 when y is $11

$9311 c ScoreAddBcd ; Adds DEBC to the score $B272-$B275 (BCD) and gives an extra life each time it passes $B276-$B279
> C to $B275, B to $B274, E to $B273, D to $B272, each with DAA and the carry. Below 60000 points ($B273 < 6) the score is compared with the extra-life score $B276-$B279: when reached, a life is added ($B284), HudLives shows it, and the extra-life score goes up 20000 ($B277 + 2). The extra lives: 10000, 30000, 50000; none from 60000 on.
> Witness: game 25 runs, i1 291, i2 424; the extra life ($9344-$935D) i1 2, i2 3. The carry into the top byte ($932C) and the carry of the extra-life score into $B276 ($935E-$9363) are not executed in any recording (game, a1, g1, t1, p2, h1, i1, i2, h2): known only from the code.
$931D ScoreAddHundreds
$9325 ScoreAddTenThousands
$932D ScoreAddMillions
$9330 ; from 60000 points on, no more extra lives
$9336 ; score >= the extra-life score?
$934E ; an extra life
$9355 ; the next one 20000 points further

$9364 c HudPrintScoreDigits ; HL = the score $B272: falls into PrintBcd4
> Witness: game 46 runs, i1 328, i2 463.

$9367 c PrintBcd4 ; Prints 4 BCD bytes (8 digits) from HL: B = 4, then PrintBcd
> Also called by the hi-score tables ($8A4C, $8A96, $8AE0) and HudDraw. Witness: game 267 runs, a1 112, i1 340, i2 475.

$9369 c PrintBcd ; Prints B BCD bytes from HL at x D y E, C pixels a digit, leading zeros left blank (the last digit always shown)
> Each digit goes out as $16 x y and the character through $FDCC. Called by PrintBcd4, HudPrintTime and the hi-score tables. Witness: routine entries game 711, a1 255, g1 96, p2 142, h1 195, i1 1017, i2 1248, h2 11 (PrintBcd4 falls into it, the time print calls it).
$9387 PrintBcdNext ; after the first digit shown: every digit
$9390 PrintBcdHigh ; the high digit of the byte
$93A7 PrintBcdLow ; the low digit of the byte
$93AA PrintBcdLowDigit

$93C5 c ClearCells ; Clears C cells from the screen address HL (8 pixel lines each)
> Witness: game 345 runs, a1 61, g1 104, p2 156, h1 104, i1 1035, i2 1268, h2 15.
$93C6 ClearCellsLoop
$93C9 ClearCellsLines

$93D3 t HudTextLamps ; Print string: ink 1 paper 1, AT x $E0 y 2 "DANGER!", AT x $E0 y $0A "CAUTION" (the lamp texts)
> Printed by HudDraw $9180 in blue on blue: HudLamps colours them. game 21 reads.
@res text/HudTextLamps.bin
$93F4 t HudText1P ; Print string: font 2, "1P" (HudDraw puts the position first)
@res text/HudText1P.bin
$93F9 t HudText2P ; Print string: AT x 2 y $11, "2P" (two players only: p2 9 reads)
@res text/HudText2P.bin
$93FF t HudTextTimePoint ; Print string: AT x $9B y 1 "TIME", AT x $9E y 9 "POINT"
@res text/HudTextTimePoint.bin
$940F t HudTextBeginner ; Print string: AT x $9B y $11 "BEGINNER" (game 21 reads)
@res text/HudTextBeginner.bin
$941B t HudTextChampion ; Print string: AT x $9B y $11 "CHAMPION" (read i1 1: the Champion course after Z; i2 11)
@res text/HudTextChampion.bin
$9427 t HudTextPointAt ; Print string: AT x $C2 y 9, font 2 (the point letter follows: HudPrintPoint)
@res text/HudTextPointAt.bin
$942D b HudLampColours 5 ; The warning lamp attributes: 4 patterns ($B27E 0-3) of 3 rows x 5 cells (the lamp, then the text)
> Read by HudLamps $927E: pattern = $942D + 15 x $B27E. Column 27 the lamp glyph, columns 28-31 the text. $08 / $09 unlit (black or blue ink on blue), $0C green, $0E yellow, $0A red, $0F white text.
$942D ; 0: all clear: the bottom lamp green
$943C ; 1: the middle lamp yellow, CAUTION
$944B ; 2: the top lamp red, DANGER!
$945A ; 3: red and yellow, DANGER! and CAUTION
$9469 b HudPlayerIcon 2 ; A 16x8 icon drawn at the top left of the HUD ($4000) by HudDraw
$9479 b HudLampGlyph 8 ; The lamp glyph (a round light), drawn in column 27 of rows 0-2 by HudDraw (game 63 reads)
$9481 b HudPanel 8 ; The HUD panel: 24 lines of 8 bytes at x $50, rows 0-2: the four buggy icons, the course marks, the progress bar frame
> Drawn by $AFAB from HudDraw. Lines 0-7: four 16x8 buggies (HudLives shows or hides each by its colour); lines 10-14: small marks over the bar; lines 16-21: the frame of the progress bar that HudProgress fills. game 21 reads.
$94C1 ; lines 8-9: empty
$94D1 ; lines 10-15: the marks over the bar
$9501 ; lines 16-23: the progress bar frame

$9541 c ScenerySetup ; The backdrop: the mountains or the city for $B27D, drawn on rows 8-11 and the scroll pointers set
> Mountains ($B27D = 0): $B22C = $E340, $B22E = $E780, $B22A = $E720; the city: $E780, $EBC0, $EB60. $B228 / $B224 = 0, $B226 = $EFA0. Draws 4 rows of 32 columns from $EBC0 at $4800 (rows 8-11) and 4 rows from ($B22C) at $4880 (rows 12-15) with SceneryDrawRows $9590, then copies 32 bytes from the ROM at $2000 to the pixel line $5080 (row 20). The scrollers that use these pointers are the main loop's ($A846, $A89A ...).
> Called by LifeStart and the title $8064 (with $B27D = $FF). Witness: game 20 runs, a1 24, g1 4, t1 1, p2 8, h1 4, i1 6, i2 6. The city: game 0, i1 2 (the sectors from F and Q).
$9559 SceneryStore

$9590 c SceneryDrawRows ; Draws 32 columns x 4 rows of 8 lines from DE (column by column) at the screen address HL
> Witness: game 40 runs, i1 12 (two calls each ScenerySetup).
$9593 SceneryColumn
$9596 SceneryCell
$9598 SceneryLine

$95B0 c SceneryColours ; Picks the scenery colour set ($B27D: SceneryColourSets $9617 or $961C) and paints rows 3-23
> $B230 = the set. Rows 3-7 = set +0, 12-15 = +2, 16-19 = +3, 20-21 = +4, then SceneryColoursRepaint: rows 8-11 = +1, rows 22-23 = +4 paper only. Called by LifeStart and the title $8064. Witness: game 20 runs, a1 24, t1 1, i1 6, i2 6; the city set ($95B9) a1 21, i1 2, i2 2.
$95BC SceneryColoursSet

$95F2 c SceneryColoursRepaint ; Paints rows 8-11 with the set's colour +1 and rows 22-23 with the paper of +4, every main-loop pass
> Called once a pass from MainLoopCalls $96FA (so the colours the sprites leave there are wiped) and by SceneryColours. Witness: game 1596 runs, a1 315, g1 497, p2 672, h1 497, i1 3606, i2 3940, h2 40.

$9617 b SceneryColourSets 5 ; The two scenery colour sets, 5 attributes each: rows 3-7, 8-11, 12-15, 16-19, 20-23
> Picked by SceneryColours from $B27D: 0 the mountains, $FF the city ($B27D comes from the sector-end record of the bonus screen $A4EE). The byte +1 is read every pass (SceneryColoursRepaint); +3 is read by other routines through $B230 too (i2: 2094 reads of $961A).
$9617 ; the mountains
$961C ; the city
$9621 b HudAttributes 32 ; The attributes of the HUD rows 0-2, copied to $5800 by HudDraw
$9681 b ShotBoxes ; The shots' boxes, copied to $5B60 at each life start: y, x, height, width of a flat 3x6 shot (+1 byte), then four 4x3 boxes
> LifeStart copies these 21 bytes to the printer buffer $5B60. y = $FF means no shot. The rock handler ObjRock $99C3 tests its box against the first one (the forward shot) with $A7FE and sets its x ($5B62) to $FF on a hit. The names forward / up shot are from the shapes and from the rock (a ground object) testing only the first box; the writers of these boxes and the users of the four 4-byte boxes (height 4, width 3) are outside this range.
$9681 ; the forward shot: y $FF (none), x 0, height 3, width 6, one more byte
$9686 ; the up shots, 4 bytes each: y $FF, x 0, height 4, width 3

@module course ; The main loop, the course interpreter, the course commands and the first object handlers
@about The main loop MainLoop $9696 reads the course script one segment at a time with the interpreter CourseRun $9751 and runs the call list MainLoopCalls $96FA once a pass; when the buggy has died it returns. One pass of the list runs the object list at $BEF8 (ObjectListRun $9745) with the same interpreter.
@about The script ($B7E8 Beginner, $BB24 Champion) is a list of command words, each followed by its data: CourseRun jumps to the word with HL past it, and the command returns HL past its data. A segment is SegmentLength $976C (the passes until the next segment), then the commands, then SegmentEnd $9760. Most commands are spawners: for each data byte up to $FF (the delay in object-list passes before the object acts) they append an object record to the list at the write pointer $B261: the handler's word, the delay, then the handler's own fields. SegmentEnd writes the list's end word $975E.
@about The object list is the same kind of program: ObjectListRun runs every record's handler, which counts its delay down and then acts; a finished object puts a "skip" routine in its word (the RET end of its own handler), so the record stays and costs a few instructions. The list starts again at $BEF8 at every MainLoop entry (life start, after each bonus screen, the course switch).
@about The record fields used here: +0 the handler word, +2 the delay, then for the obstacles the sprite, y, x, height, width (the box $A7FE compares with the buggy's at $B212 and the forward shot's at $5B60). The handlers from $9B6B on are in the next module.
$9696 c MainLoop ; The main loop of a life or a sector: reads a course segment when its time is up, runs the call list
> Saves SP at $B25F (the bonus screen $A4EE and the course switches $BB07 / $BED4 come back here with LD SP,($B25F) / JP $9696), enables the object list ($B238 = 0), starts it at $BEF8 ($B261 the write pointer) and sets the restart pointer $B27F to the course pointer $B27A. Then MainLoopSegment $96AA reads the next segment (CourseRun $9751 with DE = the write pointer) and MainLoopPass runs MainLoopCalls.
> MainLoopPass $96BB: when the buggy's death has run its course ($B216 = 8): the last frame erased (sprite $26 at y - 9), $B216 / $B217 = $FF, the course pointer back to the restart point $B27F, SetIm1 $A7CB, and RET: the life is over (LifePlay $90CC returns). Else the pass counter $B23B goes down by 1 and the segment time $B23C by 1; at 0 the segment start $B281 = the course pointer and the next segment is read.
> One pass has no frame sync: 3601 passes in about 30 175 frames of i1 (WALKTHROUGH 1). Witness: game 20 runs (1559-16753), a1 3, g1 4, p2 8, h1 4, i1 7, i2 7 (the life start, five bonus screens, the course switch); segments read ($96AA) i1 37, i2 40; deaths ($96C5) game 19, a1 2, g1 4, p2 8, h1 4, h2 1.
$969E ; the object list from $BEF8
$96A4 ; the restart point = where this life (or sector) starts
$96AA MainLoopSegment ; the next segment: commands append objects at the write pointer
$96BB MainLoopPass ; one pass of the call list; the death ends the life
$96C5 ; the death is over: erase, the restart point, IM 1, return
$96E3 MainLoopTimer ; the pass counter and the segment time
$96F2 ; the time is up: remember the segment start, read the next one

$96FA c MainLoopCalls ; The call list of one main-loop pass: keys, the buggy, shots, scrollers, objects, ground, hold
> In order: the tune key $AF15, BreakKeyTest $9737 (result ignored), $ACF1, HudPrintTime $922A, $A846, $AAD3, $AA0F, $AB37, $AD61, ObjectListRun $9745, SceneryColoursRepaint $95F2, $AB37, $A8FD, $A89A, $AB37, $AF31, the ground $A932, the hold key $AECE, $AB37, $AF31.
> Called by MainLoopPass and the title's demo $8064. Witness: game 1576 runs (1559-16757), a1 292, g1 493, p2 664, h1 493, i1 3601, i2 3935, h2 40.

$9737 c BreakKeyTest ; Reads the key '0' (row $EFFE): returns NZ when it is pressed; the caller ignores it
> IN of port $EFFE, OR $FE, INC A: Z when '0' is up. MainLoopCalls never tests the flag, and the stub CourseBreakToBasic $9740 after it is never called: a debug exit left in (a1 pressed '0', nothing happened). Witness: game 1576 runs, a1 292, i1 3601, i2 3935.

$9740 b CourseBreakToBasic 5 ; Never called: the bytes of CALL SetIm1 $A7CB, RST 8, DEFB $14 (the report L BREAK into program)
> No reference to it in the image, never executed or read in any recording (game, a1, g1, t1, p2, h1, i1, i2, h2): known only from the bytes. Kept as data: as code the walk would run on into $9744 ($14 as INC D). With BreakKeyTest before it, it reads as a removed '0' = BREAK exit to BASIC (a JR NZ to here).

$9745 c ObjectListRun ; Runs the object list at $BEF8 once (CourseRun), unless $B238 is set
> $B238 = 0 at MainLoop; the title's demo sets it to $80 ($819E): the demo runs without objects. Witness: game 1576 runs, a1 291, g1 493, p2 664, h1 493, i1 3601, i2 3934, h2 40.

$9751 c CourseRun ; The interpreter of the course script and the object list: runs the routine at the word (HL), HL past it
> IX = the word, HL += 2, CourseRun pushed as the return address, JP (IX). Each routine leaves HL past its data and returns into CourseRun, which runs the next word; the end word $975E (ListStop) pops that return and RETs to CourseRun's caller. Called by MainLoop (the script: DE = the object list write pointer, HL = the course pointer) and ObjectListRun (HL = $BEF8).
> Witness: game 8038 runs, a1 967, g1 2799, p2 3352, h1 2799, i1 89827, i2 123263, h2 200.

$975E c ListStop ; The end of a list: drops CourseRun's return address and returns to its caller
> The word $975E ends the object list (SegmentEnd writes it). Witness: game 1598 runs, i1 3631, i2 3968 (the ends of the object-list passes, and SegmentEnd's JP).

$9760 c SegmentEnd ; Script command: the end of a segment; writes the list end $975E at the write pointer (DE) and stops
> DE is not advanced: the next segment's objects go over the end word. Witness: game 22, a1 3, g1 6, p2 8, h1 6, i1 31, i2 34 (every segment read).

$976C c SegmentLength ; Script command (word): the segment's time in main-loop passes into $B23C
> Every segment starts with it: $7F (127) passes, 63 or 64 in the short point segments before a sector end. Witness: as SegmentEnd: i1 31, i2 34.

$9775 c CmdPoint ; Script command (delay, point): appends the point object ObjPoint and prints the coming point's letter at the bottom right
> Record: $9978, the delay, the point byte (bits 0-4 the point number, bit 5 the Champion course). Prints TextPointAt $97A7 (x $F9 y $B1, font 2) and the letter (the code of HudPrintPointLetter inlined), then inverts the cell at $50DF (row 22, column 31): the letter shows in reverse until ObjPoint passes it. The delay is $3F (63), or $3E in the segment before a sector end.
> Witness: game 2 runs (8345-16173), g1 2, h1 2, i1 25 (A 1123 ... Z 28136: every Beginner point), i2 28 (the 25 Champion points and A-C of Beginner after the loop).
$979E CmdPointInvert

$97A7 t TextPointAt ; Print string: AT x $F9 y $B1, font 2 (CmdPoint prints the point letter after it)
@res text/TextPointAt.bin

$97AD c CmdLamps ; Script command (delay, value pairs to $FF): appends warning-lamp objects ObjLamps
> Record: $99A3, the delay, the lamp value (0-3, HudLamps). Each point segment sets the lamps for what follows: 1 (CAUTION) with the UFO waves, 2 (DANGER!) with the CmdUfos11 waves and the boulders of Champion C and M, 3 with the low flyers from behind (Champion A, the segment after sector end P, Z) and a few mixed points (Beginner Y, Champion S, Y), 0 (all clear) in most points before a sector end (Beginner E, I, P, U; Champion E, J, P). Witness: game 2, g1 2, h1 2, i1 21, i2 27.
$97AE CmdLampsLoop
$97C3 CmdLampsDone

$97C5 c CmdRocks ; Script command (a delay a rock to $FF): appends rock objects ObjRock
> Record (8 bytes): $99C3, the delay, sprite $0F, y $94, x $F4 (off the right edge: not drawn yet), height $0C, width $0C. Witness: game 20 runs (1559-16323), a1 3, g1 4, p2 8, h1 4, i1 13 (first 263: the start segment, two rocks), i2 16.
$97C6 CmdRocksLoop
$97E6 CmdRocksDone

$97E8 c CmdCraters ; Script command (a delay a crater to $FF): appends crater objects ObjCrater
> Record (4 bytes): $9A92, the delay, x 0 (the right edge, before the first move). Witness: game 20 runs, a1 3, g1 4, p2 8, h1 4, i1 16 (first 263), i2 9.
$97E9 CmdCratersLoop
$97FD CmdCratersDone

$97FF c CmdMines ; Script command (a byte a mine to $FF: bits 0-6 the delay, bit 7 the blink phase): appends mine objects ObjMine
> Record (6 bytes): $9AEB, the delay, the phase ($00 or $80; the sprite goes in its low bits), y $A0, x $F8 (not drawn yet). Witness: i1 3 runs (11800-13545: the segment after sector end J, K, L), i2 5 (12337-28217); no other recording reaches the mines.
$9800 CmdMinesLoop
$981F CmdMinesDone

$9821 c CmdUfos1 ; Script command (a delay a UFO to $FF): UFO objects of type 1 ($9B6B), any height
> A' = 1 the type, $B236 = 0; on at CmdUfos $9832. Witness: game 2, g1 2, h1 2, i1 10, i2 10.
$9827 c CmdUfos5 ; Script command: UFO objects of type 5 ($9B6B), any height
> Witness: game 2, g1 2, h1 2, i1 10, i2 10.
$982D c CmdUfos11 ; Script command: UFO objects of type $0B ($9B6B), $B236 = $FF: start no lower than y $30, bit 3 in field +9
> Witness: i1 6 (3828-27176: points D, H, M, T, X, Y), i2 7. The lamp set with these waves is 2 (DANGER!) at D, H, M, T, X; Y sets 3.
$9832 c CmdUfos ; The UFO spawner: a 20-byte record per data byte, $9B6B with the type, a random start side and height
> Record: +0 $9B6B, +2 the delay, +3 the type, +4 $FF, +5 0, +6 dx (+2 from the left, -2 from the right), +7 y = (R XOR FRAMES) AND $1F + $18 (capped to $30 for CmdUfos11), +8 x = 0 or $F0 (bit 6 of R XOR FRAMES), +9 8 or $10 ($B236 AND 8 + 8), +10 $0F, +11 R AND $1F + $10, +12 $1D, +13 $FF, +14 4, +15 4, +18 5, +19 5 (+16, +17 not written). What the handler $9B6B makes of the fields from +9 on is annotator 03's.
> Witness: game 4 runs, g1 4, h1 4, i1 26, i2 27; the cap to y $30 ($9868) i1 2, i2 6.
$9837 CmdUfosLoop
$986A CmdUfosSide ; the start side from bit 6 of R XOR FRAMES
$9878 CmdUfosFields
$98A8 CmdUfosDone

$98AA c CmdUfosLeave ; Script command (a delay to $FF): appends $9EA5, which sets $B237 when its delay is out: the UFOs fly off
> Record (3 bytes): $9EA5, the delay. $9EA5 (annotator 03) sets $B237 = $FF; the UFO handler $9B6B then moves each UFO 4 pixels a pass to the nearer edge and removes it off screen ($9BE6), and a new wave clears $B237 ($9B99). In the script it comes after each UFO part of the course (points C, E, H, I, P, U, Y, Z of Beginner). Witness: i1 8 runs (2935-28136), i2 11.
$98AB CmdUfosLeaveLoop
$98BC CmdUfosLeaveDone

$98BE c CmdTanks ; Script command (a delay to $FF): appends tank objects $9EBD (15 bytes)
> Record: $9EBD, the delay, $13, $8F, $E7, $11, $14, $08, $00, $93, $C1, $03, $07: by the shape the tank's sprite $13, y $8F, x $E7, box $11 x $14, then a second sprite / box (y $93, x $C1, 3 x 7: its shell); the handler is annotator 03's (scores 250 / 200). Witness: i1 4 runs (23662-27176: the segment after sector end U, points V, W, Y), i2 9.
$98BF CmdTanksLoop
$98F1 CmdTanksDone

$98F3 c CmdFlyers ; Script command (a delay to $FF): appends low flyer objects $A03B (Champion only)
> Record (9 bytes): $A03B, the delay, $14, (+4 not written), y $91, x 0 (from the left: behind the buggy), $08, $0C. Witness: i2 3 runs (1352-29178: Champion A, the sector after P, Z).
$98F4 CmdFlyersLoop
$9915 CmdFlyersDone

$9917 c CmdBoulders40 ; Script command: small boulders (sprite $40, 8x8) for $A196
> Witness: i2 3 runs (3117-28218).
$991C c CmdBoulders44 ; Script command: medium boulders (sprite $44, 12x12)
> Witness: i2 5 runs.
$9921 c CmdBoulders4C ; Script command: big boulders (sprite $4C, 16x16)
> Witness: i2 5 runs.
$9924 c CmdBoulders ; The boulder spawner: an 8-byte record per data byte, $A196 with the sprite, y, x and the size
> Size s = sprite - $38 (- 4 more for $4C): 8, 12, 16. Record: $A196, the delay, the sprite, y = $A0 - s, x = -s (enters from the right edge), height s, width s. Scores in $A196 (annotator 03): 100 / 150 / 200 shot. Witness: i2 13 runs (3117-28218); Champion only.
$9926 CmdBouldersLoop
$9940 CmdBouldersSize
$9953 CmdBouldersDone

$9955 c CmdPlants ; Script command (a delay to $FF): appends green plant objects $A2C6 (Champion only)
> Record (8 bytes): $A2C6, the delay, 0, y $90, x $E4, 0, 0. Witness: i2 8 runs (4011-28218).
$9956 CmdPlantsLoop
$9976 CmdPlantsDone

$9978 c ObjPoint ; Object: a point passed when the delay is out: $B27C, the restart point, the HUD
> HL = the delay. When out: $B27C = the point byte, the restart pointer $B27F = $B281 (the start of the segment that holds the point: a death restarts there), HudPrintPoint $9247, HudProgress $92AA, HudPrintScore $92DA; then its word becomes ObjPointDone. Witness: game 11 runs, g1 51, h1 51, i1 1595; points passed ($9980) i1 25, i2 27.
$9980 ObjPointPassed

$99A0 c ObjPointDone ; Object skip: past a 4-byte record (the delay and one byte); the end of ObjPoint
> Witness: i1 7930 runs (1595 entries from ObjPoint's word), i2 8495.

$99A3 c ObjLamps ; Object: sets the warning lamps when the delay is out ($B27E = value AND 3, HudLamps)
> The unmatched handler of stage 1: no enemy, the HUD's three lamps (HudLamps $927E) set at the course's own times. Then its word becomes ObjLampsDone. Witness: game 11 runs, g1 51, h1 51, i1 885, i2 841; set ($99AB) i1 24, i2 29.
$99AB ObjLampsSet

$99C0 c ObjLampsDone ; Object skip: past a 4-byte record; the end of ObjLamps
> Witness: i1 7278 runs, i2 10082.

$99C3 c ObjRock ; Object: a rock on the ground, moving with the scroll; kills the buggy on contact, 100 when shot or hit, 80 jumped
> When the delay is out, each pass: erases the sprite at its old place (not on the first pass, x $F4), moves x left by the scroll step $B235 while the buggy is alive (x 0: removed, ObjRockRemove), then tests its box (y $94, x, 12 x 12) against the buggy's ($B212, $A7FE): contact calls the kill $A7DD and the rock explodes (ObjRockHit). Else against the forward shot at $5B60: a hit removes the shot ($5B62 = $FF) and the rock explodes. ObjRockHit: the sprite $2A, the word ObjRockExplode, 100 points (ScoreAdd), $B26F = 0. Else when the buggy's x AND $FC equals the rock's x: 80 points (jumped). Then drawn at the new place (sprite $0F through $AF74 / $A826).
> The rock of the SCORE ADVANCE TABLE (100 / 80) and of NOTES' candidate 97C5: the script's rocks start at i1 263 (two in the start segment), seen at i1 1200. Witness: game 3060 runs, a1 374, g1 871, p2 1296, h1 871, i1 3080, i2 4289, h2 72. Contact ($99F9): game 1, g1 4, i1 17 (immune: the kill is a RET, the rock still explodes); shot ($9A0B): game 1, i2 1; jumped ($9A37): game 1, g1 2, i1 10, i2 11.
$99CC ObjRockActive ; the old place erased, unless still at x $F4
$99DE ObjRockMove ; x - the scroll step while the buggy is alive; 0: gone
$99EE ObjRockTest ; the box against the buggy's
$99FE ObjRockShotTest ; the box against the forward shot
$9A10 ObjRockHit ; explodes: sprite $2A, the explosion handler, 100 points
@isub $9A10 LD DE,-4
$9A2D ObjRockMiss ; jumped over: 80 points
$9A42 ObjRockDraw
$9A50 ObjRockHitDone

$9A51 c ObjRockExplode ; Object: a rock's explosion: sprites $2B-$2E moving with the ground, then removed
> Erases the previous frame (none after $2A), moves with the scroll while the buggy is alive, the next sprite; at $2F the record is removed (ObjRockRemove). Entered from ObjRockHit in the same pass and then by its word. Witness: game 10 runs, a1 5, g1 20, p2 20, h1 20, i1 85, i2 140, h2 5 (about 4-5 passes an explosion).
$9A61 ObjRockExplodeMove
$9A6E ObjRockExplodeNext

$9A80 c ObjRockNext ; The end of a rock pass: HL back to the record
> Reached by a jump from ObjRockMiss and by falling in from ObjRockExplode; an entry of the cut (the L1 builds record it as a routine entry). Witness: game 534, a1 62, g1 273, p2 244, h1 273, i1 1171, i2 1638, h2 35.

$9A81 c ObjRockDone ; Object skip: past an 8-byte record (6 bytes after the delay); a rock's handler at its end
> Witness: i1 12370 runs, i2 13379.

$9A86 c ObjRockRemove ; A rock or its explosion removed: its word becomes ObjRockDone
> Witness: game 3 runs, a1 1, g1 6, p2 4, h1 6, i1 25, i2 37.

$9A92 c ObjCrater ; Object: a crater fed into the scrolling ground at the right edge; 100 when jumped
> No sprite: while the crater's x is above $EC (its first passes after the right edge) the ground pattern pointer $B232 points at the crater's columns ($A9CF or $A9EF, by bit 3 of x), at x $EC back to the plain ground $A9AF; the ground scroller $A932 copies from it. Then, as the crater moves left with the scroll ($B235, while the buggy is alive), the buggy's x AND $FC equal to its x scores 100 (jumped); at x 0 it is removed (its word ObjCraterDone). No contact test here: the buggy's fall into the crater is found elsewhere (the ground).
> The crater of the SCORE ADVANCE TABLE (100 jumped) and of NOTES' candidate 97E8: first i1 263, seen at i1 450. Witness: game 3039 runs, a1 371, g1 812, p2 1288, h1 812, i1 5432, i2 2857, h2 66; the crater columns ($9AB9) i1 148; jumped ($9AD2) game 3, a1 1, g1 6, p2 4, h1 6, i1 43, i2 28.
$9A9A ObjCraterActive
$9AB9 ObjCraterColumns ; the crater's columns: $A9CF or $A9EF by bit 3 of x
$9ACA ObjCraterJump ; the buggy over it: 100 points
$9ADB ObjCraterNext

$9ADC c ObjCraterDone ; Object skip: past a 4-byte record; the end of ObjCrater
> Witness: i1 18646 runs, i2 10593.
$9ADF ObjCraterRemove ; gone at x 0: the word becomes ObjCraterDone

$9AEB c ObjMine ; Object: a blinking mine on the ground; 50 when jumped
> On the first pass (x $F8) it clears 10 pixels of the line y $A0 at x $F6-$FF ($A7F4, $FD99) and sets $B288 bit 3, which the ground routine reads once and clears ($A994); later passes erase the sprite. Moves left with the scroll while the buggy is alive (x 0: removed, ObjMineRemove); the buggy's x AND $FC equal to its x scores 50 (jumped). Then the sprite $11 / $12 by bit 2 of the pass counter $B23B XOR the record's phase bit, drawn at y $A0. No contact test here.
> The mines of the SCORE ADVANCE TABLE (50) and of NOTES: only in the segments after sector end J and at K, L (Beginner: 18 mines), and in Champion after sector end J and at K, L, V, Y. Witness: i1 2298 runs (11801-14790), i2 4455 (12338-28908); first passes ($9B09) i1 18, i2 35; jumped ($9B2F) i1 17, i2 34.
$9AF3 ObjMineActive
$9B09 ObjMineEnter ; the first pass: 10 ground pixels cleared, $B288 bit 3
@isub $9B0A LD HL,$F6A0
$9B18 ObjMineMove
$9B3A ObjMineDraw ; the sprite $11 or $12: the blink

$9B5A c ObjMineDone ; Object skip: past a 6-byte record; the end of ObjMine
> Witness: i1 10169 runs, i2 19731.
