@isub $9D09 LD BC,-12
@isub $9F0A LD DE,-4
@isub $A0DB LD DE,-5
@isub $A1E4 LD DE,-4
@isub $A300 LD HL,$E4A0

@module objects ; The flying objects: the UFO saucers, the spinner, their bombs and the UFO retreat timer
@about The course interpreter $9751 calls the handler word at the head of each object record of the object list $BEF8 with HL = the record + 2; the handler moves and draws its object and returns HL at the next record. An object changes state by writing a new handler word into its record: alive, exploding, gone (a handler that only steps HL over the record).
@about One UFO record (20 bytes, made by the course commands $9821 / $9827 / $982D) holds a saucer or the spinner and its bomb: UfoHandler flies the UFO, UfoExplodeHandler plays its explosion, UfoBombHandler drops the bomb and carries on alone once the UFO is gone. The spinner's bomb makes a crater where it lands. UfoRetreatTimer (course command $98AA) sends every UFO off the screen when its wait runs out. Shooting scores: saucer 100, spinner 200, saucer bomb 5, spinner bomb 10, as the game's SCORE ADVANCE TABLE says.
$9B6B c UfoHandler ; The UFO handler: waits, then flies a saucer or the spinner, turns at random, leaves on the retreat flag, explodes when shot
> HL = record +2 (the fields are in the notes: +2 wait, +3 sprite, +4 turn timer, +5 dy, +6 dx, +7 y, +8 x, +9 height, +10 width, +11 to +19 the bomb). While the wait +2 counts down it only steps HL to the next record (UfoRecordSkip). Then each pass: $B236 = $FF for the spinner (height +9 = 16) or 0 for a saucer; the first pass (turn timer $FF) gives a turn timer of 16-40 and clears the retreat flag $B237 and $B26D; later passes erase the old sprite at (y, x) (UfoSetColour, then the sprite drawer $A826, which draws by XOR).
> The move: when the turn timer runs out (reloaded with 16-72 from R) a UFO moving across starts moving up or down by 2, one moving up or down starts moving across by 2, the sign from bit 6 of R. x must stay below $F1 (else it turns to an up / down move), y in $18-$37 for a saucer, $18-$2F for the spinner (else it turns across). With the retreat flag $B237 set (UfoRetreatTimer) it moves 4 a pass towards the nearer side, and past it (x >= $F1) the record goes on as the bomb alone (UfoSaucerGone).
> Then the four upward shots ShotsUp $5B65 are tested against its box (y, x, height, width) with $A7FE. A hit marks the shot spent (its byte +2 = $FF), stops the UFO, sets the first explosion sprite ($26 saucer, $2E spinner), puts UfoExplodeHandler in the record and adds 100 (saucer) or 200 (spinner) to the score ($92D3, BC = $0100 + $0100 for the spinner), and clears $B267. Else the sprite steps on through its animation (saucer A 1-4, saucer B 5-10, spinner 11-14) and is drawn at the new place. Either way the bomb part (UfoBombHandler) runs after it in the same pass.
> Witness: game 44 runs, g1 / h1 204 (only the wait: no UFO reached the screen in those runs), i1 13 759 (8172 active, frames 1125-30 439), i2 11 528 (445-33 813). UFOs shot: i1 37, i2 28 ($9C42). Turns: across to up / down i1 223, up / down to across i1 206. Retreat steps i1 462, i2 491.
$9B74 UfoActive ; the wait is over
$9B84 UfoKindSet ; $B236 = $FF: the spinner (height 16), 0: a saucer (height 8)
$9B8E ; the first pass: the UFO enters the screen
$9B96 ; $B26D and the retreat flag $B237 cleared: a new UFO cancels the retreat
$9BA2 UfoErase ; erase the sprite at its old place (XOR draw)
$9BB2 UfoMove ; D = x, E = y, B = dx, C = dy, HL = the turn timer
$9BCA UfoTurnVertical ; move up or down by 2, at random
$9BD6 UfoSetDy
$9BD9 UfoTurnAcross ; move across by 2, at random
$9BE5 UfoSetDx
$9BE6 UfoStep ; the retreat flag set: 4 a pass to the nearer side
$9BF5 UfoLeaveRight
$9BF7 UfoLeaveCheck ; past the side: the UFO is gone, its bomb goes on
$9C03 UfoStepX ; x + dx, turn at the sides
$9C0A UfoStepY ; y + dy, kept in $18 .. $38 - (8 for the spinner)
$9C2A ; the four upward shots
$9C2F UfoShotLoop
$9C41 UfoShot ; hit: the shot spent, the UFO stopped, the explosion set
$9C5C ; the record's handler: UfoExplodeHandler
$9C62 ; 100 for a saucer, 200 for the spinner
$9C7A UfoAnimate ; no hit: the next animation frame, drawn at the new place
$9C97 UfoWrapSaucerB ; saucer B: frames 5-10
$9C9B UfoWrapSaucerA ; saucer A: frames 1-4
$9C9D UfoDraw
$9CA4 UfoShotDone

$9CA5 c UfoExplodeHandler ; The UFO's explosion: five frames in place, then the record goes on as its bomb alone
> HL = record +2. Erases the last frame (not on the first pass, sprite $26 or $2E), steps the sprite +3 and draws it at (y, x): saucer $26-$2A, spinner $2E-$32. At $2B / $33 the record becomes UfoBombHandler (UfoSaucerGone). Falls into UfoBombHandler: a bomb already falling falls on.
> Put in the record by UfoHandler at a hit. Witness: i1 185 passes (frames 2236-30 297), i2 140 (994-33 311).
$9CC3 UfoExplodeNext ; the next frame: past the last one the UFO is gone
$9CDF UfoBombPart ; the bomb part runs after the UFO

$9CE0 c UfoBombHandler ; The UFO's bomb: dropped under the UFO, falls, explodes into a crater for the spinner, scores 5 / 10 when shot
> HL = record +2. The bomb's own wait +11 (16-47 from R) counts down first. Not dropped yet (+13 = $FF): when the UFO is gone too (the record's handler is UfoBombHandler) the record is spent (UfoRecordSkip from now on); else the bomb starts under the UFO, y + 8 (saucer) or y + 16 (spinner), x + 16, with sprite $1D and a timer of 8 (no drop when the UFO's x >= $E7). A bomb in flight is erased first.
> It moves by dx 4, dy 4 for 8 passes, then falls straight down by 4 with sprite $1E (also at x >= $F7). At the ground (y >= $A0): a saucer's bomb ends; the spinner's bomb tests five places 4 apart from x - 5 on the screen rows 159 and 162 ($5760, $5280, the pixel masks from the table $7D00) and, when they are all clear, appends a crater record ($9A92, wait 0, x AND $FC) at the end of the object list ($B261) before the end marker $975E, clears 16 pixels of the ground's top row $A0 ($A7F4) and draws the crater sprite $10.
> In flight it is tested against the four upward shots ShotsUp $5B65, then the forward shot ShotForward $5B60: a hit spends the shot and adds 5 (saucer bomb) or 10 (spinner bomb), BC = 5 + ($B236 AND 5). Then against the buggy (the box at $B212): a touch calls the kill $A7DD. Else it is drawn at (y, x + the band's scroll step, $AF74).
> When the bomb ends: if the UFO is gone the record is spent, else the bomb is reloaded (wait 16-47, sprite $1D, not dropped) for the next drop.
> Witness: i1 9546 passes (8319 as the record's handler, frames 1560-30 439), i2 8334 (771-33 813). Drops i1 143, i2 131; craters made i1 15, i2 16 ($9DAD); bombs shot i1 12, i2 18; bombs on the buggy i1 16, i2 17 ($9E28, the kill a RET in the immune clone).
$9CED UfoBombActive ; the bomb's wait is over
$9CFA UfoBombKindSet ; $B236 = $FF for the spinner's bomb
$9D05 ; not dropped yet: drop it now
$9D09 ; the record's handler word: is the UFO gone?
$9D1B ; UFO and bomb both gone: the record is spent
$9D21 UfoBombDrop ; under the UFO: y + 8 / + 16, x + 16
$9D45 UfoBombErase ; a bomb in flight: erase it
$9D53 UfoBombMove ; D = x, E = y, B = dx, C = dy, HL = the timer
$9D60 UfoBombFall ; straight down by 4, sprite $1E
$9D69 UfoBombStep
$9D72 ; the ground: a saucer's bomb ends, the spinner's makes a crater
$9D86 UfoCraterTest ; five places 4 apart must be clear on rows 162 and 159
$9D9B UfoCraterRefused
$9D9F UfoCraterTestLow
$9DAD ; a crater record ($9A92) at the end of the list, then the end marker $975E
$9DCB ; 16 pixels of the ground's top row cleared, the crater sprite $10 drawn
$9DD9 UfoBombStore ; store dy, dx, y, x; then the hit tests
$9DE4 ; the four upward shots
$9DE9 UfoBombShotLoop
$9DFB UfoBombShot ; shot: 5 for a saucer's bomb, 10 for the spinner's
$9E11 UfoBombHitTests ; the forward shot, then the buggy
$9E28 ; on the buggy: the kill
$9E2D UfoBombDraw
$9E3F UfoBombNext
$9E44 UfoBombEnd ; the bomb ended: is the UFO gone too?
$9E56 UfoRecordSpent ; the record's handler: UfoRecordSkip
$9E5F UfoRecordSkip ; the next record: 18 bytes on
$9E64 UfoBombReload ; the UFO lives: a new bomb, wait 16-47

$9E81 c UfoSaucerGone ; The UFO has left or exploded: the record's handler becomes UfoBombHandler, which runs at once
> Entered by a JP with the record +2 on the stack, from UfoHandler (the retreat past the side) and UfoExplodeHandler (the last frame). Witness: i1 72, i2 63 (frames 2266-30 297 in i1).

$9E8E c UfoSetColour ; Picks the drawing colour of a UFO sprite into E' for the sprite drawer
> A = the sprite: below 5 (saucer A) $44 bright green, below 11 (saucer B) $06 yellow, below 15 (the spinner) $05 cyan, else $07 white (the bombs $1D / $1E and the explosions). Keeps the main registers. Called before each draw and erase of UfoHandler and UfoExplodeHandler: i1 16 491, i2 14 805; white i1 296.
$9EA3 UfoColourSet

$9EA5 c UfoRetreatTimer ; The UFO retreat timer: after its wait sets the retreat flag $B237, and the UFOs fly off the screen
> The record (3 bytes, course command $98AA): the handler and a wait. When the wait runs out it sets $B237 = $FF (UfoHandler then moves every UFO 4 a pass off the nearer side) and the record becomes UfoRetreatSkip. The flag is cleared by the life start ($90F4) and by the next UFO to enter the screen.
> This is the unmatched handler of stage 1. Witness: the flag set i1 8 times, i2 10 ($9EAD): one per $98AA record, at the course points C E H I P U Y Z of Beginner and A E G H J P T U Y Z of Champion (course-commands.txt); handler runs i1 167 (frames 2937-28 403), i2 198.
$9EAD UfoRetreatStart ; the wait is over: the UFOs retreat
$9EBB UfoRetreatSkip ; the next record: 1 byte on

@module enemies ; The ground enemies: the tank and its shell, the low flyer from behind, the boulders and the green plant
@about The object handlers of the tank (course command $98BE), the low flyer that comes from behind ($98F3), the boulders of three sizes ($9917 / $991C / $9921) and the green plant ($9955), with the same record protocol as the UFOs (module objects): the handler gets HL = record +2 and returns HL at the next record, and changes its record's handler word to explode or end.
@about Each one scrolls left with the ground ($B235, while the buggy lives), is tested against the buggy (the kill $A7DD) and the forward shot $5B60 (only the UFOs and their bombs meet the upward shots), and scores when shot or when the buggy passes over it (its x AND $FC equal to the object's x). The scores are the SCORE ADVANCE TABLE's, except the green plant's jump: the code gives 200, the table says 250.
@about The immune clone of i1 / i2 makes the kill a RET, so there the object that touches the buggy explodes and scores as if shot: most of the explosions of the low flyer, the shell and the plant in i2 are touches, not shots (the notes count them).
$9EBD c TankHandler ; The tank: rolls in from the right with the ground, fires its shell, 250 when shot, 200 when jumped
> Record (14 bytes): +2 wait, +3 sprite $13, +4 y $8F, +5 x $E7, +6 height $11, +7 width $14, +8 to +13 the shell (TankShellHandler). After the wait, each pass: erases the sprite (not on the first pass, x = $E7), x - the scroll step $B235 while the buggy lives (off the left side: TankGone), then the tests: the buggy (the kill $A7DD) or the forward shot ShotForward $5B60 (spent) both explode the tank: sprite $32, TankExplodeHandler in the record, 250 points ($92D3, BC = $0250), $B270 cleared. Else 200 when the buggy's x AND $FC, less 1, equals the tank's x, and the sprite is drawn at x + the band's scroll step ($AF74). Then the shell part runs (TankShellHandler).
> Witness: i1 912 runs (frames 23 663-28 206, Beginner V W Y), i2 2503 (2241-28 706, Champion B E I M N S T W Y). Shot i1 9, i2 19 ($9F07); touched i2 6 ($9EF3). The jump score $9F32 was not executed in any recording (game, a1, g1, t1, p2, h1, i1, i2, h2): known only from the code.
$9EC6 TankActive
$9ED8 TankScroll ; x - the scroll step while the buggy lives; off the left side: gone
$9EE8 TankHitTests ; the buggy
$9EF8 TankShotTest ; the forward shot
$9F0A TankHit ; explosion sprite $32, TankExplodeHandler, 250 points
$9F27 TankJumpTest ; the buggy over the tank: 200
$9F3D TankDraw
$9F4B TankHitDone

$9F4C c TankExplodeHandler ; The tank's explosion: five frames drawn 3 rows up, then the record goes on as the shell alone
> A shell still waiting to be fired (wait +8 not 0) is cancelled ($FF). Erases the last frame (not the first, $32), scrolls x with the ground while the buggy lives, steps the sprite +3 and draws it at (y - 3, x + the band's step): $32-$36. At $37: TankGone. Falls into TankShellHandler. Witness: i1 45 passes (frames 24 668-28 241), i2 125.
$9F58 TankExplodeErase
$9F6B TankExplodeScroll
$9F78 TankExplodeDraw
$9F8E TankShellPart ; the shell part runs after the tank

$9F8F c TankShellHandler ; The tank's shell: fired 8 passes after the tank appears, flies left, 20 when shot, 15 when jumped
> Shell fields: +8 wait (8 from the course command; 0 in flight, $FF done), +9 sprite (0 not yet fired, then $3B), +10 y $93, +11 x $C1, +12 height 3, +13 width 7. HL = record +2 on entry, back at the next record (+12) on return. In flight it is erased, moves left by 2 + the scroll step ($B235 masked by $B216 rotated left 3: the whole step while the buggy lives), and past the left side ends (+8 = $FF).
> Tests: the buggy (the kill) or the forward shot (spent): 20 points ($0020), the shell ends. Else 15 ($0015) when the buggy's x AND $FC, less 1, equals its x, and it is drawn at x + the band's step.
> Witness: i1 2961 runs (as the record's handler from frame 24 476), i2 5612. Fired i1 7, i2 24 ($9FB1); on the buggy i1 6, i2 19 ($9FD3; every 20 of the runs came from a touch: the shot path $9FE7 was not executed in any recording); jumped i1 1, i2 4 ($A000).
$9FA0 TankShellActive
$9FB1 TankShellFire ; first pass: sprite $3B
$9FB5 TankShellMove ; x - (2 + the scroll step)
$9FD8 TankShellShotTest
$9FEA TankShellHit ; 20 points, the shell ends
$9FF5 TankShellJumpTest ; the buggy over the shell: 15
$A00B TankShellDraw
$A018 TankShellNext ; the next record: 12 bytes on
$A01D TankShellEnd ; the shell is done: +8 = $FF

$A022 c TankGone ; The tank has left or exploded: the record's handler becomes TankShellHandler, which runs at once
> Entered by a JP with the record +2 on the stack, from TankHandler (off the left side) and TankExplodeHandler (the last frame). The wait +2 is always 0 here, so the other choice, TankRecordSkip ($A02A), was not executed in any recording: known only from the code. Witness: i1 9, i2 25.
$A02D TankGoneSet

$A036 c TankRecordSkip ; A tank record still waiting: steps HL 12 bytes to the next record
> Reached from TankHandler while the wait +2 counts down. Witness: i1 781, i2 1932.

$A03B c LowFlyerHandler ; The low flyer from behind: creeps in from the left, pauses, crosses the screen, 300 / 500 / 800 when shot, 125 when jumped
> Record (9 bytes): +2 wait, +3 sprite $14, +4 pause count, +5 y $91, +6 x 0, +7 height 8, +8 width $0C. Drawn in the ink of the current colour set (byte +3 of the set at $B230, in E'). Each pass after the wait: erases the sprite (not while x = 0), then the pace: x + 1 up to $10; at $0F a pause of 16-23 passes (FRAMES XOR R) and it waits at $10; then x + 4 a pass; at $88 a pause of 4-11 and it waits at $8C; then x + 4 to $F0, where it is gone (LowFlyerGone). While the buggy is not alive ($B216 not $FF) x also moves by the scroll step.
> Tests: the buggy (the kill) or the forward shot (spent): sprite $36, LowFlyerExplodeHandler, a random score of 300, 500 or 800 (FRAMES XOR R AND 3, not 0, picks a bit of $C0 rotated: BC = $0300 / $0500 / $0800), $B26C cleared: the ??? of the SCORE ADVANCE TABLE. Else 125 ($0125) when the buggy's x AND $FC equals its x, and it is drawn at x + the band's step ($AF74).
> Champion only. Witness: i2 430 runs (frames 1353-29 538; the command $98F3 at Champion A and Z). Exploded i2 4, all on the buggy ($A0C4); the shot path $A0D8 was not executed in any recording; jumped i2 1 ($A116).
$A044 LowFlyerActive
$A05F LowFlyerPace ; x = $0F: a pause of 16-23
$A076 LowFlyerPaceTest ; below $10: x + 1; at $10: wait
$A07F LowFlyerPastEdge ; x = $88: a pause of 4-11
$A095 LowFlyerAtStop ; x = $8C: wait
$A099 LowFlyerWait ; count the pause down, then go on
$A0A0 LowFlyerAdvance ; x + 4; at $F0 gone
$A0A9 LowFlyerScroll ; only while the buggy is not alive
$A0B9 LowFlyerHitTests ; the buggy
$A0C9 LowFlyerShotTest ; the forward shot
$A0DB LowFlyerHit ; explosion sprite $36, LowFlyerExplodeHandler
$A0E9 LowFlyerScoreRandom ; 1-3 from FRAMES XOR R
$A0F7 LowFlyerScoreRotate ; $C0 rotated 1-3 times AND 7, + 2: 3, 5 or 8 hundred
$A10C LowFlyerJumpTest ; the buggy under it: 125
$A121 LowFlyerDraw
$A139 LowFlyerHitDone

$A13A c LowFlyerExplodeHandler ; The low flyer's explosion: frames $36-$3A in place, then the record is spent
> Erases the last frame (not the first, $36), steps the sprite and draws it at (y, x + the band's step) in the colour set's ink. At $3B: LowFlyerGone. Witness: i2 20 passes (frames 2100-29 565).
$A154 LowFlyerExplodeNext
$A170 LowFlyerDone
$A171 LowFlyerRecordSkip ; the next record: 7 bytes on
$A176 LowFlyerGone ; the record's handler: LowFlyerRecordSkip

$A182 b LifeStartDateText 5 ; A print string for $FFD0 that ends at its first byte: under it the build date 03:40 30/10/84, never printed
> The life start prints it at $9126 (LD HL,$A182, CALL $FFD0). $FFD0 stops at $1F, the first byte, so nothing is printed: only $A182 is read (game 20, a1 4, g1 4, p2 8, h1 4, i1 6, i2 6, once a life start); $A183-$A195 are never read in any recording. A leftover of the build, like the date 03:35 30/10/84 at $7F05 five minutes earlier.
$A182 ; the end code $1F: the string ends here
$A183 ; print codes of $FFD0 ($00, $29, $1A $02), never read
$A187 ; "03:40 30/10/84", the build date
$A195 ; the end code $1F

$A196 c BoulderHandler ; A boulder: one of three sizes rolls in with the ground, 100 / 150 / 200 when shot, 50 / 100 / 150 when jumped
> Record (8 bytes, commands $9917 / $991C / $9921): +2 wait, +3 sprite ($40 small, $44 medium, $4C large), +4 y = $A0 - size, +5 x = 256 - size, +6 height, +7 width = the size (8, 12, 16). Each pass after the wait: erases the sprite (not on the first pass, x + size = 0), x - the scroll step while the buggy lives (at x = 0: BoulderGone).
> Tests: the buggy (the kill) or the forward shot (spent): the explosion sprite ($7F small, $57 medium, $5F large), BoulderExplodeHandler, 100 / 150 / 200 points by the sprite (below $44, below $4C, else), $B26E cleared. Else 50 / 100 / 150 when the buggy's x AND $FC equals its x. Then, while the buggy lives, it rolls: the sprite steps through small $40-$43, medium $44-$4B, large $4C-$57; drawn at x + the band's step.
> Champion only. Witness: i2 3448 runs (frames 3118-29 354, Champion C G M W Y). Exploded i2 26 (small 4, medium 10, large 12; 22 of them touches $A1CD, 4 shots $A1E1); jumped i2 9 (small 3, medium 3, large 3).
$A19F BoulderActive
$A1B2 BoulderScroll ; x - the scroll step; at 0 gone
$A1C2 BoulderHitTests ; the buggy
$A1D2 BoulderShotTest ; the forward shot
$A1E4 BoulderHit ; by size: explosion sprite and 100 / 150 / 200
$A201 BoulderExplodeSet
$A219 BoulderJumpTest ; the buggy over it: 50 / 100 / 150
$A238 BoulderJumpScore
$A23F BoulderRoll ; the next frame of its size while the buggy lives
$A255 BoulderRollMedium
$A25B BoulderRollLarge
$A261 BoulderDraw
$A267 BoulderHitDone

$A268 c BoulderExplodeHandler ; A boulder's explosion: five frames of its size, then the record is spent
> Frames small $7F-$83, medium $57-$5B, large $5F-$63; the frames $60-$63 and $7C-$7F are drawn one row higher. Erases the last frame (not the first: sprite AND $D7 = $57), scrolls x while the buggy lives, steps the sprite and draws it; when (sprite - $5C) AND $D7 = 0 ($5C, $64, $84): BoulderGone. Witness: i2 130 passes (frames 3411-29 144).
$A287 BoulderExplodeScroll
$A294 BoulderExplodeDraw
$A2B4 BoulderDone
$A2B5 BoulderRecordSkip ; the next record: 6 bytes on
$A2BA BoulderGone ; the record's handler: BoulderRecordSkip

$A2C6 c PlantHandler ; The green plant: grows and shrinks in a 15-frame cycle as it scrolls in, 500 when shot, 200 when jumped
> Record (8 bytes, command $9955): +2 wait, +3 frame (0 before the first pass, then 1-15), +4 y $90, +5 x $E4, +6 / +7 the hit box's height and width (set each pass). The plant is two sprites: the stem $63 + frame at (y + 16, x), and in frames 5-11 the head $6F + frame at (y, x), with the head's attributes on rows 18-19 (PlantColourOn; PlantColourOff in the other frames).
> The first pass clears 28 pixels of the ground's top row $A0 from x $E4 ($A7F4) and sets $B288 bit 3 (tested and cleared at $A994), then draws frame 1. Later passes erase the sprites, x - the scroll step while the buggy lives (at 0: PlantGone), then the hit box from PlantHitBoxes by the frame: (y + dy, x + dx, dy, dx).
> Tests: the buggy (the kill) or the forward shot (spent): frame $7A, PlantExplodeHandler, 500 points ($0500), $B26A cleared. Else 200 ($0200) when the buggy's x AND $FC equals its x (the SCORE ADVANCE TABLE says 250). Then the next frame (15 wraps to 1) is drawn at x + the band's step.
> Champion only. Witness: i2 1497 runs (frames 4013-29 106, Champion D F K Q V X Y). Exploded i2 8, all touches ($A358; the shot path $A36F was not executed in any recording); jumped i2 4 ($A3A8).
$A2CF PlantActive
$A2E3 ; frames 5-11: the head $6F + frame, with the head's colours
$A2EF PlantHeadHidden ; other frames: plain colours
$A2F2 PlantStemErase ; the stem $63 + frame, 16 rows lower
$A2FF PlantFirst ; first pass: 28 pixels of the ground's top row cleared
$A311 PlantScroll ; x - the scroll step; at 0 gone
$A326 PlantHitBox ; PlantHitBoxes[frame - 1]: y + dy, x + dx, height dy, width dx
$A35E PlantShotTest ; the forward shot
$A372 PlantHit ; frame $7A, PlantExplodeHandler, 500 points
$A396 PlantJumpTest ; the buggy over it: 200
$A3B3 PlantGrow ; the next frame, 15 wraps to 1
$A3C0 PlantFrameSet
$A3DA PlantHeadless
$A3DD PlantDrawStem
$A3EC PlantHitDone

$A3ED c PlantExplodeHandler ; The green plant's explosion: frames $7A-$7E at (y + 4, x + 8) over the head's colours, then the record is spent
> The first pass (frame $7A) draws sprite $73 at (y + 16, x) instead of erasing; later passes erase the last frame. Scrolls x while the buggy lives, steps the frame and draws it with PlantColourOn. At $7F: PlantGone puts the plain colours back. Witness: i2 40 passes (frames 4348-28 413).
$A409 PlantExplodeFirst ; the first pass: sprite $73 at (y + 16, x)
$A41B PlantExplodeScroll
$A428 PlantExplodeDraw
$A443 PlantDone
$A444 PlantRecordSkip ; the next record: 6 bytes on
$A449 PlantGone ; plain colours back; the record's handler: PlantRecordSkip

$A458 c PlantColourOn ; Colours the plant's head: 3 or 4 cells of rows 18 and 19 at the plant's column
> HL = (L y, H x). PlantAttrAddress gives the cell and the count; each cell of rows 18 and 19 gets the paper of the colour set's byte +3 with the colour set's byte +4 paper as ink ($B230 the set), the next cell gets byte +3, and AttrRow20 $5A80 (row 20, column 0) gets byte +4. Keeps A (in A'), DE and HL. Witness: i2 546 (frames 4112-29 099).
$A474 PlantColourLoop ; rows 18 and 19, B cells

$A48D c PlantColourOff ; Puts the plain colour (the colour set's byte +3) back on the plant's cells of rows 18 and 19
> As PlantColourOn, with the one colour for every cell. Witness: i2 564 (frames 4083-29 106).

$A49F c PlantAttrAddress ; HL = the attribute cell of row 18 under x, B = 3 cells, or 4 when x is not on a cell boundary
> In: H = x. HL = AttrRow18 $5A40 + x / 8; B = 3, + 1 when bit 2 of x is set. Witness: i2 1110 (frames 4083-29 106).

$A4B2 b PlantHitBoxes 4 ; The green plant's hit box by frame 1-15: dy, dx, then two bytes the code loads and never uses
> PlantHandler reads row frame - 1 into E, D, C, B; only E (dy) and D (dx) are used, as the box's offset and as its height and width. Bytes 3-4 look like the intended height and width (dy + byte 3 = 16 in every row, byte 4 the width), but C and B are overwritten by $A7FE: known only from the code. Read i2 28-40 times a row.
$A4B2 ; frames 1-4: the stem only
$A4C2 ; frames 5-11: the head up and down
$A4DE ; frames 12-15: the stem only

@module bonus ; The sector bonus screen at each sector end, with the best sector time kept in the course script
@about The course interpreter jumps to SectorBonus $A4EE at each of the five sector-end records of a course script (the command word $A4EE; Beginner $B87C, ... Champion ...). The record after the command word: the time required for the bonus (BCD word, high byte first), the sector bonus (one BCD byte), the scenery flag for the next sector, and the best time so far (BCD word, $0999 in the image).
@about The screen: the time taken, the time required and the best time; the sector bonus added to the score; the time bonus (100 points a second under the time required) counted up with a blip a second, or SORRY NO TIME BONUS; a better time is written back into the script, so the best time lives in the course script for the rest of the session. Then the next sector starts. Witness: i1 and i2, the five sector ends of each course (i1 frames 5176-29659, i2 5357-30694).

$A4EE c SectorBonus ; The sector bonus screen: the times, the sector and time bonuses, the best time written back into the course script
> Entered by a jump from the course interpreter $9751 at a sector-end record, HL just past the command word. Reads the record: TimeRequired $B241 (BCD word, high byte first), SectorBonus $B245 (one BCD byte, $B246 = 0), the scenery flag $B27D ($FF / $00, alternate sectors: picks the hills graphics at the life start $90D3 through $95B0 / $9541), BestTime $B243 (BCD word, $0999 in the image), and leaves the course pointer $B27A just past the record.
> SetIm1 $A7CB (the ROM's interrupt: the game's clock $7F98 stops), clears the screen ($9044, colour 7), $9136, prints BonusHeaderText and the course letter ($9258 with $B27C), then the time taken $B285, TimeRequired and BestTime ($9369, 2 BCD bytes at x $C2, y $49 / $51 / $59). Prints SectorBonusText and the bonus at x $A0, y $69, and adds it to the score: ScoreAdd $92D3 with B = the bonus byte, C = 0 ($10 = 1000 points). Plays BonusTune $A773 (22 BEEPER notes), waits 50 frames.
> The time bonus: TimeRequired - time taken in BCD. Not positive: NoTimeBonus (SORRY NO TIME BONUS and a low note). Positive: TimeBonusText, a two-note fanfare (BonusBeep $A690), 50 frames, then one pass of TimeBonusLoop a second of the difference: the bonus counter $B245 + 100 shown at x $A0, y $71, 100 points to the score, a 15-half-cycle blip on the beeper with interrupts off (DI $A5E5, OUT $A60A, EI $A610), 4 frames. Only the low byte of the difference counts (DEC A / DAA to zero): a difference of 100 seconds or more would lose its hundreds (known only from the code: no time required in the scripts is over $0150).
> Then the 100-frame wait $903D and the best time: if the time taken is below BestTime, BestTimeWrite stores it (DE = $B285, low byte $B285 first, that is the same big-endian BCD order) over the record's last word, through the course pointer $B27A - 2: this is the writer of the script words $B884, $B925, $B9AE, $BA51, $BB03 (Beginner) and $BBC7, $BC95, $BD42, $BDF0, $BED0 (Champion). The AWESOME! BEST SECTOR TIME SO FAR!! text and the longer flash (50 passes, else 25) only when the old best is not the image's placeholder (high byte $09): the first finish of a sector records its time silently.
> Last: BonusFlash 25 or 50 passes of $8349 (the attribute cycle of the menu, here on $5A03-$5A1D) and the delay $820D; the time $B285 / $B286 and the tick counter $B287 to zero, the screen cleared, the life start $90D3, SP back from $B25F and a jump to the main loop $9696 with the course pointer at the next sector.
> Witness: i1 5 and i2 5 runs (l1sum $A4EE, i1 frames 5176-29659, i2 5357-30694, the five sector ends of NOTES); TimeBonusLoop i1 134 seconds, i2 172; the blip OUT $A60A i1 2010 = 134 x 15; BestTimeWrite $A659 i1 5, i2 5; the reads of $B884 (i1 1). NoTimeBonus $A620 and the AWESOME text $A651 were not executed in any recording (game, a1, g1, t1, p2, h1, i1, i2, h2): known only from the code (both runs finished every sector in time, and no sector was finished twice).
$A4F2 ; TimeRequired $B241: the record's first word, high BCD byte first
$A4FA ; SectorBonus $B245: the record's bonus byte, $B246 = 0
$A500 ; the scenery flag $B27D for the next sector
$A507 ; BestTime $B243: the record's last word
$A50B ; the course pointer $B27A: past the record (BestTimeWrite writes through it)
$A50E ; IM 1: the game's handler and its clock stop
$A528 ; the time taken $B285 at x $C2, y $49
$A534 ; TimeRequired at x $C2, y $51
$A540 ; BestTime at x $C2, y $59
$A552 ; the sector bonus at x $A0, y $69
$A563 ; the sector bonus to the score: B = the bonus byte, C = 0
$A566 ; the fanfare: 22 notes of BonusTune
$A56B BonusTuneLoop ; HL = the period word, DE = the length byte; the fourth byte is skipped
$A57F ; 50 frames
$A581 BonusWait1
$A584 ; TimeRequired - time taken, BCD, low bytes first: H = low byte, L = high byte
$A593 ; negative or zero: no time bonus
$A5A3 ; the two-note fanfare of the time bonus
$A5AD ; 50 frames
$A5AF BonusWait2
$A5B2 ; A = the seconds to count: only the low BCD byte of the difference
$A5B6 ; the bonus counter $B245 / $B246 = 0
$A5B9 TimeBonusLoop ; one second of the difference: the counter + 100 (BCD, high byte first)
$A5CA ; three characters cleared before the new count
$A5D3 ; the counter at x $A0, y $71
$A5DC ; 100 points to the score
$A5E5 ; the blip with interrupts off; A' = the border colour from BORDCR
$A5F0 ; the half-cycle lengths come from the ROM bytes at $0B16
$A5F5 BlipHalfCycle ; one half-cycle: (DE) x 10 DJNZ loops, then $77 short ones
$A5F7 BlipDelayOuter
$A5F9 BlipDelayInner
$A600 BlipDelayFixed
$A602 BlipDelayFixedInner
$A608 ; toggles the speaker and MIC bits, the border unchanged
$A610 ; interrupts back on, 4 frames
$A613 BonusWait3
$A616 ; the next second, in BCD
$A61B ; the 100-frame wait, then the best time
$A620 NoTimeBonus ; SORRY NO TIME BONUS and a low note (not executed in any recording)
$A632 BestTimeCheck ; BestTime - time taken, BCD: positive means a new best
$A641 ; 25 flash passes unless AWESOME
$A64A ; BestTime high byte $09: the image's placeholder $0999, no text
$A651 ; AWESOME! BEST SECTOR TIME SO FAR!! and 50 flash passes (not executed in any recording)
$A659 BestTimeWrite ; the writer of the script's best time: the time taken over the record's last word
$A664 BonusFlash ; one pass of the attribute cycle $8349 on $5A03-$5A1D and the delay $820D
$A677 ; the time and the tick counter to zero for the next sector
$A686 ; the life start, then the main loop with the course pointer at the next sector
@isub $A528 LD DE,$C249
@isub $A534 LD DE,$C251
@isub $A540 LD DE,$C259
@isub $A552 LD DE,$A069
@isub $A5D3 LD DE,$A071

$A690 c BonusBeep ; Two BEEPER calls with the same note, 7 frames apart (the bonus fanfares)
> HL = the period, DE = the length, as for BEEPER $03B5 (the ROM's). Called by SectorBonus with HL $01B7, DE $00A8 (the time bonus) and with HL $1BBA, DE $000B (no time bonus; not executed in any recording). Witness: i1 5, i2 5 entries (i1 frames 5423-28918), the 7-frame wait $A699 35 times each.
$A699 BonusBeepWait

$A6A0 t BonusHeaderText ; The bonus screen's three labels: TIME TAKEN TO REACH POINT, TIME REQUIRED FOR BONUS, BEST TIME SO FAR
> A string of the print routine $FFD0: $16 x y moves to the pixel position x, y ($16 $1A $49 here), $0D starts the next line, $1F ends; $18 and $1A take one byte each (their role is the print routine's). The numbers are printed after it by SectorBonus at x $C2. Read by SectorBonus $A519 (i1 5, i2 5 reads a byte).
@res text/BonusHeaderText.bin
$A706 t SectorBonusText ; SECTOR BONUS = at x $46, y $69
@res text/SectorBonusText.bin
$A719 t TimeBonusText ; TIME BONUS   = at x $46, y $71
@res text/TimeBonusText.bin
$A72C t NoTimeBonusText ; SORRY NO TIME BONUS at x $46, y $71 (never read in the recordings)
@res text/NoTimeBonusText.bin
$A743 t BestTimeText ; AWESOME! BEST SECTOR TIME SO FAR!! in its own colours ($10 ink 4, $11 paper 0, $13 bright 1) at x $1C, y $81 (never read in the recordings)
@res text/BestTimeText.bin
$A773 b BonusTune 4 ; The sector-end fanfare: 22 notes of 4 bytes: BEEPER period (word), length (byte), a spare byte
> Played by BonusTuneLoop $A56B: HL = the period, DE = the length byte; the fourth byte is never read (l1sum --reads $A773-$A7CA: three bytes of each four read 5 times in i1). Doc 2's "never touched $A72C-$A7CA" holds only for the two texts and these spare bytes.

@module play ; The play code: interrupt modes, the buggy, its jump, shots and death, the scrolling, the controls, the hold and the tune keys
@about The routines of the main loop's call list $96FA and their helpers: SetIm1 / SetIm2, BuggyKill (every death goes through it), the box test, the sprite dispatch, the four scrolling bands, the buggy (BuggyUpdate, BuggyErase, the jump arcs, the speed governor), the wheels flying off at a death, the shots, the six control readers, the hold, the tune toggle and the BREAK abort.
@about Variables: the buggy record $B212 (y, x, box height $0D, box width $1D), the buggy state $B216 ($FF alive, 0-8 the death frames), the wheel step $B217, the jump index $B223 ($FF not jumping) and arc $B221, the crater-death flag $B220, the flags $B288 (bit 0 T held, bit 1 fire held, bit 3 a narrow road texture, bit 4 H held, bit 7 tune off), the control type $B259 (1-6).

$A7CB c SetIm1 ; IM 1 with I = $3F: the ROM's frame interrupt (menus, death, hold, bonus)
> DI, IM 1, I = $3F, EI. Called from the menu code ($81CD, $823D, $8250, $8328), the death ($96DF), SectorBonus $A50E, the abort $AACD and the hold $AEE0. Witness: game 27, a1 28, g1 5, t1 1, p2 10, h1 5, i1 6, i2 7, h2 1 entries (a1 frames 153-21155).

$A7D4 c SetIm2 ; IM 2 with I = $3A: the vector word at $3AFF in the ROM sends interrupts to $FFFF, the game's handler $7F14
> DI, I = $3A, IM 2, EI. Called from the life start ($9132) and the end of the hold ($AF0E). Witness: game 20, a1 5, g1 4, p2 8, h1 4, i1 6, i2 6 entries (a1 frames 1424-20428).

$A7DD c BuggyKill ; Kills the buggy: the death starts (state 0), 25 points, unless already dying
> Does nothing unless the buggy state $B216 is $FF (alive). Else $B216 = 0 (the first death frame: BuggyUpdate counts it to 8, the main loop $96BB ends the life there) and the wheel step $B217 = 0 (WheelsFly starts), then ScoreAdd $92D3 with DE = 0, BC = $0025: 25 points for the death (known only from the code: no run checked the score at a death). Keeps HL.
> The immune clones (i1, i2) patch the first byte to RET. Callers, one a death cause each (l1sum of the CALL): the rock $99F9 in $99C3 (command $97C5; game 1, a1 1, g1 4, p2 4, h1 4, i1 17, i2 27, h2 1), the UFO bomb $9E28 in $9CE0 (dropped by the UFO handler $9B6B through $9E81; i1 16, i2 17), the tank $9EF3 in $9EBD (command $98BE; i2 6), the tank shell $9FD3 in $9F8F (installed by $A022; i1 6, i2 19), the low flyer $A0C4 in $A03B (command $98F3; i2 4), the boulders $A1CD in $A196 (commands $9917 / $991C / $9921; i2 22), the green plant $A358 in $A2C6 (command $9955; i2 8), and the crater $AA9C in BuggyUpdate (the ground probe; game 18, a1 1, p2 4, i1 69, i2 67). The rock, bomb, tank, shell, flyer, boulder and plant handlers test their box against the buggy's with BoxOverlap $A7FE first.
> The crater and mine handlers ($9A92, $9AEB) and the UFO saucers ($9B6B) never call it: a crater kills through the ground probe, and so does a mine: drawn at y $A0, its ink is on line 162, and the probe's 5-pixel steps cannot miss its 8-pixel width (known only from the code; no recording shows a mine death). Witness of the deaths that counted (not immune): game 19 = 1 rock + 18 craters, a1 2 = 1 + 1, g1 4 rocks, p2 8 = 4 + 4, h1 4 rocks, h2 1 rock (l1sum $A7DD entries: game frames 2029-16149).

$A7F4 c UnplotRun ; Clears E points left to right from (H = x, L = y) through UnplotPixel $FD99
> E = the count. Calls UnplotPixel $FD99 for x, x + 1, ...; keeps L. Callers: $8118 (the score advance page, $8133 / $813B / $8143), the mine $9AEB, the bomb $9CE0, the plant $A2C6. Witness: a1 60 entries (frames 1218-18835), t1 3, i1 33, i2 63.

$A7FE c BoxOverlap ; Do two boxes overlap? HL and HL' point at y, x, height, width; NC when they do
> B, C, D, E = the box at HL, B', C', D', E' = the box at HL' (the buggy $B212 or a shot $5B60 / $5B65 for the object handlers). Carry as soon as one box ends above or left of the other: y + height < y', y' + height' < y, then the same with x and width. NC: they overlap. Witness: game 1055, a1 117, g1 518, p2 460, h1 518, i1 54 624, i2 54 655, h2 63 entries (i1 frames 604-30439).

$A826 c DrawSprite ; Draws sprite A at H = x, L = y (XOR) through the sprite table $B2D6: drawer word, graphics word
> The table $B2D6 holds 4 bytes a sprite: the drawer (one of the sprites module's DrawSprite16x4 ... DrawSprite40x23) and its graphics. Sprite 0 is the buggy ($E140, DrawSprite40x23), $1F-$26 its death frames, $3C-$3E the three wheels, $3B the forward shot's DrawShotForward. The drawer gets HL = the position, DE = the graphics and returns through DrawSpriteDone, which restores BC and DE; HL is the drawer's. Drawn twice (XOR) to erase. Witness: game 7358, a1 1375, g1 2268, t1 16, p2 3220, h1 2268, i1 35 224, i2 42 588, h2 322 entries (i1 frames 262-30439).
$A843 DrawSpriteDone ; the drawers return here: BC and DE back

$A846 c ScrollFarBand ; The far band (lines 64-95): one pixel left a pass, 8 new columns from HillsStrip $EBC0 every 8 passes
> Only while the buggy is alive ($B216 = $FF). The counter $B224 runs 8 passes; at 0 the next 32 bytes of HillsStrip ($B226 + $20, from $EBC0 up to $F000 and round) go to FeedFarBand $5B00. Each pass every line of 64-95 (the line table at $FC80) rolls left one pixel through its 32 bytes, the new bit from the line's FeedFarBand byte. Witness: game 1576, a1 292, g1 493, p2 664, h1 493, i1 3601, i2 3935, h2 40 entries: once a main-loop pass.
$A865 FarFeedSet ; the next strip into FeedFarBand, 8 passes
$A872 FarRoll ; the 32 lines from the line table at $FC80 (line 64)
$A87F FarLine ; HL = the line's last byte, one bit from the feed byte
$A88E FarByte

$A89A c ScrollMiddleBand ; The middle band (lines 96-127): two pixels left a pass, 8 new columns from MountainsStrip or MoonBaseStrip every 4 passes
> As ScrollFarBand with the counter $B228 (4 passes), the source $B22A going from $B22C to $B22E and round (set at the life start by $95B0 from the scenery flag $B27D), the feed FeedMiddleBand $5B20 and the lines 96-127 ($FCC0). Witness: the same counts as ScrollFarBand (i1 3600).
$A8BA MiddleFeedSet ; the next strip into FeedMiddleBand, 4 passes
$A8C7 MiddleRoll ; the 32 lines from the line table at $FCC0 (line 96)
$A8D4 MiddleLine ; two bits from the feed byte
$A8EA MiddleByte

$A8FD c ScrollNearBand ; The band of lines 128-159: two pixels left while the buggy is in the air, else the same time in a delay
> Only while alive. BuggyAirborne $AFA5 (buggy y < $89): the 32 lines from $FD00 (line 128) roll two pixels left with zero bits coming in; on the ground the delay $A929 ($0AF5 loops) instead, so the pass takes about as long. ScrollAdjustX moves what is drawn in this band by 2 to match (it holds the buggy: drawn at y - 9 = $89). Witness: same counts as ScrollFarBand; the delay ran game 1114, i1 1524 of 3600 passes.
$A90C NearBandLine
$A918 NearBandByte
$A929 NearBandDelay ; the buggy on the ground: no scroll, a delay of $0AF5 loops
$A92C NearBandDelayLoop

$A932 c ScrollRoad ; The road (lines 160-191): four pixels left a pass, new columns from RoadFeed every 2 passes; random texture on its top line
> Only while alive. The counter $B234 runs 2 passes; at 0 the 32 bytes at $B232 (RoadFeed $A9AF, its crater edge strips set by the crater handler $9A92) go to FeedRoad $5B40. Each pass the 32 lines from $FD40 (line 160) roll four pixels left. Then random bits (FRAMES xor R, AND $0F, or AND $03 once when $B288 bit 3 is set, which it clears) are ORed into $509F, the last byte of line 160: the road's broken top edge.
> Witness: same counts as ScrollFarBand (i1 3600). The h2 snapshot was taken inside it (PC $A981), which is why the cut had entries at $A957 / $A977 / $A981.
$A94A RoadRoll ; the 32 lines from the line table at $FD40 (line 160)
$A957 RoadLine ; four bits from the feed byte
$A977 RoadByte
$A981 RoadByteBits
$A99E RoadTexture ; C = $0F, or $03 once when $B288 bit 3 was set

$A9AF b RoadFeed 16 ; The road's feed strips: 32 bytes a strip, one a line of 160-191: plain road, then the crater's two walls
> ScrollRoad copies the strip at $B232 to FeedRoad every 2 passes. The life start ($90D3) and the crater handler ($9A92, x = $EC) set the plain strip, the crater handler the wall strips as the crater comes in from the right (RoadFeedWallA or RoadFeedWallB). l1sum --reads: every byte read (game 667, i1 1727 times each).
$A9AF ; plain road: no bits under line 160
$A9CF RoadFeedWallA ; a crater wall: $FF $FF $7F $7F $3F ... down to line 175, then nothing
$A9EF RoadFeedWallB ; the other wall: $FF $FF $FE $FE $FC ... down to line 175, then nothing

$AA0F c BuggyUpdate ; The buggy each pass: the controls, the jump, left / right, the crater probe, the draw; the death frames; the BREAK abort
> Alive ($B216 = $FF): ControlsRead $AE17 into C. Jump (bit 1): the jump index $B223 goes from $FF to 0 and then on, one step a pass even with the key up, through the arc at $B221 (JumpArcs, chosen by SpeedGovernor) until its $80 ends it ($FF again). The step adds to the buggy y $B212.
> On the ground only: slow (bit 4) moves x $B213 two pixels left down to $29, fast (bit 3) two right up to $50, neither drifts back to $38 by two. Then the crater probe: five points x + 5, + 10, ... + 25 on the screen line 162 ($5280, just under the road's top line 160, through the pixel table $FA00): a set pixel there is a crater's wall, BuggyKill $A7DD and the crater flag $B220 = $FF.
> Then the draw at (x, y - 9) through ScrollAdjustIfAlive: sprite 0 alive, else the death frame: $B216 + 1 stored, sprite $1E + $B216 ($1F-$26). The main loop $96BB ends the life when $B216 reaches 8.
> Last the BREAK test: CAPS SHIFT (port $FEFE bit 0) and SPACE (port $7FFE bit 0) both down: SetIm1 and a jump to $8004, the title (a1 frame 3174, $AACD 1 time). The test runs in play and during the death frames.
> Witness: game 1576, a1 292, g1 493, p2 664, h1 493, i1 3601, i2 3935, h2 40 entries (i1 frames 263-30438); jump steps (AA2A, one a pass of a jump) game 463, i1 3249; the jump key down ($AA24) game 75 passes; the probe hits $AA9C game 18, a1 1, p2 4, i1 69, i2 67.
$AA17 ; C = the controls: bit 0 fire, 1 jump, 3 fast, 4 slow
$AA1F ; the jump index $B223: $FF not jumping
$AA27 BuggyNoJumpKey ; not jumping and no key: no step
$AA2A BuggyJumpStep ; the next byte of the arc: a y step, or $80 the end
$AA3F BuggyJumpDelta
$AA40 BuggyMoveY ; y + the step
$AA4D ; in the air: no left / right and no probe
$AA5B BuggyFast ; bit 3: right, up to x = $50
$AA67 BuggyDrift ; no key: back towards x = $38
$AA77 BuggyDriftLeft
$AA79 BuggyProbe ; five points of line 162 under the buggy, from x + 5 in steps of 5
$AA81 BuggyProbeLoop ; the pixel table $FA00: byte column and mask of x
$AA9C BuggyFellInCrater ; a set pixel: the crater kills; flag $B220
$AAA4 BuggyDrawAt
$AAA7 BuggyDraw ; drawn 9 lines higher than its y
$AAB5 ; dying: the next death frame
$AABB BuggySprite ; sprite 0 alive, $1F-$26 dying
$AABF BuggyBreakTest ; CAPS SHIFT and SPACE together: back to the title
$AACD ; the abort (a1 frame 3174)

$AAD3 c BuggyErase ; Erases the buggy drawn last pass (XOR), alive or in a death frame
> HL = (x, y - 9). Alive: sprite 0. $B216 = 0 (killed this pass): the position through ScrollAdjustX and sprite 0. Dying: sprite $1E + $B216, the frame BuggyUpdate drew. Witness: the same counts as BuggyUpdate (game 1576, i1 3601).
$AAE5 BuggyEraseFrame
$AAEB BuggyEraseSprite

$AAF0 b JumpArcs 13 ; The three jump arcs: y steps a pass, $80 ends; back position (short), centre, forward (long)
> SpeedGovernor sets $B221 to one of them from the buggy's x (JumpSpeeds $AF68), BuggyUpdate adds a byte a pass. The sums are 0: the buggy lands where it took off. l1sum --reads: all 71 bytes read.
$AAF0 ; JumpArcs proper, the back arc: x < $38: 12 steps, 10 pixels up
$AAFD JumpArcCentre ; x = $38: 24 steps, 19 pixels up
$AB16 JumpArcForward ; x > $38: 32 steps, 20 pixels up

$AB37 c WheelsFly ; At a death: the three wheels fly off for 32 steps along WheelPaths
> Only while dying ($B216 not $FF), four calls a main-loop pass. At wheel step $B217 = 0 the wheels start at y $97 and x + 1, x + $0A, x + $16 ($B21A, $B21C, $B21E; sprites $3C, $3D, $3E), the path pointer $B218 = WheelPaths. Each step every wheel still on screen is erased (WheelErase), moved by its two bytes (y up by the first, x by the second) and drawn; the first leaves left, the third right (a carry out of x ends a wheel, its word = 0). $B217 + 1.
> Witness: game 6303, a1 1164, g1 1972, p2 2656, h1 1972, i1 14 401, i2 15 737, h2 161 entries (the calls); the work after the alive test ran only when a death counted: $AB5F game 611, a1 67, g1 140, p2 268, h1 140, h2 35, none in i1 / i2 (BuggyKill is RET there).
$AB5F WheelsStep ; IX = the path, the three wheels in turn
$AB70 WheelOneMove ; the left wheel: x - 3 a step
$AB8E WheelOneDraw
$AB9E WheelTwo ; the middle wheel
$ABAB WheelTwoMove
$ABC9 WheelTwoDraw
$ABD9 WheelThree ; the right wheel
$ABE6 WheelThreeMove
$AC04 WheelThreeDraw
$AC14 WheelsStepDone ; the path pointer and the step $B217 + 1

$AC20 c WheelErase ; Erases wheel sprite A at HL, except at step 0 (nothing drawn yet)
> Witness: game 1598, a1 176, g1 376, p2 716, h1 376, h2 88 entries (a1 frames 2010-2886); none in i1 / i2.

$AC31 b WheelPaths 6 ; The wheels' paths: 32 steps of y up, x for the left, middle and right wheel
> Read by WheelsFly. The y steps (up positive) fall from 11 or 12 to -7 (the outer wheels) or -2 (the middle one): arcs; x steps -3, 0, +4. l1sum --reads: read in game, a1, g1, p2, h1, h2 (the deaths).

$ACF1 c FireShots ; Fire: a forward shot and an upward shot on each new press
> Only while alive. ControlsRead bit 0; up: $B288 bit 1 cleared. A new press (bit 1 was clear) sets it, then: ShotForward $5B60 free ($FF): y + 1, x + $20, the end x + $80, drawn (DrawShotForward). And an upward shot when no shot in ShotsUp is still below line $6D: a free one gets y - 12 (AND $FC) and x + 8, drawn (DrawShotUp). Witness: game 1576, i1 3601 entries; new presses $AD06 game 153, a1 12, g1 42, p2 55, h1 42, i1 332, i2 367.
$AD06 FireNewPress
$AD2E FireUpCheck ; no new upward shot while one is still below line $6D
$AD33 FireUpCheckLoop
$AD44 FireUpFree
$AD4F FireUpStart ; y - 12 on a multiple of 4, x + 8

$AD61 c ShotsMove ; Moves the forward shot and the four upward shots
> ShotForwardMove then ShotsUpMove. Witness: game 1576, a1 291, g1 493, p2 664, h1 493, i1 3601, i2 3935, h2 40 entries.

$AD68 c ShotForwardMove ; The forward shot: erase, 6 pixels right, end at its end x or when hit, draw
> Erased (DrawShotForward), first through ScrollAdjustX when the buggy was killed this pass by something else than a crater ($B216 = 1, $B220 = 0). Its height byte $FF (an object handler hit it): free ($5B60 = $FF). Else height 3, x + 6; at the end x free, else drawn again. Witness: same entry counts as ShotsMove; moves $AD9E game 440, i1 2640.
$AD8E ShotForwardStep
$AD9E ShotForwardDraw

$ADAC c ShotsUpMove ; The four upward shots: ShotUpMove on each one in use
> Witness: same entry counts as ShotsMove.
$ADB1 ShotsUpLoop

$ADBF c ShotUpMove ; One upward shot: erase, 4 pixels up, end above line $18 or when hit, draw
> As ShotForwardMove with height 4 and DrawShotUp. Witness: game 951, a1 271, g1 987, p2 1236, h1 987, i1 7833, i2 8636 entries.
$ADDF ShotUpStep
$ADE9 ShotUpDraw

$ADF7 c DrawShotForward ; The forward shot: 6 pixels across from (x, y + 1), XOR
> Also sprite $3B of the sprite table. Witness: game 936, a1 211, g1 709, p2 914, h1 709, i1 5642, i2 6103 entries.
$ADFA DrawShotForwardLoop

$AE04 c DrawShotUp ; An upward shot: 4 pixels down from (x + 1, y), XOR
> Witness: game 1913, a1 545, g1 1980, p2 2483, h1 1980, i1 15 680, i2 17 284, h2 30 entries.
$AE07 DrawShotUpLoop

$AE11 c ShotPlot ; One pixel through $FDB3, HL kept
> Witness: game 13 268, a1 3566, i1 97 928 entries.

$AE17 c ControlsRead ; Reads the chosen control (control type $B259, 1-6) and returns its bits active high: 0 fire, 1 up / jump, 2 down, 3 right / fast, 4 left / slow
> Jumps to ControlHandlers[$B259 - 1]; each handler returns the five bits active low with the others 1, and ControlsReadDone inverts them (CPL): bits 5-7 come back 0. Bit 2 (down) is read by no caller. Callers: the options page ($8227: fire alone starts the game when the control is not the keyboard), BuggyUpdate, FireShots, the hold.
> $B259 is the third option of the options page (keys Z X C V B K, the text at $8586; 6 in the entry image). Witness: game 2939, a1 28 792, g1 922, p2 1208, h1 922, i1 7202, i2 7870, h2 64 entries; the handler of each run below.
$AE2C ControlsReadDone ; active low to active high

$AE2E w ControlHandlers 1 ; The six control readers by $B259 - 1, in the options page's order Z X C V B K
> Read by ControlsRead (l1sum --reads: $AE38 a1 28 753, g1 922, ... i1 7202: the keyboard; $AE2E game 2296, $AE30 game 643: c1's random keys chose Sinclair and Kempston; every word a1 7-8: the options page).
$AE2E ; 1 Z: Sinclair with 1 Joystick
$AE30 ; 2 X: Kempston Joystick
$AE32 ; 3 C: Cursor Joystick
$AE34 ; 4 V: Old-type Fuller Box with Joystick
$AE36 ; 5 B: Sinclair with 2 Joysticks
$AE38 ; 6 K: Keyboard (A slow, Q fast, O jump, P fire)

$AE3A c JoySinclair1 ; Control 1 (Z, Sinclair with 1 Joystick): both Interface 2 sticks, keys 6-0 and 1-5, ANDed
> Port $EFFE (6 left, 7 right, 8 down, 9 up, 0 fire: bits 4-0 already in the common order) into E; port $F7FE (1 left, 2 right, 3 down, 4 up, 5 fire) with its five bits reversed into C; C AND E OR $E0. Either stick drives the buggy. Witness: a1 8 runs (frames 779-833, the options page with Z chosen), game 2296 (c1's random keys chose it: frames 1395-13759). No joystick was moved: the paths ran with keys or nothing.
$AE3F JoySinclairKeys1To5 ; port $F7FE, its bits 0-4 reversed (JoySinclair2 enters here with E = $FF for player 2)
$AE45 JoySinclairReverse

$AE4F c JoySinclair2 ; Control 5 (B, Sinclair with 2 Joysticks): one stick a player in a two-player game
> One player ($B257 = 1): as JoySinclair1. Two players: player 1 ($B271 = 1) reads port $EFFE (keys 6-0) only, player 2 port $F7FE (keys 1-5) only, through JoySinclairKeys1To5 with E = $FF. Witness: a1 8 runs (frames 1016-1069, the options page, one player); the two-player paths were not executed in any recording (game, a1, g1, t1, p2, h1, i1, i2, h2): known only from the code (p2 used the keyboard).

$AE64 c JoyKempston ; Control 2 (X, Kempston Joystick): IN ($1F), active high bits 0 right, 1 left, 2 down, 3 up, 4 fire, put in the common order
> The five bits rotated through B into fire 0, up 1, down 2, right 3, left 4, then CPL and OR $E0: active low like the others. Witness: a1 8 runs (frames 841-894, the options page), game 643 (frames 13 766-16 753). The emulator had no Kempston input: the port read only what it gives when nothing is pressed.

$AE7E c JoyCursor ; Control 3 (C, Cursor Joystick): 5 left, 6 down, 7 up, 8 right, 0 fire
> Port $F7FE bit 4 (5) to bit 4 left; port $EFFE bit 0 (0) fire, bit 3 (7) to bit 1 up, bit 4 (6) to bit 2 down, bit 2 (8) to bit 3 right. Witness: a1 7 runs (frames 902-947, the options page); never in play.

$AE9D c JoyFuller ; Control 4 (V, Old-type Fuller Box with Joystick): IN ($7F), active low bits 0 up, 1 down, 2 left, 3 right, 7 fire
> Rotated into fire 0, up 1, down 2, right 3, left 4 (the other bits 1). Witness: a1 8 runs (frames 955-1008, the options page); never in play.

$AEB2 c KeysQAOP ; Control 6 (K, Keyboard): P fire, O jump, Q fast, A slow
> Port $FDFE bit 0 (A) to bit 4, port $FBFE bit 0 (Q) to bit 3, port $DFFE bits 0 (P) and 1 (O) to bits 0 and 1. The only control of every play recording: a1 28 753 runs (frames 1424-21149), g1 922, p2 1208, h1 922, i1 7202, i2 7870, h2 64.

$AECE c HoldCheck ; The hold: H stops the game (IM 1, the clock frozen) until H again, S or fire
> Port $BFFE bit 4 (H). Up: $B288 bit 4 cleared (HoldKeyUp). Down while bit 4 is set (still the same press): nothing. A new press: bit 4 set, SetIm1 (the game's handler and its clock $7F98 stop), wait for H up, 5 frames, then HoldLoop until fire (ControlsRead bit 0: at once), S (port $FDFE bit 1: at once) or H (5 frames); SetIm2 $A7D4 and back to the main loop.
> Witness: game 1575, a1 291, g1 493, p2 664, h1 493, i1 3600, i2 3934, h2 41 entries; the hold itself only in a1 (a new press $AEE0 1 time, HoldWaitRelease 30 472 loops, HoldLoop 28 199, left with H through HoldResumeWait 5 HALTs: a1 frames 2060-2220 of NOTES). Leaving with S or fire was not executed in any recording: known only from the code.
$AEE0 ; IM 1: the game's clock stops while held
$AEE3 HoldWaitRelease ; until H is up
$AEEC HoldWait
$AEEF HoldLoop ; fire, S or H again
$AEF6 ; S: resume at once
$AF00 ; H: resume after 5 frames
$AF0B HoldResumeWait
$AF0E HoldResume ; IM 2 again
$AF12 HoldKeyUp ; H up: the next press holds again

$AF15 c TuneToggleCheck ; T toggles the tune: $B288 bit 7 flips on each new press
> Port $FBFE bit 4 (T). Up: $B288 bit 0 cleared. A new press (bit 0 clear): bit 0 set and bit 7 flipped; the tune player $7F47 is silent while bit 7 is set. Witness: game 1576, a1 292, g1 493, p2 664, h1 493, i1 3601, i2 3935, h2 40 entries; a1 pressed T at 2540 and 2740 (NOTES), the toggle path $AF22-$AF29 a1 6 runs (292 - 286).
$AF2B TuneKeyUp
$AF2D TuneFlagsStore

$AF31 c SpeedGovernor ; The pace of the game: a delay a pass and the jump arc chosen by the buggy's x
> x $B213 against $38: forward (> $38) the index 0, centre 1, back (< $38) 2 into JumpSpeeds $AF68: the jump arc to $B221 and the delay count, + $1800 while dying. Then a busy loop of that many iterations: forward 1, centre $02EE, back $05DC. Called twice a main-loop pass (game 3151, a1 582, g1 986, p2 1328, h1 986, i1 7200, i2 7868, h2 81 entries).
$AF3D SpeedFromX
$AF49 SpeedIndex
$AF62 SpeedDelay

$AF68 w JumpSpeeds 2 ; Three records by the buggy's position: jump arc, delay count
> l1sum --reads: forward $AF68 i1 7056 of 7200 (the immune runs held fast), centre $AF6C game 2663, back $AF70 game 228 only.
$AF68 ; forward (x > $38): JumpArcForward, 1 loop
$AF6C ; centre (x = $38): JumpArcCentre, $02EE loops
$AF70 ; back (x < $38): JumpArcs (the back arc), $05DC loops

$AF74 c ScrollAdjustIfAlive ; ScrollAdjustX, only while the buggy is alive
> Witness: game 3474, a1 716, g1 2054, p2 2534, h1 2054, i1 19 540, i2 25 107, h2 90 entries.

$AF79 c ScrollAdjustX ; Adds the scroll step of y's band to x (H): what is drawn there moves with its band
> L = y: below $40 nothing; $40-$5F the far band's step HillsSpeed $B225 (1); $60-$7F the middle band's MiddleSpeed $B229 (2); $80-$9F MiddleSpeed only while BuggyAirborne (the near band scrolls then); $A0 and down the road's $B235 (4). Returns through BuggyAirborne. Witness: game 3022, a1 672, g1 1932, p2 2277, h1 1932, h2 78 entries (falls in from ScrollAdjustIfAlive in i1 / i2, so no entries there).
$AF87 ScrollAdjustMiddle
$AF90 ScrollAdjustNear
$AF99 ScrollAdjustNearBand ; lines $80-$9F: only while the buggy is in the air
$AFA3 ScrollAdjustAdd

$AFA5 c BuggyAirborne ; Carry when the buggy is in the air: its y $B212 below $89 (on the ground $92)
> Witness: game 4445, a1 946, g1 2389, p2 2873, h1 2389, i1 23 140, i2 29 041, h2 109 entries.

@module sprites ; The block copy and the pre-shifted XOR sprite drawers of the sprite table
@about BlitRows copies a block of bytes to the screen (the title and the HUD). The drawers serve DrawSprite $A826: HL = the position (H = x, L = y), DE = the graphics. Each finds the screen line through the line table $FC00 (a word a line), the byte column from x / 8, and the shifted copy from x AND 7 (8 copies) or x AND 6 (4 copies, two pixels apart), then XORs the rows. Two of them also colour the cells with the caller's E'.

$AFAB c BlitRows ; Copies rows of bytes to the screen: rows $B247 to $B248 - 1, at x $B249, $B24A bytes a row, from HL
> The line table $FC00 gives each row's address, the pixel table $FA00 x's byte column. Callers: the title ($8004: two logos) and the HUD of each life start ($9136). Witness: game 23, a1 8, g1 7, t1 2, p2 11, h1 9, i1 14, i2 14, h2 1 entries (a1 frames 20-20424).
$AFAF BlitRowsLoop

$AFDD c DrawSprite16x4 ; A 2-byte, 4-line sprite: 8 shifted copies of 8 bytes
> Sprites $11, $12, $1D, $1E. Witness: a1, t1 (the score advance table), i1 8626, i2 9653 instructions at the entry.

$B003 c DrawSprite16x8 ; A 2-byte, 8-line sprite: 4 shifted copies of 16 bytes
> Sprites $40-$43. Witness: a1 20, t1 1, i2 670 runs.

$B02A c DrawSprite16x9 ; A 2-byte, 9-line sprite: 8 shifted copies of 18 bytes (the wheels)
> Sprites $3C-$3E. Witness: game 3118, a1 342, g1 728, p2 1392, h1 728, h2 170 runs: the deaths (WheelsFly); none in i1 / i2.

$B052 c SpriteXor2Wide ; The rows of the 2-byte drawers: XOR 2 bytes a line, B lines
> Witness: game 28 062, i1 34 504 instructions at the entry.

$B069 c DrawSprite24x8Attr ; A 3-byte, 8-line sprite in colour: 4 shifted copies of 24 bytes; 3 x 2 cells set to E'
> Sprites 1-$0A, $27-$2A, $37-$3A. Witness: a1 40, t1 2, i1 13 327, i2 10 725 runs.
$B0AA DrawSprite24x8Cells

$B0BA c DrawSprite24x8 ; A 3-byte, 8-line sprite: 8 shifted copies of 24 bytes
> Sprite $14. Witness: a1 20, t1 1, i2 374 runs.

$B0E2 c DrawSprite24x12 ; A 3-byte, 12-line sprite: 4 shifted copies of 36 bytes
> Sprites $0F, $2B-$2E, $44, $45. Witness: game 1049, a1 163, g1 546, p2 484, h1 546, i1 2342, i2 4368, h2 71 runs.

$B10D c DrawSprite24x16 ; A 3-byte, 16-line sprite: 4 shifted copies of 48 bytes
> Sprite $10. Witness: a1 40, t1 2, i1 15, i2 1072 runs.

$B115 c DrawSprite24x16Attr ; A 3-byte, 16-line sprite in colour: 4 shifted copies of 48 bytes; 3 x 3 cells set to E'
> Sprites $0B-$0E, $2F-$32. Witness: a1 20, t1 1, i1 3164, i2 4112 runs.
$B134 DrawSprite24x16Cells

$B143 c SpriteSetup24 ; The line table entry and the copy of a 3-byte, 16-line sprite (48 bytes a shift)
> Witness: a1 60, t1 3, i1 3179, i2 5184 entries.

$B168 c SpriteXor3Wide ; The rows of the 3-byte drawers: XOR 3 bytes a line, B lines
> Witness: game 12 588, i1 185 584 instructions at the entry.

$B184 c DrawSprite32x16 ; A 4-byte, 16-line sprite: 4 shifted copies of 64 bytes
> No sprite of $00-$45 uses it. Witness: a1 20, t1 1, i2 1524 runs.
$B1A7 DrawSprite32x16Rows

$B1C8 c DrawSprite40x23 ; A 5-byte, 23-line sprite: 4 shifted copies, 128 bytes apart (the buggy)
> Sprite 0 (the buggy, $E140), $13, the death frames $1F-$26 and $33-$36. Witness: game 3191, a1 610, g1 994, t1 1, p2 1344, h1 994, i1 7524, i2 9168, h2 81 runs.
$B1EC DrawSprite40x23Rows

@module variables ; Variables: the buggy, the scrolling layers, the course state, the menus and the two player records
@about All of $B212-$B2C1 is game state; the loaded image holds the values the build had when it was saved, and the code sets most of them before use. A variable's readers and writers are given as code sites; the routines there are named by their address.
$B212 b BuggyY 1 ; The buggy's pixel line (top of its sprite), $92 at each life start
> Set with BuggyX at the life start ($90EB LD HL,$3892; $90EE: y $92, x $38) and by the jump in the buggy routine at $AA0F ($AA46, $AA79); read as a pair with BuggyX by the main loop ($96C5), by the shots ($AD0E) and by every object handler that tests a hit against the buggy ($99F0, $9E1F, $9EEA, $9FCA, $A0BB, $A1C4, $A34C).
$B213 b BuggyX 1 ; The buggy's x in pixels (left edge), $38 at each life start
> Read by the object handlers to place or aim at the buggy ($9A2F, $9ACA, $9B27, $9F29, $9FF7, $A10E).
$B214 b BuggyBoxHeight 1 ; The buggy's collision box height, $0D
> Read with BuggyBoxWidth by the box test at $A7FE; never written.
$B215 b BuggyBoxWidth 1 ; The buggy's collision box width, $1D
> Read by the box test at $A7FE; never written.
$B216 b DeathStep 1 ; $FF while the buggy lives; 0 when hit, then 1-8 through the explosion frames
> Set to $FF at the life start ($90E5), to 0 by the routine at $A7DD (a hit). The buggy routine at $AA0F counts it up and draws sprite $1E + DeathStep; the main loop ($96BE) ends the life when it reaches 8. Every hazard handler reads it to stop hitting a dead buggy ($99DE, $9A61, $9A9B ...).
$B217 b WreckStep 1 ; The wreck pieces' step after a death
> Set to $FF at the life start ($90E8), set at a hit ($A7E5) and by the main loop ($96D6); counted by the wreck routine at $AB37 ($AB3C, $AC18, $AC1C).
$B218 w WreckPath ; Pointer into the wreck pieces' flight path at $AC31
> Set at $AB5C, read through IX at $AB5F, moved on at $AC14.
$B21A w WreckPiece1 ; Wreck piece 1's position (low byte y, high byte x)
> Set at $AB4A, read and moved at $AB63-$AB92 by the wreck routine at $AB37.
$B21C w WreckPiece2 ; Wreck piece 2's position
> $AB50, $AB9E-$ABCD.
$B21E w WreckPiece3 ; Wreck piece 3's position
> $AB56, $ABD9-$AC08.
$B220 b DeadFlag 1 ; $FF after a crater fall until the next life start, 0 otherwise
> Set only by the crater fall at $AAA1 (the shots use it to tell a crater death from an object kill), cleared at the life start ($90F7). While 0 the shots move with the ground ($AD72, $ADC3 read it).
$B221 w JumpArc ; Pointer into the jump arc table at $AF68 during a jump
> Set at $AF5E when the jump starts; read each pass by $AA2E.
$B223 b JumpStep 1 ; The jump's step, $FF on the ground
> Set at the life start ($90E2); read and written by the buggy routine at $AA0F ($AA1F-$AA49).
$B224 b FarCountdown 1 ; Passes left to the next column fed into FeedFarBand (the far band rolls 1 pixel every pass)
> Set at $9568 and $A873, counted at $A84B by the far-band scroller at $A846 (a new column every 8 passes).
$B225 b HillsSpeed 1 ; The far band's (HillsStrip's) step, 1; never written
> Read by $AF82 (the shots' drift).
$B226 w FarColumn ; Pointer to the next column of HillsStrip $EBC0 to feed in
> Set at $956E; read and moved by the scroller at $A851/$A865, wrapping from $F000 to $EBC0.
$B228 b MiddleCountdown 1 ; Passes left to the next 2-pixel scroll of the middle layer (mountains or city)
> Set at $9565 and $A8C8, counted at $A89F by the scroller at $A89A (one scroll every 4 passes).
$B229 b MiddleSpeed 1 ; The middle layer's step, 2; never written
> Read by $AF8B and $AFA0.
$B22A w MiddleColumn ; Pointer to the next column of the middle strip to feed in
> Set at $9561; read and moved at $A8A5/$A8BA.
$B22C w MiddleStart ; The middle strip's first column: MountainsStrip $E340 or MoonBaseStrip $E780
> Set by the routine at $9541 ($9559) from the scenery byte; read at $957A and $A8B7 (the wrap).
$B22E w MiddleEnd ; The middle strip's end, compared at $A8AC
> Set at $955D.
$B230 w ColourScheme ; Pointer to the scenery's colour set at $9617 or $961C
> Set by the routine at $95B0 ($95BC); read through IX by $95F2 and by the handlers that colour sprites ($A053, $A12C, $A148, $A165 ...).
$B232 w GroundProfile ; Pointer to the ground's current height profile
> Set at the life start ($9111) and by the crater handler ($9AB4, $9AC5); read by the ground scroller at $A93D.
$B234 b GroundCountdown 1 ; The ground scroller's pass counter
> Set at $90FA; read and written at $A937/$A94B.
$B235 b GroundSpeed 1 ; The ground's step in pixels a pass, 4; never written
> Read by every handler that moves an object with the ground ($99E4, $9A67, $9AA2, $9B1E, $9EDE, $9F71 ...).
$B236 b UfoWave 1 ; The UFO wave's state, set by the UFO commands and by the UFO handler
> Written at $9833 and $9B84; read at $9862, $987F, $9C0E, $9C52 ...
$B237 b UfosLeave 1 ; $FF: the UFOs fly off (the course command at $98AA); 0 at the life start
> Set at $90F4, $9B99 and $9EAF; read by the UFO handler at $9BE6.
$B238 b ObjectsOff 1 ; Nonzero: the main loop does not run the object list
> Set at $819E (the demo's start, $80) and $969B; read by $9745.
$B239 b TimeShown 1 ; Nonzero once this second's time is on screen; cleared each second
> Cleared by the interrupt routine at $7FAE when FrameCount reaches 50; set at $9223 after the time is printed; tested at $922A.
$B23A b TapeKey 1 ; The key read by the tape menu at $8282, tested at $8309
$B23B b PassCount 1 ; A pass counter; its bit 2 blinks the mines
> Incremented by the main loop ($96E3); read by the mine handler ($9B42): sprite $11 + bit 2.
$B23C w SegmentWait ; Passes left before the course script runs its next segment
> Set by the script command at $976C ($9770); counted down by the main loop ($96E7/$96EB).
$B23E w TunePointer ; The next word of Tune $B4E6
> Set at the life start ($9117); read and moved by the interrupt routine ($7F52, $7F60), reset to the start at $FFFF ($7F70).
$B240 b TuneDivider 1 ; Interrupts left before the next note (one note every 3)
> Set at $90FD; counted at $7F47 and $7FA1.
$B241 w SectorTime ; The sector's par time, a big-endian BCD word of seconds ($0120 = 120 s; the clock never wraps at 60), from the SectorEnd command
> Set at $A4F2; read at $A531 and $A584.
$B243 w BestTime ; The sector's best time so far (BCD), from the SectorEnd command
> Set at $A507; read at $A53D, $A632, $A64A.
$B245 w SectorBonusPoints ; The sector's bonus points (BCD hundreds) and the extra byte after it
> Set at $A4FA; read at $A54F, $A55B, $A5D0; rewritten at $A5B6. $A5BA reads its second byte ($B246).
$B247 b BlitTop 1 ; The copier $AFAB's first line
> Set as a pair with BlitBottom ($801B, $802D, $9157); read at $AFAB.
$B248 b BlitBottom 1 ; The copier's line after the last; read at $AFD5
$B249 b BlitX 1 ; The copier's x in pixels (the byte column is BlitX / 8)
> Set with BlitWidth ($8021, $8033, $915D); read at $AFB4.
$B24A b BlitWidth 1 ; The copier's width in bytes; read at $AFCD
$B24B b EntryLength 1 ; The initials entry field: number of characters
> The field $B24B-$B24F is filled from a 5-byte block by $8C06 / $8E99 / $8EB9 (LD DE,$B24B; LDIR) and read at $8CD0, $8D6F, $8D8A.
$B24C w EntryBuffer ; The entry field's buffer pointer; read at $8CDA, $8D82, $8D87, $8DAC
$B24E b EntryY 1 ; The entry field's pixel line; read at $8C9C, $8CCA
$B24F b EntryX 1 ; The entry field's x; read at $8CC4
$B250 w HeroSlot ; Pointer to the heroes table slot being written
> Set at $8C2E, $8C71, $8EE7; read at $8C44, $8C87, $8EEF.
$B252 b HeroCount 1 ; Heroes table work byte; with HeroIndex it is saved as a word
> $8EE3 LD ($B252),DE and $8EFB LD DE,($B252) keep DE here across the slot search loop $8EEA-$8EFF; $8CD3 reads it through HL.
$B253 b HeroIndex 1 ; The heroes table's index; read at $8CDE, $8D03
$B254 b EntryCursor 1 ; The entry field's cursor position
> Read and written at $8CE1-$8D76.
$B255 b TitleColour 1 ; The title frame's colour step, read and moved at $884D/$8853
$B256 b TableRow 1 ; The score advance table's row
> $8A52, $8A9C, $8AEF, $8B07, $8B50, $8B63.
$B257 b Players 1 ; Number of players, 1 or 2
> Set by the options page ($80DF); read at $8083, $80D3, $8F61, $8F8B, $8FB3 ...
$B258 b Course 1 ; The course: 1 Beginner, 2 Champion
> Set at $80F6; read at $808B, $80EA, $8F32.
$B259 b Controls 1 ; The control type, 1-6 (keys or a joystick)
> Set at $810D; read at $8093, $8101, $8231 and by the controls reader at $AE1C.
$B25A b OptionColour1 1 ; Colour of the players option line, moved through the cycle at $8408 by $8349
> Set at $8076; read at $80B7 (LD DE,$B25A), $80CF.
$B25B b OptionColour2 1 ; Colour of the course option line; $807B, $80E6
$B25C b OptionColour3 1 ; Colour of the controls option line; $8080, $80FD
$B25D w BasicStack ; BASIC's stack pointer, saved at the start ($8000)
> Restored at $8004, $8240, $8253, $831A ...; $80BA reads it through IX.
$B25F w LoopStack ; The main loop's stack pointer, saved at $9696
> Restored at $A689 (life end), $BB1D and $BEE9 (the course switches BeginnerToChampion and ChampionToBeginner).
$B261 w ObjectWrite ; Where the next object record is written in ObjectList $BEF8
> Set at $96A1; moved by the script commands ($96AA/$96B4) and by $9DAD/$9DC6.
$B263 w TapeStack ; The stack pointer saved around a tape load or save
> Set at $826B; restored at $827A, $82A4, $82B5, $8304.
$B265 w TapeErrSp ; BASIC's ERR_SP handler, saved around a tape load or save
> Set at $8277; read at $82A0, $82B1, $8300.
$B267 b UfoSeen 1 ; Written by the UFO handler ($9C75); never read
> The bytes $B267-$B270 are write-only flags, one set by each object kind when it first runs; nothing reads them. $B268, $B269 and $B26B are never touched.
$B268 b UnusedFlag1 2 ; Never read or written
$B26A b PlantSeen 1 ; Written by the plant handler ($A390); never read
$B26B b UnusedFlag2 1 ; Never read or written
$B26C b FlyerSeen 1 ; Written by the low flyer handler ($A107); never read
$B26D b UfoWaveSeen 1 ; Written by the UFO handler ($9B96); never read
$B26E b BoulderSeen 1 ; Written by the boulder handler ($A213); never read
$B26F b RockSeen 1 ; Written by the rock handler ($9A28); never read
$B270 b TankSeen 1 ; Written by the tank handler ($9F22); never read
$B271 b Player 1 ; The current player's record, 27 bytes: number, score, extra-life mark, course state, lives, time, flags, initials
> The player playing is always here; with two players the routine at $9022 swaps it with OtherPlayer $B28C through SwapBuffer $B2A7 (three LDIRs of 27 bytes). +0 is the player's number (1 or 2), printed at $8F80.
$B272 PlayerScore ; +1: the score, 4 BCD bytes big-endian (8 digits)
$B276 PlayerNextLife ; +5: the score of the next extra life, 4 BCD bytes (00 01 00 00 = 10000 at the start, +20000 after each; none once the score's second byte $B273 reaches 6)
$B27A PlayerCourse ; +9: the course script pointer (word)
$B27C PlayerPoint ; +$0B: the point letter code reached (+$20 on the Champion course)
$B27D PlayerScenery ; +$0C: the scenery, 0 mountains, $FF moon base city
$B27E PlayerLights ; +$0D: the warning lights state, drawn by $927E
$B27F PlayerRestart ; +$0E: the script pointer a new life restarts from (word)
$B281 PlayerSegment ; +$10: the script pointer of the current point (word)
$B283 PlayerActive ; +$12: nonzero while this player still has lives (word; $8F6B clears the other player's in a one-player game)
$B284 PlayerLives ; +$13: lives left (4 at the start, decremented at $8F74)
$B285 PlayerTime ; +$14: the sector time, 2 BCD bytes
$B287 PlayerFrames ; +$16: interrupts counted to 50 for each second
$B288 PlayerFlags ; +$17: bit 0 the T key's latch, bit 1 the fire latch, bit 3 set by the mine and plant handlers ($9B15, $A30B; read by the ground at $A994), bit 4 hold, bit 7 tune off (the T key toggles it, $AF27; the demo sets it, $8199)
$B289 PlayerInitials ; +$18: three initials ("JAS" in the image)
$B28C b OtherPlayer 1 ; The other player's record, same layout as Player $B271 (player 2, initials "MIK" in the image)
$B28D ; score
$B291 ; next extra life
$B295 ; course state, lights, pointers, active, lives, time, flags
$B2A4 ; initials
$B2A7 b SwapBuffer 1 ; 27 bytes used by the routine at $9022 to swap Player and OtherPlayer

@module tables ; Tables: a build-date string, the sprite table and the tune
$B2C2 t BuildDate0245 ; A print string with the build date 02:45 29/10/84, disabled like BuildDate0335 $7F00
> Bytes: $1F (the end, where an AT $16 would be), x 0, line $31, FONT 2 ($1A $02), "02:45 29/10/84", $1F. Printed by the life start at $912C (LD HL,$B2C2; CALL $FFD0): only the first byte is read (game 20, a1 4, g1 4, p2 8, h1 4, i1 6, i2 6), nothing appears.
@res text/BuildDate0245.bin
$B2D6 w SpriteTable 2 ; The sprite table: 132 entries of two words, drawer and graphics, indexed by sprite number
> The sprite drawer $A826 takes A = sprite number, reads the entry at $B2D6 + 4A and jumps to the drawer (JP (IX)) with HL = graphics, H = x, L = y. The drawers and their formats: see the graphics A module. An entry with drawer 0 is empty. 472 of the 528 bytes are read (the empty entries and the never-drawn $5C-$5F are not).
$B2D6 ; $00: the buggy (Buggy $E140)
$B2DA ; $01-$04: UFO A, 4 frames (UfoA $E020)
$B2EA ; $05-$0A: UFO B, 6 frames (UfoB $DEA0)
$B302 ; $0B-$0E: the spinner, 4 frames (Spinner $DBA0)
$B312 ; $0F: the rock (Rock $DB10)
$B316 ; $10: the bomb crater (BombCrater $DA50)
$B31A ; $11-$12: the mine's two blink frames (Mine $D9D0)
$B322 ; $13: the tank (Tank $D7D0)
$B326 ; $14: the low flyer (LowFlyer $D710)
$B32A ; $15-$1C: eight empty entries
$B34A ; $1D-$1E: the UFO bombs (UfoBomb $D690)
$B352 ; $1F-$26: the buggy's explosion, 8 frames (BuggyExplosion $6500)
$B372 ; $27-$2A: a UFO's small burst (BurstSmall $7D80)
$B382 ; $2B-$2E: a shot rock's debris (Debris $7900)
$B392 ; $2F-$32: a shot UFO's large burst, coloured (BurstLarge $7600)
$B3A2 ; $33-$36: the tank's explosion: BuggyExplosion's first 4 frames
$B3B2 ; $37-$3A: the low flyer's burst (BurstSmall $7D80)
$B3C2 ; $3B: drawer $ADF7 with graphics 0
$B3C6 ; $3C-$3E: the three wreck pieces (BuggyWreck1-3)
$B3D2 ; $3F: empty
$B3D6 ; $40-$43: the small boulder, 4 frames (BoulderSmall $D590)
$B3E6 ; $44-$4B: the medium boulder, 4 frames each twice (BoulderMedium $D350)
$B406 ; $4C-$57: the big boulder, 4 frames each three times (BoulderLarge $D050)
$B436 ; $58-$5B: the medium boulder's burst (BurstMedium $7B40)
$B446 ; $5C-$5F: Debris $7900 again, no user found
$B456 ; $60-$63: the big boulder's burst, uncoloured (BurstLarge $7600)
$B466 ; $64-$72: the plant opening and closing, 15 entries (PlantFrames $C450)
$B4A2 ; $73: the 500 sign (PlantScore $7500)
$B4A6 ; $74-$7A: the plant's spray, 7 entries (PlantSpray $CC50)
$B4C2 ; $7B-$7E: a shot plant's debris (Debris $7900)
$B4D2 ; $7F: empty
$B4D6 ; $80-$83: the small boulder's burst (BurstTiny $C350)
$B4E6 w Tune 8 ; The tune played through the game: 384 words, one note every 3 interrupts, $FFFF ends it
> The interrupt routine (from $7F47) counts TuneDivider $B240 down from 3; at 0 it takes the word at TunePointer $B23E. $0000 is a rest; another word is the period HL for the ROM BEEPER, with the duration DE chosen so a note lasts about 12 ms. $FFFF sends the pointer back to $B4E6 ($7F70). PlayerFlags bit 7 silences it. All 770 bytes (with the end word) are read in the runs.
@res tune/Tune.bin
$B7E6 TuneEnd ; $FFFF: start again

@module courses ; Courses: the Beginner and Champion course scripts and the two course switches
@about A course is a script of threaded words run by the interpreter at $9751: each word is the address of a command routine, and the command takes its parameters from the bytes after it. A segment runs until its End word ($9760); the main loop starts the next one when SegmentWait $B23C runs out. The commands: $976C Wait (a word: passes before the next segment), $9775 Point (delay byte, point letter code: the record at $9978 stores the code in PlayerPoint and the restart pointers), $97AD Lights (pairs of delay and lights state 0-3, $FF ends; the record at $99A3 stores PlayerLights and draws it at $927E), $97C5 Rocks, $97E8 Craters, $97FF Mines (byte AND $7F = delay, bit 7 = the other mine kind), $9821 / $9827 / $982D UFOs of kind 1 / 5 / $0B (UfosA, UfosB, Spinners), $98AA UfosLeave (one delay byte), $98BE Tanks, $98F3 LowFlyers, $9917 / $991C / $9921 small / medium / big boulders, $9955 Plants: each list command takes delay bytes until $FF and writes one object record per byte into ObjectList $BEF8; the delay is the record's countdown before the object appears. $A4EE SectorEnd ends a sector: par time (2 BCD bytes), bonus (1 BCD byte of hundreds), scenery ($FF city, 0 mountains), best time (2 BCD bytes, a word the code rewrites when beaten), then the next segment.
@about Below, each segment is labelled with the point it starts at and the first frame the point letter changes in the immune recording (i1 for Beginner, i2 for Champion); the commands are listed with their delay bytes in decimal.
$B7E8 b BeginnerCourse 16 ; The Beginner course script, points A-Z in five sectors (E, J, P, U, Z)
> Chosen by the game start ($8F2F LD BC,$B7E8) and by ChampionToBeginner $BED4; i1 plays it to Z. The first segment (i1 frame 160): Wait 127; Craters 20 80; Rocks 50 110.
> BeginnerBestE $B884: the best time for the sector to E, BCD, rewritten when beaten
> BeginnerAfterE $B886: the segment after the sector end
> BeginnerBestJ $B925: the best time for the sector to J, BCD, rewritten when beaten
> BeginnerAfterJ $B927: the segment after the sector end
> BeginnerBestP $B9AE: the best time for the sector to P, BCD, rewritten when beaten
> BeginnerAfterP $B9B0: the segment after the sector end
> BeginnerBestU $BA51: the best time for the sector to U, BCD, rewritten when beaten
> BeginnerAfterU $BA53: the segment after the sector end
> BeginnerBestZ $BB03: the best time for the sector to Z, BCD, rewritten when beaten
@res courses/BeginnerCourse.bin
$B7F8 BeginnerPointA ; point A
$B811 BeginnerPointB ; point B
$B82D BeginnerPointC ; point C
$B84F BeginnerPointD ; point D
$B86B BeginnerPointE ; point E
$B87E BeginnerSectorE ; SectorEnd after point E
$B884 BeginnerBestE
$B886 BeginnerAfterE
$B896 BeginnerPointF ; point F
$B8B8 BeginnerPointG ; point G
$B8D6 BeginnerPointH ; point H
$B8F5 BeginnerPointI ; point I
$B915 BeginnerPointJ ; point J
$B91F BeginnerSectorJ ; SectorEnd after point J
$B925 BeginnerBestJ
$B927 BeginnerAfterJ
$B936 BeginnerPointK ; point K
$B948 BeginnerPointL ; point L
$B95C BeginnerPointM ; point M
$B980 BeginnerPointN ; point N
$B995 BeginnerPointP ; point P
$B9A8 BeginnerSectorP ; SectorEnd after point P
$B9AE BeginnerBestP
$B9B0 BeginnerAfterP
$B9C1 BeginnerPointQ ; point Q
$B9E5 BeginnerPointR ; point R
$BA06 BeginnerPointS ; point S
$BA21 BeginnerPointT ; point T
$BA38 BeginnerPointU ; point U
$BA4B BeginnerSectorU ; SectorEnd after point U
$BA51 BeginnerBestU
$BA53 BeginnerAfterU
$BA6E BeginnerPointV ; point V
$BA83 BeginnerPointW ; point W
$BA98 BeginnerPointX ; point X
$BAB8 BeginnerPointY ; point Y
$BAEF BeginnerPointZ ; point Z
$BAFD BeginnerSectorZ ; SectorEnd after point Z
$BB03 BeginnerBestZ
$BB05 BeginnerSwitch ; the word $BB07
$BB07 c BeginnerToChampion ; The end of the Beginner course: go on with the Champion course
> Run once, in i1 at frames 29659-29663, after point Z: LD A,$20 into PlayerPoint (the Champion letters), CALL $93C5 with HL $4000 and C $60, CALL $9136, ChampionCourse $BB24 into PlayerCourse, then LD SP,(LoopStack) and JP $9696 restart the main loop.
$BB24 b ChampionCourse 16 ; The Champion course script, points A-Z in five sectors
> Chosen by the game start ($8F3A LD BC,$BB24) and by BeginnerToChampion $BB07; i2 plays it to Z. The first segment (i2 frame 340): Wait 127; Lights 15:1; UfosA 48 48 48; UfosB 48 48 48.
> ChampionBestE $BBC7: the best time for the sector to E, BCD, rewritten when beaten
> ChampionAfterE $BBC9: the segment after the sector end
> ChampionBestJ $BC95: the best time for the sector to J, BCD, rewritten when beaten
> ChampionAfterJ $BC97: the segment after the sector end
> ChampionBestP $BD42: the best time for the sector to P, BCD, rewritten when beaten
> ChampionAfterP $BD44: the segment after the sector end
> ChampionBestU $BDF0: the best time for the sector to U, BCD, rewritten when beaten
> ChampionAfterU $BDF2: the segment after the sector end
> ChampionBestZ $BED0: the best time for the sector to Z, BCD, rewritten when beaten
@res courses/ChampionCourse.bin
$BB3B ChampionPointA ; point A
$BB52 ChampionPointB ; point B
$BB6E ChampionPointC ; point C
$BB8F ChampionPointD ; point D
$BBAA ChampionPointE ; point E
$BBC1 ChampionSectorE ; SectorEnd after point E
$BBC7 ChampionBestE
$BBC9 ChampionAfterE
$BBEF ChampionPointF ; point F
$BC14 ChampionPointG ; point G
$BC3C ChampionPointH ; point H
$BC5B ChampionPointI ; point I
$BC7C ChampionPointJ ; point J
$BC8F ChampionSectorJ ; SectorEnd after point J
$BC95 ChampionBestJ
$BC97 ChampionAfterJ
$BCAC ChampionPointK ; point K
$BCC9 ChampionPointL ; point L
$BCDE ChampionPointM ; point M
$BD06 ChampionPointN ; point N
$BD29 ChampionPointP ; point P
$BD3C ChampionSectorP ; SectorEnd after point P
$BD42 ChampionBestP
$BD44 ChampionAfterP
$BD59 ChampionPointQ ; point Q
$BD78 ChampionPointR ; point R
$BD95 ChampionPointS ; point S
$BDAD ChampionPointT ; point T
$BDDC ChampionPointU ; point U
$BDEA ChampionSectorU ; SectorEnd after point U
$BDF0 ChampionBestU
$BDF2 ChampionAfterU
$BE07 ChampionPointV ; point V
$BE21 ChampionPointW ; point W
$BE44 ChampionPointX ; point X
$BE73 ChampionPointY ; point Y
$BEB3 ChampionPointZ ; point Z
$BECA ChampionSectorZ ; SectorEnd after point Z
$BED0 ChampionBestZ
$BED2 ChampionSwitch ; the word $BED4
$BED4 c ChampionToBeginner ; The end of the Champion course: start the Beginner course again
> Run once, in i2 at frames 30694-30698: XOR A into PlayerPoint, the same two calls as BeginnerToChampion, BeginnerCourse $B7E8 into PlayerCourse, the main loop restarted.
$BEF0 b CourseLeftover 8 ; Leftover: 8 bytes after the scripts ($02 $BB $5A $30 $5F $EE $3D $A8), never read

@module objectlist ; The object list, free space and the stack: zero in the loaded image
$BEF8 s ObjectList ; The live objects: one record per object, written by the course commands, run each pass
> Each record starts with the handler's address (a word) and the handler's own bytes; the first of them is the countdown before the object appears. The script commands write records at ObjectWrite $B261 (from $96A1: LD HL,$BEF8); the main loop runs the list through $9745 ($974A LD HL,$BEF8) and the interpreter at $9751, which stops at the end word $975E. The tape menu also clears it ($8288 LD IX,$BEF8, $830F). The record sizes the commands write: 20 bytes for a UFO ($9B6B), 14 a tank ($9EBD), 9 a low flyer ($A03B), 8 a boulder ($A196) or a plant ($A2C6). $BEF8-$C194 is written in the runs.
$C195 s FreeSpace ; Never written in the runs: free space between the object list and the stack ($C195-$C2FB), zero in the image
$C2FC s Stack ; The machine stack below BASIC's RAMTOP: the game keeps BASIC's stack (BasicStack $B25D); written from $C2FC to $C34F in the runs ($C2FC-$C311 by the IM 2 handler, its pushes and BEEPER's, which the L1 counters miss; $C312-$C337 seen by them)

@module graphicsb ; Graphics B: the enemies, the obstacles, the buggy and the three scenery strips
@about Same formats as graphics A (the drawers are listed there). The Beginner enemies are read in game and the other plays; the Champion-only ones (plants, boulders, tanks, low flyers, their bursts) in i2, and in a1 / t1 where the score advance table draws them ($8141, sixteen sprites from $83D8).
$C350 b BurstTiny 2 ; The small boulder's burst (sprites $80-$83): 4 frames of 2x8 bytes, 4 slots of 16
> Drawer $B003: 2 bytes x 8 lines, slot = x AND 6, 64 bytes a frame, frames at $C350, $C390, $C3D0, $C410. The boulder handler sets a small boulder's frame to $7F ($A1EA) and counts it to $83 ($A29C). Read only in i2, 96 of 256 bytes.
@res graphics/BurstTiny.bin
$C390 ; frame 2
$C3D0 ; frame 3
$C410 ; frame 4
$C450 b PlantFrames 4 ; The plant (sprites $64-$72): 8 frames of 4x16 bytes, 4 slots of 64; the sprites play them forward then back
> Drawer $B184: 4 bytes x 16 lines, slot = x AND 6, 256 bytes a frame, frames at $C450 + $100 n (n 0-7). Sprites $64-$6B are frames 1-8, $6C-$72 frames 7-1. The plant handler at $A2C6 draws sprite $63 + its step. Read in i2 66176 times and in a1 1284 (the score table); 1024 of 2048 bytes.
@res graphics/PlantFrames.bin
$C550 ; frame 2
$C650 ; frame 3
$C750 ; frame 4
$C850 ; frame 5
$C950 ; frame 6
$CA50 ; frame 7
$CB50 ; frame 8
$CC50 b PlantSpray 4 ; The plant's spray (sprites $74-$7A): 4 frames of 4x16, played forward then back
> Drawer $B184 as PlantFrames. Sprites $74-$77 are frames 1-4, $78-$7A frames 3-1; the plant handler uses step + $6F for steps 5-$0B ($A2E3). All 1024 bytes read, in i2 only.
@res graphics/PlantSpray.bin
$CD50 ; frame 2
$CE50 ; frame 3
$CF50 ; frame 4
$D050 b BoulderLarge 3 ; The big boulder (sprites $4C-$57): 4 frames of 3x16, each used by three sprites
> Drawer $B10D: 3 bytes x 16 lines, slot = x AND 6, 192 bytes a frame, frames at $D050, $D110, $D1D0, $D290. The course command gives the kind's first sprite ($40 / $44 / $4C); the handler at $A196 steps the sprite number through the kind's range, and consecutive numbers share a frame (three here, two for the medium boulder), so the picture changes every third step. Read in i2 46080 times and in a1 / t1.
@res graphics/BoulderLarge.bin
$D110 ; frame 2
$D1D0 ; frame 3
$D290 ; frame 4
$D350 b BoulderMedium 3 ; The medium boulder (sprites $44-$4B): 4 frames of 3x12, each used by two sprites
> Drawer $B0E2: 3 bytes x 12 lines, 4 slots of 36, 144 bytes a frame, frames at $D350, $D3E0, $D470, $D500. Read in i2, a1 and t1.
@res graphics/BoulderMedium.bin
$D3E0 ; frame 2
$D470 ; frame 3
$D500 ; frame 4
$D590 b BoulderSmall 2 ; The small boulder (sprites $40-$43): 4 frames of 2x8, 4 slots of 16
> Drawer $B003: 64 bytes a frame, frames at $D590, $D5D0, $D610, $D650. 128 of 256 bytes read (i2, a1, t1).
@res graphics/BoulderSmall.bin
$D5D0 ; frame 2
$D610 ; frame 3
$D650 ; frame 4
$D690 b UfoBomb 2 ; The UFO bombs (sprites $1D, $1E): 2 frames of 2x4 bytes, 8 slots of 8
> Drawer $AFDD: 2 bytes x 4 lines, slot = x AND 7, 64 bytes a frame. $1D is the falling bomb ($9892 stores it in a new bomb), $1E the bomb landing ($9D61). Read in i1 and i2  and in a1 / t1.
@res graphics/UfoBomb.bin
$D6D0 ; frame 2 (sprite $1E)
$D710 b LowFlyer 3 ; The low flyer (sprite $14): 3x8 bytes, 8 shift slots of 24
> Drawer $B0BA: 3 bytes x 8 lines, slot = x AND 7, 192 bytes. Its handler is at $A03B (record $9903); the tank's record also holds sprite $14 ($98DA). Read in i2, a1 and t1.
@res graphics/LowFlyer.bin
$D728 ; slot 1
$D740 ; slot 2
$D758 ; slot 3
$D770 ; slot 4
$D788 ; slot 5
$D7A0 ; slot 6
$D7B8 ; slot 7
$D7D0 b Tank 5 ; The tank (sprite $13): 5x23 bytes, 4 shift slots of 128
> Drawer $B1C8 (as the buggy): 115 bytes of each 128-byte slot used, slot = x AND 6. The handler at $9EBD; its explosion is sprites $33-$36 (BuggyExplosion's first frames). 460 of 512 bytes read: the padding never is. Read in i1, i2, a1, t1.
@res graphics/Tank.bin
$D843 ; 13 bytes of padding
$D850 ;  slot 1 (x AND 6 = 2)
$D8C3 ; 13 bytes of padding
$D8D0 ;  slot 2 (x AND 6 = 4)
$D943 ; 13 bytes of padding
$D950 ;  slot 3 (x AND 6 = 6)
$D9C3 ; 13 bytes of padding
$D9D0 b Mine 2 ; The mine (sprites $11, $12): 2 blink frames of 2x4 bytes, 8 slots of 8
> Drawer $AFDD. The mine handler at $9AEB draws sprite $11 + bit 2 of PassCount $B23B ($9B42), so the mine blinks every 4 passes. Only 40 of 128 bytes are read (i1, i2, a1, t1).
@res graphics/Mine.bin
$DA10 ; frame 2 (sprite $12)
$DA50 b BombCrater 3 ; The crater a UFO bomb leaves (sprite $10): 3x16, 4 slots of 48
> Drawer $B10D, 192 bytes; drawn by the crater handler at $9A92. All read (i1, i2, a1, t1).
@res graphics/BombCrater.bin
$DA80 ; slot 2
$DAB0 ; slot 3
$DAE0 ; slot 4
$DB10 b Rock 3 ; The rock (sprite $0F): 3x12, 4 slots of 36
> Drawer $B0E2, 144 bytes; the rock handler at $99C3. Read in every build (game 37188 times).
@res graphics/Rock.bin
$DB34 ; slot 2
$DB58 ; slot 3
$DB7C ; slot 4
$DBA0 b Spinner 3 ; The spinner, the third UFO kind (sprites $0B-$0E): 4 frames of 3x16 with colour, 4 slots of 48
> Drawer $B115: 3 bytes x 16 lines and the colour of 3x3 cells, 192 bytes a frame, frames at $DBA0, $DC60, $DD20, $DDE0. The UFO handler at $9B6B animates it ($9C93 LD A,$0B is its base). Read in i1, i2, a1, t1.
@res graphics/Spinner.bin
$DC60 ; frame 2
$DD20 ; frame 3
$DDE0 ; frame 4
$DEA0 b UfoB 3 ; The second UFO kind (sprites $05-$0A): 4 frames of 3x8 with colour, played 1 2 3 4 3 2
> Drawer $B069: 3 bytes x 8 lines, 4 slots of 24, 96 bytes a frame, frames at $DEA0, $DF00, $DF60, $DFC0. Base sprite $05 ($9C97). Read in i1, i2, a1, t1.
@res graphics/UfoB.bin
$DF00 ; frame 2
$DF60 ; frame 3
$DFC0 ; frame 4
$E020 b UfoA 3 ; The first UFO kind (sprites $01-$04): 3 frames of 3x8 with colour, played 1 2 3 2
> Drawer $B069, frames at $E020, $E080, $E0E0. Base sprite $01 ($9C9B). Read in i1, i2, a1, t1.
@res graphics/UfoA.bin
$E080 ; frame 2
$E0E0 ; frame 3
$E140 b Buggy 5 ; The moon buggy (sprite $00): 5x23 bytes, 4 shift slots of 128
> Drawer $B1C8: 115 bytes of each 128-byte slot, slot = x AND 6. Drawn every pass by the buggy routine at $AA0F ($AABC with A = 0 while DeathStep is $FF). 460 of 512 bytes read, in every build.
@res graphics/Buggy.bin
$E1B3 ; 13 bytes of padding
$E1C0 ;  slot 1 (x AND 6 = 2)
$E233 ; 13 bytes of padding
$E240 ;  slot 2 (x AND 6 = 4)
$E2B3 ; 13 bytes of padding
$E2C0 ;  slot 3 (x AND 6 = 6)
$E333 ; 13 bytes of padding
$E340 b MountainsStrip 32 ; The mountains, the middle scenery layer (lines 96-127) when the scenery byte is 0: 34 columns of 8 pixels x 32 lines
> Column-major: each 32 bytes are one 8-pixel column, top line first; 34 columns = 272 pixels. Drawn whole by $9590 at the life start, then fed in a column at a time by the middle scroller at $A89A (2 pixels every 4 passes) between MiddleStart and MiddleEnd. All 1088 bytes read in game and the other mountain plays.
