@module printvars ; Print variables: the print position, colours, metrics and state, the code table and the font descriptors
@about The print code at $FD80-$FFF3 addresses these through IX = $F000 (PrintChar $FDCC sets it). The loaded image holds the state the developer left: font 3 (the ROM font) selected and the shifted rows of a copyright sign in PrintShifted.
$F000 b PrintY 1 ; The print position's pixel line (0-184), the top of the next glyph
> Set by AT ($FE8E) and by a new line ($FE21: + line step, less $B9 past the bottom); read at $FF2A. The menus read PrintY and PrintX as one word ($8B20, $8CE7).
$F001 b PrintX 1 ; The print position's x in pixels
> Set by AT ($FE78), TAB ($FE9E) and a new line (the left margin, $FE15); moved on by each glyph ($FFCC).
$F002 b PrintShift 1 ; PrintX AND 7: the glyph's shift within its byte ($FF4B, read at $FF74)
$F003 b PrintCode 1 ; The character being printed ($FEF7, read at $FF5F)
$F004 b PrintAttr 1 ; The attribute of the cells a glyph touches: set by INK, PAPER, FLASH, BRIGHT ($FE33-$FE73); stored at $FFA0
$F005 b PrintMargin 1 ; The left margin a new line returns to (code $18, $FEAF)
$F006 b PrintLimit 1 ; The right limit: a glyph that would pass it starts a new line (code $19, $FEC3; read at $FF20)
$F007 b PrintError 1 ; Bit 0 is set when a parameter is out of range ($FEBD, $FEEF); never read
$F008 w PrintFont ; The current font's base: glyph = base + 8 x code (set by FONT, $FEE1; read at $FF65)
> Bit 2 of its high byte ($F009) tells PrintAttrCheck $F0F0 to leave the colours alone: set for FontPlain ($F600) and the ROM font ($3C00).
$F00A w PrintState ; The handler of the next code: PrintCharNormal $FDE3, or a control code's parameter handler
> PrintChar $FDCC jumps through it ($FDD9). The control code handlers store themselves here ($FE0A) and put PrintCharNormal back when their parameter arrives ($FEE6).
$F00C b PrintWidth 1 ; The current font's metrics, copied from its descriptor by FONT ($FED6): width of most characters
$F00D PrintWidthI ; width of "I" ($FF02)
$F00E PrintWidthMW ; width of "M" and "W" ($FF14)
$F00F PrintWidthSpace ; width of a space ($FF07)
$F010 PrintLineStep ; pixel lines from one text line to the next ($FE18)
$F011 PrintRows ; glyph rows drawn ($FF51, $FF71, $FFAF)
$F012 w PrintLines 2 ; The screen addresses of the glyph's rows (one word a row, up to 8), filled at $FF4E from ScreenLineTable plus the byte column
$F022 b PrintShifted 2 ; The glyph's rows shifted by PrintShift: two bytes a row, up to 8 rows ($FF6E), ORed onto the screen at $FFB2
$F032 s PrintSpare ; 78 zero bytes, never read or written
$F080 w PrintCodeTable ; The handlers of the control codes $10-$1A, one word each, read at $FDFD
> In order: $10 INK $FE2E, $11 PAPER $FE3D, $12 FLASH $FE52, $13 BRIGHT $FE65, $14 / $15 $FE23, $16 AT $FE78, $17 TAB $FE92, $18 MARGIN $FEAA, $19 LIMIT $FEB3, $1A FONT $FEC7.
$F096 b FontDescriptors 8 ; Four font descriptors of 8 bytes: width, width of I, width of M/W, width of a space, line step, rows, base (word)
> FONT n copies descriptor n's six metrics to PrintWidth $F00C and its base to PrintFont $F008 ($FEC7). Font 0: 4 4 6 2, step 6, 5 rows, base $F000 (glyphs FontSmall $F100). Font 1: 6 5 6 5, step 9, 6 rows, base $F300 (FontBold $F400); the title sets its step to 8 ($800A LD ($F0A2),A). Font 2: 6 6 6 6, step 8, 6 rows, base $F600 (FontPlain $F700). Font 3: all 8, base $3C00, the ROM font; its descriptor is never read in the runs (no reads of $F0AE-$F0B5).
$F09E ; font 1
$F0A6 ; font 2
$F0AE ; font 3
$F0B6 s PrintSpare2 ; 58 zero bytes, never read or written

@module printing ; Printing: the three fonts, the pixel and line tables, and the print code
@about Text is printed by PrintText $FFD0 (a string at HL ending with $1F) or PrintChar $FDCC (the code in A). Codes $20-$7F print a glyph of the current font at the pixel position PrintX, PrintY, ORed onto the screen; with fonts 0 and 1 the 2x2 attribute cells under the glyph take PrintAttr. Codes $80-$FF print "!". $0D is a new line. $10-$1A are control codes with one parameter byte after them (AT $16 takes two): $10 INK n, $11 PAPER n, $12 FLASH n, $13 BRIGHT n (n masked to 0-7 or 0-1; a larger n sets PrintError bit 0), $14 n and $15 n print "?" in place of n, $16 AT x y (pixels; y wraps at 185), $17 TAB x (a new line first when x is left of PrintX), $18 MARGIN n (n AND $3F), $19 LIMIT n (n OR $80 when below $80, with the error bit), $1A FONT n (n AND 3). Other codes below $20 print "?". $1F ends a string and cannot be printed.
$F0F0 c PrintAttrCheck ; Colour the glyph's cells unless the font is FontPlain or the ROM font, then draw it
> Entered from PrintGlyph $FEF7 ($FF92 JP $F0F0) with the first row's address on the stack and in PrintLines $F012. Bit 2 of the font base's high byte set: straight to the drawing at $FFAD; otherwise the first row's screen address to PrintColour $FF95. Run 5268 times in game (as often as PrintGlyph).
$F0FD b PrintPad 3 ; 3 zero bytes, never read
$F100 b FontSmall 8 ; Font 0: glyphs for $20-$7F, 8 bytes each, 5 rows used
> Glyph = PrintFont + 8 x code with PrintFont $F000, so the table starts at code $20. One byte a row, top row first, bits 7-0 left to right, set = ink; only the rows the descriptor names are drawn, the others are zero. The character set follows the Spectrum's ($5E up arrow, $60 pound, $7F copyright, lowercase present). 175 of 768 bytes are read in the runs (the characters the game prints).
@res fonts/FontSmall.bin
$F400 b FontBold 8 ; Font 1, the thick title font: glyphs $20-$7F, 6 rows used
> Same layout with base $F300. 420 of 768 bytes read.
@res fonts/FontBold.bin
$F700 b FontPlain 8 ; Font 2: glyphs $20-$7F, 6 rows used, printed without colours
> Same layout with base $F600. 246 of 768 bytes read.
@res fonts/FontPlain.bin
$FA00 b PixelTable 2 ; For each x 0-255: the byte column (x / 8) and the pixel's bit mask
> Indexed by $FA00 + 2x: by the pixel routines ($FD80 B = $7D, C = x, doubled), and by the copier $AFAB (its byte column). Not a font.
$FC00 w ScreenLineTable 8 ; The screen address of each pixel line 0-191 (192 words)
> Indexed by $FC00 + 2y ($7E00 doubled: H = $7E, L = y, then SLA L / RL H) by the pixel routines, PrintGlyph ($FF2D) and the sprite drawers. The scrollers start at line 64 ($FC80), 96 ($FCC0), 128 ($FD00) and 160 ($FD40). A line number past 191 reads past the table into PlotPixel's bytes (the wreck pieces, see BuggyWreck1 $638A).
$FD80 c PlotPixel ; Set the pixel at x H, line L
> Address from PixelTable and ScreenLineTable, then OR the mask. Run 61349 times in game; called from $87AE, $87B9, $8CF3 and $92CE.
$FD99 c UnplotPixel ; Reset the pixel at x H, line L (AND NOT the mask)
> Run in a1 (1160), t1 (58), i1 (420) and i2 (942): called from $A7F5.
$FDB3 c FlipPixel ; Invert the pixel at x H, line L (XOR the mask)
> Called from $AE12; run in i1 97928 times.
$FDCC c PrintChar ; Print the code in A at the print position (or take it as a control code's parameter)
> Keeps BC, DE, HL and IX; works in the other register set. Sets IX = $F000 and jumps to the handler in PrintState $F00A with a return to $FDDD. Run 13526 times in game.
$FDDD PrintCharDone ; restore and return
$FDE3 c PrintCharNormal ; The handler of a fresh code: a glyph, a new line, a control code or "?"
> $80 and up print "!"; $20-$7F print the glyph; $0D a new line; $10-$1A put the code's parameter handler from PrintCodeTable $F080 into PrintState ($FE0A); anything else prints "?". Run 8169 times in game.
$FDEC PrintCharLow ; codes below $80
$FE0F c PrintNewline ; New line: PrintX to the left margin, then the next line
> Run in game (33) and a1 (231): the menus' multi-line texts.
$FE17 c PrintNextLine ; Add the line step to the line at HL; past 184 wrap to the top
> Also called by TAB ($FEA6) and by PrintGlyph when a glyph would pass the limit ($FF25).
$FE21 PrintNextLineStore ; store the new line
$FE23 c PrintSwallow ; The handler for codes $14 and $15: back to PrintCharNormal and print "?" for the byte after them
> Not executed in any recording (game, a1, g1, t1, p2, h1, i1, i2, h2): known only from the code. The game's texts never use $14 or $15.
$FE29 PrintUnknown ; print "?": not executed in any recording, known only from the code
$FE2E c PrintInk ; INK n: PrintAttr's bits 0-2 = n AND 7 (game 82 runs)
$FE3D c PrintPaper ; PAPER n: bits 3-5 = n AND 7 (game 54)
$FE52 c PrintFlash ; FLASH n: bit 7 = n AND 1 (game 5)
$FE60 PrintFlashOff ; flash off
$FE65 c PrintBright ; BRIGHT n: bit 6 = n AND 1 (game 82)
$FE73 PrintBrightOff ; bright off
$FE78 c PrintAtX ; AT x y, first parameter: PrintX = x, the next byte to PrintAtY (game 2489)
$FE82 c PrintAtY ; AT, second parameter: PrintY = y, less 185 when 185 or more
$FE8E PrintAtYStore ; store the line
$FE92 c PrintTab ; TAB x: PrintX = x; a new line when x is left of the old PrintX
> Run only in a1 (320) and t1 (16): the score advance table's columns.
$FEAA c PrintMarginSet ; MARGIN n: PrintMargin = n AND $3F (game 25)
$FEB3 c PrintLimitSet ; LIMIT n: PrintLimit = n, with bit 7 forced and PrintError set when n is below $80 (game 25)
$FEC3 PrintLimitStore ; store the limit
$FEC7 c PrintFontSet ; FONT n: copy descriptor n AND 3 to the metrics and the base (game 106)
$FEE6 c PrintTakeParam ; Put PrintCharNormal back into PrintState and mask the parameter A with B - 1 (error bit when A >= B)
> Called by each control code handler that takes a number (game 354 runs).
$FEF3 PrintTakeParamMask ; mask
$FEF7 c PrintGlyph ; Draw the glyph of the code in A at the print position and advance PrintX by its width
> The width: PrintWidthSpace for a space, PrintWidthI for "I", PrintWidthMW for "M" and "W", PrintWidth otherwise. A glyph that would pass PrintLimit starts a new line first. Then the row addresses into PrintLines (ScreenLineTable plus the byte column), the rows shifted right by PrintShift into PrintShifted, and PrintAttrCheck $F0F0. Run 5268 times in game.
$FF07 PrintGlyphSpace ; width of a space
$FF0C PrintGlyphNotI ; not "I"
$FF14 PrintGlyphWide ; "M" or "W"
$FF19 PrintGlyphNormal ; any other character
$FF1C PrintGlyphFit ; new line when the glyph does not fit
$FF54 PrintGlyphLines ; one row address a pass
$FF74 PrintGlyphShift ; shift one glyph row into two bytes
$FF7F PrintGlyphShiftBit ; one bit
$FF84 PrintGlyphStore ; store the right byte
$FF95 c PrintColour ; Set the 2x2 attribute cells under the glyph to PrintAttr, then draw it
> HL = the first row's screen address; the cell's attribute address from its high byte ($58 + bits 3-4). Run 4055 times in game (fonts 0 and 1 only).
$FFAD c PrintRowsOr ; OR the shifted rows onto the screen, two bytes a row, and advance PrintX by the width
> Run 5268 times in game.
$FFB2 PrintRowsLoop ; one row
$FFD0 c PrintText ; Print the string at HL up to its $1F
> Run 4004 times in game. The build-date strings start with $1F and print nothing (BuildDate0335 $7F00).
$FFDA c PrintReset ; Reset the print state and print PrintResetText
> Not executed in any recording: known only from the code. Nothing calls it; the image's print state (font 3, the ROM font) is what this leaves, so it was the developer's.
$FFE7 t PrintResetText ; INK 0, AT 0 0, MARGIN 0, LIMIT $FF, FONT 3, end ($12 00 16 00 00 18 00 19 FF 1A 03 1F)
@res text/PrintResetText.bin
$FFF3 b PrintResetPad 1 ; A zero byte, never read
$FFF4 c Im2Jump ; The IM 2 interrupt's target: JP $7F14
> The game runs in IM 2 with I = $3A ($A7D5): the ROM's bytes at $3A00-$3AFF are $FF, so the vector is $FFFF, and the JR there (its offset is the ROM's $F3 at $0000) lands here. Run 25747 times in i1.
$FFF7 t BuildTag ; Leftover: "ROYD3313", never read
$FFFF b Im2Vector 1 ; $18, the JR the interrupt lands on: with the byte $F3 at $0000 it jumps back 13 to Im2Jump $FFF4 (run 25747 times in i1)
